      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>COLD and distant—the North Pole. "ONE half the world doesn't know how the other half lives." Neither dees the other half. JONES: "I say, Miss Brown, how is it that you are always out when I call?" She: "Oh, just luck 1" A GENIUS is a man who when he accidentally says a good thing, can make his hearers believe it was intentional. IT is easy for a man to paddle his own canoe when his parents buy the canoe and paddle for him. TALL men live longer than short ones, because the short man is not long for this world. THE Cheapest of Lawyers—Keeping ones's own council. SHE: "All extremely bright men are conceited, anyway." He: "Oh, I don't know. I'm not." TEacnER (to junior pupil of phpsiology) : "What teeth come last !" Pupil: "False teeth." LOST.—lrate Passenger, as train is moving off: "Why didn't you put my luggage in as I told you?" Porter: "Eh, moo, yer luggage is no sic a fule as yersel'. Ye're i' the wrang train !" HOPE DEFERRED.—Cleverton : "When do you expect to get married, old man ?" Dashaway (gloomily) :"I don't know." Cleverton : "Why, hasn't she set the day ? ' Dashaway "The day? Why, she hasn't set the year yet !" "Do you understand me now ?" thundered a hasty pedagogue to an urchin, at whose head he threw an inkstand. "I have got an inkling of what you mean?" replied the boy. REGARDLESS OF COST. —Clerk (to employer) : "What shall I mark that new lot of black silk at?" Employer: "Mark the selling price three shillings a yard." Clerk: "But it only cost one shilling a yard." Employer "1 don't care what it cost. lam selling off regardless of cost." " YE pays no more attention to me," said Patrick to his children, "than if I was a dumb beast talk- ing to yez." IT was at a charity dinner that a careless waiter spilt a plate of soup over one of the clergymen present. "What "--he commenced. Then, remembering himself, he turned to his neighbour and asked: "Will some layman kindly say a few words appropriate to the occasion?" THE REASON WHY.—" I can take one hundred worth a minute," said the shorthand writer. "I often take more than that," remarked the other, in sorrowful accents; "but then I have to. I'm married !" THE RULING PASSION.—In the last illness of witty George Colman, the doctor, being late in an appointment, apologised to his patient, saying that he had been called in to see a man who had fallen down a well. "Did he kick the bucket, doctor?" groaned out poor George. DE LONG BEACH: " Hoffy, I shall marwy Miss Wockingham after all. Yesterday •I saved her life. It was most exciting. I was on the beach, she was in the watah. A big wave knocked her ovals, the undahtow gwabbed her. She was being carwied out to sea. There was only one thing to do. I—" Hoffman: " Wushed to her wescue?" De Long Beach (proudly) : " No ; I scweamed for help " "SHORT AND SWEET."—That was what the grocer remarked, as he tied up fifteen ounces of sugar and put it amongst the "pound" packets. WORTH THE RlSK.—Fair Homeseeker : "I like the appearance of that house; but I wouldn't live in it for the world. People say it's haunted, and I am dreadfully nervous." Agent (craftily) : "Yes, ma'am; they say that ghosts come out of every one of the thirty-two closets." Fair Home. seeker (setting her teeth hard) : "I'll take it !" CONTRADICTORY,—" The house is so lonesome without you and the dear children," writes Smith now to the absent wife, who is with the family at Margate. tut the faithful maid-of-all-work writes : "Shore, mum, Mr. Smith hasn't been in until after twelve o'clock never a night since you left." VERY PROSAIC.—The wife of Jean Paul Richter, the German novelist, was an excellent housekeeper, but a matter-offaait woman, who had little sym- pathy with her husband's poetical fancies. He was once reading to her a fine passage which he had just written, and was not a little surprised to find that she stopped her knitting and seemed lost in thought, as he read sentence after sentence. "Jean Paul," said she, as soon as he had ceased reading, "you must put on another pair of stock- ings to-morrow morning; I see that those you have en want darning." M'BRIDE's MISTAKE.—" What on earth are you ding?" exclaimed Mrs. A.l'Bride, when she saw her husband jabbing a pin into an ornamental piece of embroidery on her dressing-table. "Merely putting a pin into this cushion, dear," he replied. " Mercy me ! I pail fifteen shillings for that pie-cushion at a church fair, and do you suppose I'm going to allow pins to be stuck into it ? " THE NEW FRENCH COOK.—Mrs. Fitz-Nickel aspires to be as fashionable as any of her "swell" neighbours. She was telling a lady visitor the other morning that she had just engaged a very efficient, experienced, and high-priced French cook. While her friend was congratulating her upon her new acquisition, the French cook inserted her head within the doorway, and asked: "Shure, missus, an' shall I pale the praties, or bile 'em wid their jackets on?" DAUGHTER: "Mr. Jawkins has proposed to me, ma, but I don't know whether to say 'yes' or no.'." Mother: "My dear, in the game of whist the advice is, when in doubt, play a trump.' As regards matrimony, when in doubt, say yes.' You may never have another chance 1" A SAILOR'S LOT.—In a speech on woman's rights, the lady orator exclaimed, "It is well known that Solomon owed his wisdom to the number of his wives !" Another lecturer, g lug further still, moved that women should be entrusted with com- missions in the navy; to which a deep, gruff, nautical voice amongst the audience responded with the observation that "Lot's wife was an old salt, you know.' 4 HE KNEW HIS BUSINESS.—Pokeby : "Say, Shearer, why don't you get a new editor for your Woman's Department ? ' Of all the silly, ignorant twaddle I ever read, those articles are the worst." Shearer (Editor-in-Chief) : "That's just what we want. Just think what pleasure and pride it gives every woman who reads those articles to reflect on how superior she is to the common run of her sex." "TICKETS, please, sir," demanded the inspector at tie carriage door, of a gentleman who, accom- panied by his little girl, occupied seats in a first- class railway compartment. On the production of one only, the man asked for that of the young lady. "What ! a *ticket fer that baby, only four yeat s old? I never heard of such a thing 1" "Oh, papa," struck up the mite, "you know I was seven yesterday." A QUEER SEX.—Call a girl a chick and she smiles; call a woman a hen and she howls; call a young woman a witch and she is pleased; call an old woman a witch and she is indignant; cal a girl a kitten and she rather likes it; call a woman a cat and she'll hate you. THERE is no place like the House of Commons for a "nice derangement of metaphors." It will be a long time before we have a " mixture " equal to the outburst of an effusive orator who said, "The British lion, whether it is roaming the deserts of India or el nibing the forest of Canada, will not draw in its horns or retire into its shell." Two little children were being put to bed. The little girl was kneeling at her mother's side saying her prayers, while her mischievous brother was standing at the leek of the chair making grimaces. His little sister evidently had one eye open if not two, for at last she stopped her prayers, and with "'Scooze me, Lord, while I go and kick Georgie," jumped up, kicked her brother, and pr _mptly knelt down again. Was, THE TIME EVER COME ?—Spatts : " What is your idea of the 4 Bloobumper ?" Blooburnper : "My idea of the millennium is the time when one man will accept, without hesita- tion, another's explanation of how he got that black eye." WHAT A QUESTION !—A young masher in a barber's shop not long since was particular about having his moustache nicely pt:rfumed. "Going to call on a young lady, I suppose?" the barber asked. The youth, with much dignity, replied: "See here, my friend, do you suppose I put per- fumery on my moustache because I am going to see a man, or a boy, or an old woman, or a babe in arms? Do men gati_er grapes-of thorns or figs of thistles?"</p>
        <p>A FOOL can ask what a wle,e, rn?ol can't answer; but because a man asks questions it isn't safe to take him for a fool.</p>
        <p>SHOW THIS TO SOME. trid OMAN. I will and a valuable. instructive, and highly interesting book whicn eve y wcman would be glad to have Contains important and confident:al advice and tells both mauled and single how to keep. ii.althy and avoid the anxieties so distressing to • the sex. Sent tree, securely sealed, tor one stan p to cover postßge. ss Profeszor E. LOALLI,;, 17, Eastbourne Terrace Fadaington, London,</p>
