      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladstone is to go to the South of France after Christmas—that is, if he can be persuaded to leave his library. His friends are urging him to take this trip, but he is so full of delight in the pleasures of his library that they find it an uphill task to get him to promise he will go. Mr. Armi- stead has offered the hospitalities of such a trip, and goes to Hawarden on Saturday next to per- sonally urge Mr. Gladstone, for the sake of his own and Mrs. Gladstone's health, to go abroad.</p>
        <p>The preliminary steps for the municipalisation ofl the London tramway systems continue to be taken by the County Council. Notice has already been served on the London Tramways' Company calling upon them to sell two miles of their line on the south side of the river, and that portion of the system will very shortly come into possession of the Council. By the end of 1898 almost the whole of the undertakings on the north side of London will fall within the Council's scheme of municipal- isation, and by 1903 possession will have been obtained of the lines on the south side. It was on Tuesday agreed that, as purchased, the lines shall be leased, subject to the condition that they shall all revert to the Council rot later than 1903. Once they have complete possession. of the undertakings, the .Council hope to do much for the public benefit.</p>
        <p>In addition to the rumours pointing to anticipa- tion of the reductiom of the price of the "Times" to a penny, there is in circulation some gossip refer- ring to the other London dailies. I have informa- tion confirming my statement that the story of a coming reduction in the price of the "Times" is inaccurate. The tale is believed to arise simply from the fact that the proprietors are substituting Hoe machines for the old Walter presses. The other rumours are of more interest to the pro- vinces. The' Daily Telegraph" is arranging for a country edition for the special benefit of Liverpool, Manchester, Sheffield, and the otfreera-rte towns in the North and Midlands. For some time the sport- -1 • ing papers have been getting out a country edition, ' which is despatched to the North by the train leav- ing St. Pancras at midnight, and which reaches Northern sporting men at the same time as their local papers. The " Telegraph " has got hold of the same idea, and proposes to publish an edition in time to catch the Midland train, and so compete with the local dailies. It will also be able to go down into the Vest by the new midnight train from Paddington. The scheme is not yet fully matured, but it is contemplated, and will probably be put into effect with the new year. The London papers in recent years have all had to face a steadily diminishing circulation in the country, due to the growing excellence of the provincial daily papers, and this is one of the ways in which it is proposed to meet that competition.</p>
        <p>Among the more active and energetic tradesmen of London. a movement has been set on foot for the purpose of establishing an association to check a system which has grown to enormous proportions in the metropolis, at least in some trades—the system, namely, of wholesale houses entering into competition with retail firms and underselling them. Several trades are quite destroyed over great areas and in the best localities, by the extent to which this goes on. The wholesale houses which carry on this system, retail to the general public at wholesale prices, and the small trader, who goes to the wholesale establishment for his goods, finds that be is paying the same price for them himself as the general public. The method of fighting this custom which finds most favour with the promoters of the proposed association is an extensive applica- tion of the boycott to wholesale, houses which enter into this unequal rivalry. But that would not be enough. The public would require to boycott them also; and that it will not do—it is more likely to patronise the shop wlhich supplies it most cheaply with the goods it wants.</p>
        <p>Whenever politics seem to have got to a lull an attempt is made in a Liberal Unionist quarter north of the Tweed to make out that the Radicals below the gangway are going to upset the Ministerial coach. It may be news to the critic, but Lord Tweed- mouth is thought by all Liberals in the House to be more in touch with every section of the party then any party whip or leader for many years. His attitude of masterly reserve in withholding parti- oulars as to the form and scope of the resolution against the Lords causes no irritation, but is ac- cepted as the dictate of ordinary common sense. It is more than doubtful whether Mr. Labouchere himself wants a dissolution in February or March. Certainly nobody else does in the House of Com- mons. There is a unanimous approval of a steady working session. The last phase of opinion Points rather to the session being given to the ordinary business of legislation, and a special autumn session being called f.,r the Lords resolution. This would have the advan- tage of concentrating the attention of Parliament and of the country on one momentous issue apart from all others ; and when the full results of the working session were plainly visible before the country we should have Welsh Disestablishment, the new Irish Land Act,one man one vote, Registra- tion, Local Veto all wrecked by the Lords, and most probably Labour, Conciliation, and Factory Bills mangled, possibly Tenant Right Bills for England and Wales and Scotland either smashed or talked out. The House would adjourn in September with this record ; and in November, after-gatherings all over the country, would meet with a red-hot mandate to "clear the way." A month's debate would bring the issue to white heat, end the dis- solution would come early in January, 1896, on the fresh register.</p>
        <p>The complete military breakdown of China and the possibility, if the Japanese persist in carrying on the war, that the Manf.hii dynasty may disappear, and that the empire itself may break up, put the Anglo-Siamese que,tion in a somewhat new light. It was contemplated that the buffer State which was to keep separate the English and the French territories was to be placed under the con- trol of China. This part of the preliminary ar- rangement will be in suspense until all the conse- quences of the war manifest themselves, unless, in- deed, Lord Rosebery assents to the wishes of France. and agrees to make the frontier of the twfl European Powers conterminous. In that case the work of the present delimitation commission would be use- less, and the commission would require instead to delimit the frontiers of the two Western Powers. The commission has not yet commenced its work, and will not begin it for three weeks more, as a year or two will be necessary to complete it. The political condition of China will have had time to develop before the work has made much progress; but present events in the north of China may in- duce Fiance to press more strongly for the accept- ance by England of a common frontier, and for the abandonment of England's pet theory of a neutral zone.</p>
        <p>Lord Randolph Churchill's state of health is giv- ing a good deal of anxiety to his friends. He was in a very poor way when he started on his trip round the world some three or four months ago. Those who saw him in America regretfully reported that he was looking very much pulled down. He recovered somewhat on the voyage to Yokohama, and was in good spirits and very active while in Japan. But since he has reached India he has broken down again, and no one would he surprised if on his return the doctors ordered him complete rest. In any case it is unlikely that he will he able to contest Bradford against Mr. Shaw-Lefevre as he intended to when he left England.</p>
        <p>Lord Carrington on his arrival from St. Peters- burg, went direct to the Queen at, Windsor, tr 4 whom he was commanded to give o full description of the great functions he had witnessed in St. Peter,burg. He was greatly impressed with the magnificence of the funeral obsequies of the late Czar, which lasted for a week. Hardly had the mourning trappings been taken down than, as if in fairyland, St. Petersburg burst out into splendour for the Imperial wedding. Lord Carrington has got many hints that might be utilised in our pagenn- tries, which, having seen no change for over fiftN veers, are growing dreary and monotonous. If Lord Carrington is allowed a free hand it is quite possible that even leveas and drawing-ro2ms may be materially altered.</p>
        <p>IMOXON'S NURSERY BOON</p>
        <p>(Unequalled for DESTROYING NITS, and PARASITES in the HUMAN HAI(.) OF CHEMISTS and the PROPRIETOR, J. •L. M 0 X N. PAPX-ROID, LIVETIPOOL. ed. told lb. per box. Foat Free 74 and la. Please mention this paper.</p>
