      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY DECEMBER 8, 1894. LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>Though the German Em...,-,ror employs Berlin tailors, he believes in giving pYtivincials employment also. la every good sized town there is a Court taller, who occa- sionally has the honour of supplying the Emperor with a uniform. Her youthful Majesty, Queen Wilhelmina of Holland, is a young lady with strong and well-marked individual- ity ; and, good as she generally is, her mother occasion.- ally finds it necessary to chasten her somewhat preco- cious sense of Royal dignity by maternal reproof. A story is told of the daughter demanding entrance to her mother's room with the imperious announcement of her- self as the "Queen of Holland," and being compelled by the Queen Regent's firmness to sue for admittance in the more becoming character of a " little daughter," desirous of kissing her mamma.</p>
        <p>Treatment of Rheumatism.</p>
        <p>There are three things says the " Popular Medical Monthly, which make for rheumatism. First a chill, especially a damp chill ; this depresses the vitality of the body, and through the nerves acts on the internal organs, stomach, liver, kidneys, and joints. Then there is indigestion, not necessarily accompanied with pain. Many think their digestion is good because they feel no pain, nor any other particular discomtort. But this is not so. The corn, so to speak, may go through the mill, and be imperfectly ground, but being once put in it has to be disposed of in some way, and nature in its efforts to get rid of offending particles upsets some of the machinery. And for reasons it would take too long to explain the joints are the first things to suffer. Damp and diet then are the two chief causes of rheumatism ; either may produce it, but both are more certain to de it. This being so, the treatment or rather the preven- tion is not far to seek. For prevention is the treatment, all other treatment is secondary and auxiliary at the best. Unless the causes are removed, medicines may be taken ad infinitum, and with only partial relief. A spare,light, easily digested diet must be rigidly adopted, the absence of wine and beer being a sine qua non. A farinaceous diet with fish and eggs will enable the sys- tem sooner to recover itself, and,in some cases, a purely milk diet will be found better than anything else to bring back the organs to health. One article of diet should never be touched by those either suffering from, or are predisposed to rheumatism, namely, preserved fruits. Jam will bring oh an attack of gout or rheumatism sooner than almost anything else. This is believed to be 'tie to the action of great heat on the sugar put into the fruit, for fresh fruit has not this injurious action. The sugar more readily splits up into lactic acid after cook- ing, and lactic acid is,the acid of rheumatism. As for drugs, mild aperients, the alkaline mineral waters, bi- carbonate of potash, lithia, and a host of other drugs are all more or less useful, but prevention is better than Cure.</p>
        <p>A VALUABLE -MANUSCRIPT. • has been discovered and, no doubt, its contents will throw a clear light upon a question of great interest and importance. The original manuscript was written, evidently, by Professor J. Poiritud, in the ancient town of Wargrave- on-Thames. An epitome is as follews :—" Weeks of treatmEnt by the best London physicians failed to relieve me of Inflammation of the Bladder and Prostrate gland, but Warner's " Safe" Cure cured me, and now I can sonscientiously say that my present good health is solely attributable to Warner's SafA Cure."</p>
        <p>THE DOUBLE LIFE:</p>
        <p>OR, THE HAMPTON MYSTERY. By the Author of " DARKWOODe"</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XLW.</p>
        <p>his movements, and men-pursued his course, reav- ing Wigby to perform the congenial part of spy. We need not detail the incidents of Lord Ad- lowe's excursion to Cardiff. It is enough to say that the parliamentary train proceeded with its customary slowness, and that Adlowe was an- noyed beyond expression at the long stoppages ; that he arrived safely at last at his destination ; that he found the agent in, secured the house under an assumed name, paid a .quarter's rent, and with the receipt and the house-key in his pocket, returned to Dalton in fine spirits. It was six o'clock—already dusk—when he strode up the street toward the inn. The shops were lit. The April day was closing with a fine mist. The little town that had looked so pretty in the morning sunlight looked very dreary and gloomy now. There were few people abroad, and those few pattered over the slippery pavements in goloshes and under umbrellas—not very enlivening objects in the general gloom. Lord Adlowe hurried to his room on reaching his inn, flung aside his outer garments, and rang for Wigby and his supper. Both speedily made their appearance. "Well !" said Adlowe, impatiently, when the landlord, who had personally served him, had withdrawn. " What luck?" "The best, the very best, my lcrd," responded Wigby, triumphantly. "I went first to look at lodgings where a sign was hung out, pretending that I was sent by my master. I did not find the young lady at any .of these places, although I in- quired carelessly if they had any new lodgers, as my master half expected to meet here some friends who had lately left town. After that idea failed I went into one or two shops, thinking the young lady would be ant to make some purchases after her hurried flight, but I got no satisfaction out of</p>
        <p>RUN TO BARTEL. The winds, Imprison'd in the Caverns of the earth, Break out in hideous earthquakes; passions so Increased by opposition. CLAPTHORNE. After Mrs. Pumpton had taken such a spirited leave of him, and he had resumed his ride down the principal streets of the little market town of Dalton, Lord Adlowe proceeded directly and swiftly to the railroad station to prosecute his search for Giralda. Arrived there he learned that the morning ex- press train for London had passed through an hour before. For a moment his heart almost stood still, in fear that his prey had again escaped him. He sought out the stationmaster, and by a judieious fee and skilful inquiries discovered that no young lady answering to Giralda's description had been seen to depart by the train. A glow of satisfaction replaced his lordship's ap- prehension. He turned away, remounted, and rode to the vil- lage inn, satisfied that piralda was there. The inn proved to be one of those quaint old creations of stone that serve as a monument of by-gone generations. It had oddly shaped case- ment windows, with tiny diamond panes of dull- coloured glass set in lead, and a profusion of gables, the whole surmounted by an overhanging roof. Yet, despite its age and quaintness, perhaps because of them, the old inn had a pleasant, homely, and inviting air. On its signboard was painted a golden harp, and above the harp was inscribed in Welsh the name of the inn—the Harp of Alyrddyn. Lord Adlowe rode into the paved yard, glancing up at the windows as he did so, but failing to de- tect any sign of her he sought. The landlord, a stout man with a florid face, genuinely Welsh, came out to meet his guest. Lord Adlowe slipped from his saddle and has- tened at once to make his inquiries. "Is there a young lady stopping here, landlord ?" he inquired. "A young lady, accompanied only by a little boy?" The landlord replied in the negative, speaking also in English. Adlowe could not restrain his feeling of disap- pointment. 'She came into the town last night," he ex- claimed, "attended by a woman servant. She has not left the place, lam positive. Is there any other inn ? " "No, sir," replied the landlord, "there is no other inn in Dalton. There is an ale shop where bagmen sometimes put up, but the Harp of Myrddyn enjoys all the patronage of any account. Strangers'sometimes go to lodgings—" "Perhaps the young lady I am looking for took lodgings," interrupted Adlowe, with reviving hope. "1 will make inquiries. I ordered my servants to meet me here. Have they arrived ? " The landlord's manner became instantly obse- quious. He had evidently imbibed the English lower-class veneration for titles. "Lord Adlowe's 'valet is here," he exclaimed, bowing and smiling. "Show me to my room, then," said Adlowe, "and send him to me!" The landlord summoned a stable-boy to take charge of his lordship's horse, and then conducted Adlowe into the inn, to a neat and pleasant little parlour, where a fire was burning. "Your lordship's valet ordered the rooms to be in readiness for you, my lord. Your lordship's breakfast will be served immediately. I will send your lordship's man at once !" He4withdrew, full of importance at the growing grandeur of his inn, at which he was not wont to often entertain " live lords," and Adlowe threw off his top coat and settled himself cosily by the fire. He had scarcely done so when Wigby entered his presence. "Oh, its you !" exclaimed Adlowe, with some surprise. "The landlord said my valet was here. Where is Haskins ?" "I don't know, my lord," replied Wigby. "We were together at the shrubbery near Trevelyan Park the other night when you expected to take the young lady in charge. After her escape and your iuterview with Lord Trevelyan, your lordship sent Aaskins in one direction, to search for Miss Arevalo, me in another, and you, yourself took a third road, bidding us meet your lordship here this morning in ease oefailure, to report our proceed- ings. If we found the young lady we were to take her to Peggy Willsey's cottage on the Burnt Downs, and await your coining there. I have not seen Haskins since we separated in your lordship's presence." " Strange ! " muttered Adlowe. "Where can he be ? " Not a suspicion entered his mind that the " relative " of Peggy Willsey who had been dangerously injured on the Burnt Downs, and of whom the shepherd lad had told him, was his valet. Adlowe mused a moment, and then said: "I suppose you have discovered no clue to the young lady's retreat ? " "No, my lord. She could not have left the Park by any public road, for I have made many inquiries, but faided to discover any one who had seen her. Lord Trevelyan has had the roads fairly scoured. He is now in London, where, I suppose, he is searching for her. I went back to the l'ark yesterday, fancying that the young lady might have gone back, The young Lord Grosvenor • has put off a journey to town he had been intending to make, and is looking far and near for Miss Arevolds Mrs. Pumpton is still missing—" "she is on her way home," interrupted Adlowe. " I lost her as I was entering this town, and she wts leaving it. Miss .Arevalo is in Dalton. I have traced her here." Witr,by expressed his surnrise and satisfaction. "You can go out immediately," continued " and commence judicious inquiries a‘bout her. I have reason to think that she has taken lodgings. No doubt she intends to remain here until she can hear from her parents. Be cautious, Wighy. You had better, before showing yourself in toe streets, shave your face, and have your hair trimmed in different fashion, as a sort of dis- guise." After bestowing upon him a few further direc- tions, Adlowe dismissed his man, and gave himself up to meditations. His breakfast was brought in. He partook of it In a preoccupied manner, and as soon as the dishes were removed engaged in the perusal of a late Cardiff paper. It was noticeable that he paid no attention to the news, but applied himself to the advertisement columns. In fact, Adlowe had begun to consider anew the question where he should secure Giralda after recovering possession of her. It was out of the question to think of taking her to Peggy Willsey's cottage on the Burnt Downs. Peggy Willsey was too honest to further his wicked plans knowingly ; there was a stranger—a sick man —in her tiny cottage; and, filially, there would be too many comers 'and goers there now, if there were no other objections. "I Can't take her to my own house in any secrecy," he thought. "I can put her in a school or asylum. And the boy is with her to be cared for. It's out of the question to carry her back to the custody of old Mrs. Bittly at the bluff cottage. What am I to do with her ? ' He scanned the advertisements as if he expected them to suggest something. If so, his expectation was realised. His attention was attracted to the announcement of a furnished house to let near Pencoed, a r ilway station on the southern line. The house was described as exceedingly retired, and well suited to any nue desiring to economise. " engage it," declared Adlowe, well satis- fied. "It seems the house is closed, and applica- tions to see it may be obtained, with the key, from the agent in Cardiff. Good ! I can run down to Cardiff and return by night, whereas to go to Pen- coed I must proceed to Cardiff and change to the other line." He rang his bell, summoning the landlord. In answer to his inquiries, the host informed him that a parliamentary train would stop in half an hour on its way to Cardiff, and a gentleman going by that train would have an hour or two in the train, and could then return by afternoon express, arriving in Dalton before dusk. Adlowe made his preparations at once to take the parliamentary. He wrote a guarded note to Wigby, explaining his absence, and then set out to walk to the station. • On the way he kept up a close watch for Giralda, but her face did not appear at any of the windows, nor was her figure visible in the street. He did, however, see Wigby as the latter emerged from a barber's shop so changed in looks by the application of razor and scissors that Adlowe was satisfied that Giralda would not readily lulus, the mawHi lordship stopped a moment to explain</p>
        <p>that—" "Well, what then?" demanded Adlowe, with increasing impatience. "Is she in town? Have you seen her ?" "I have not seen her, my lord, but she is in town. I discovered her hiding-place through a little street urchin whom I fortunately thought to question. He saw a beautiful young lady, accompanied by a little boy, an old woman, and a country lad, dismount before the door of the pastrycook's shop yesterday. He described them. They were, of course, Mis Girahla, Mrs. Pumpton, the shepherd lad who conducted them, and—" "Miss Giralda's brother I So you have run the game to earth, Wigby. You shall have a hand- some reward. Haskins hasn't come yet ?"</p>
        <p>"Not yet, my lord." "It's odd. I suppose he's on some false trail. The man can't be playing me false," added Ad- lowe, musingly, in a tone too low for Wigby's ears. "He can't have gone to the Lady Beatrice in one of his fits of remorse? No, no. lam sure of his fidelity. Yet I will hasten to secure safety i this marriage with Giralda." Wigby waited respectfully for Adlowe to arouse himself from the painful reverie into which his lordship had fallen. After a brief space he was rewarded, Acllowe starting up, with an air of de-</p>
        <p>oision. "You have found the bird, Wigby," he said, "and I have secured the cage. I have taken a small, lonely country house near Pencoed, and we must secure the young lady and convey her there between this and daybreak. Can we get horses, and some sort of conveyance?" Wigby replied in the affirmative. "They can be hired under pretence of going in an opposite direction. My horse can be left hero as security until you return with those we hire. I will drive the young lady and her brother, if I can contrive to get hold of them, and you will take the night express for Cardiff, changing there for the southern line. Yoe must go to the bluff cottage, find Mrs. Bittly, and bring her to my house at Pencoed, to act as housekeeper and jailer. You must be speedy and secret." "Yes, my lord. I will order your conveyance to be got ready at once. I shall have time to assist you off before leaving on the train. But how are. we to get hold of the young lady? Enter the pastry-cook's sh,yp and boldly abduct her?" "No. Miss Arevalo ha.s been confined to the house all day, and will 'be likely to seek the fresh air as soon as the darkness renders it safe. She isn't afraid of rain, as I know. We will lie in wait for her neat the pastry-cook's door." This decision was carried into effect. Horses and a vehicle were procurred, and master and man set out ostensibly on a day or two's excursion to the northward, a liberal fee blinding the landlord to the singularity of starting after nightfall in the face of the mist. A little later, Lord Adlowe and Wighy were crouching in a dark door-way near the pastry-cook's shop, the horses being secured to a post around an adjacent rorner. An hour passed here in painful watching and waiting. "She won't come I" muttered Adlowe at last, disappointedly. "The rain had frightened her. I shall have to wait till to-morrow night. unless I useoli. Into the house, and that, of course, is out , of the question. Was there ever anything more 1 u.toioying ?—" "Hush I" whispered Wigby, laying his hand on his master's arm. The pastry-cook's, like the other shops, had been closed and shuttered. At this moment its door opened, and a long ray of light fell upon the wet pavement. The two men gathered themselves like tigers for a spring. "Oh, I'm not at all afraid, Mrs. Jones," tbey heard a voice, which they recognised as Giralda's, saying. "I'm equipped for the rain, and, as for company and protection, I've got little Fay here. The fact is, I want to send a message to Peggy and her new inmate, and the doctor will go to the Burnt Downs early in the morning. If I see him before he goes, I must see him to-night. You know I don't care to go out in the daytime. It's only a little way to the doctor's !" "I know it, Miss," answered Mrs. Jones, un- easily, flashing her light so that its rays were thrown up and down the misty street. "But I wish I had somebody to send with you. I'll go myself, spite of leaving the shop alone—" "No, no, Mrs. Jones," interposed. Giralda, in her sweet, clear voice. "Give me the jelly and the dainties for the sick man, and let me go. No one will harm me. I shall be back immediately l" "She received from the good woman a tumbler of jelly and a package of cakes. The next moment Mrs. Jones closed the door, and with Fay clinging to her Giralda hurried along the street toward the doctor's residence, the two sheltering them- selves with an umbrella. The two villains, master and man, crept after the innocent young pair with gleaming eyes and eager stealthiness. (To be continued).</p>
        <p>WHENEVER a man makes a good guess he begins to tilk about his good judgment. MATID: "What is the best way to retain a man's love?" ' Marie : "Don't return it." TAILOR: "I tell you, my friendt, dhose elodhings viii year lice iron." Cusl omer : "I believe you; they look a little rusty already."</p>
        <p>FROM ERYING-rAN TO ' v tnust devise sonic means of paying these bills against t:.e church. We are being pressed for the money." Vestryman (in surprise) : " Why, what are they for?" Deacon: "For flowers, decora- tions, music, and so forth, furnished for the enter- tainment last month to celebrate the church being at last free from debt."</p>
        <p>A HOPELESS CASE.—Caller: How is MT. Light- out, the etni,ezzler, now?" Asylum Superinten- dent : " About the same. He NI/ il I not be likely to improve o long as he continues worrying." " What does he worry about mostly?" "He worries over the fact that if he ever becomes sane he will have to stand trial." A SENsmvE PLANT. —The company are seated at the ~inner table. Enter Baptiste (a servant), haggard, excited, and exclaiming :—" Quick, quick, a. glass of wine " People stare at each other, and at last the wish is complied with. Baptiste drinks off a glass of wine which has been poured out by the mistress of the house, who inquires what has happened. "Oh Madame, I've been dreadfully upset. Ah ! that wine has done me good ! I feel better now. , Only fancy, I have just managed to break both the large Sevres china marmalade dishes !" LITTLE BUT MUCH.—In the gardens of a certain nobleman's country house there were fixed ab different spots painted boards with this request; "Please do not pick the flowers without leave." Some wag got a paint brush and added ." '' to the last word.</p>
        <p>"'Fins introduction gives me great pleasure, believe me," frankly explained Brown, when in- troduced to a popular society actress. "Really you flatter me, Mr. Brown." "Not at all. I have worshipped you from a distance for over twenty years and-" Brown is still engaged racking his brain trying to find out why the actress cut him short, and has since declined to recognisehim when they accidentally met.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>MT DEAREST NELLIE,</head>
        <p>MT DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Fur collars are very much worn this autumn, and the ugly " sable " tie, with its crossed head and tail, is getting almost painfully general, and is to be had at very low prices. Rather longer, fiat boas, with a single tail at each end and no head, are in much better taste, and are not likely to be- come so common owing to the price being higher; but one of the most fashionable neck-wraps of the day is a plain, rather wide, tight-fitting sable collar, with a bib or bow of lace, and a cluster of artificial flowers in front. Fur, lace, and flowers go to make up a curiously incongruous mixture, but one which is exceedingly fashionable this season, not only for collarettes, but for gowns and hats. Fur pelerines are not much worn, but vests of caracul, astrachan, and sable are in vogue for wearing with open-fronted coats. These waist- coats, which are quite close-fitting, are moat useful, as they are very warm, and so render a compara- tively thin jacket wearable in almost the coldest weather. A stylish garmont is a dark blue Hima- laya cloth circular cape, three qua-3r length, lined with Siberian squirrel, with boa and detu.oh- able cape of lovely pale silver fox.</p>
        <p>The Highland cape Is just as fashionable for very young girls as for their mothers and older sisters, and for school wraps nothing could be more convenient. The long coat, really effective and novel, is out something after the style of a man's Newmarket coat, the front and back cut in one piece, and the lapped seams coming just below the waist, giving the appearance of a long back. The skirt splits up behind to the waist, and the loose fronts are double-breasted. This for travelling is perfection. A smart dress for a girl of 14 is of pale majenta face cloth, trimmed with black silk gip and black satin rosettes, embroidered in</p>
        <p>A stylish hat is of black beaver in one of the new- est she.pcs. The crown is high, and is simply trimmed with a folded band of cerise velvet, drawn through a jetted buckle in which two black ostrich tip are fastened. A long black feather rests on the brim, and droops over the hair at the back. The bandeau is made of small black rms. Sailor hats are shown in felt, and are trimmed with satin band round the crown, and two great wings in the front. Very stylish is one in fawn felt, the satin band and wings {wing white. A style suit. able for children from four to nine years is a smart felt in pale lentil shade, trimmed with canary- oolour ostrich plume, with reaeUes of cream satin ribbon.</p>
        <p>Snort Eton and other jackets in black or coloured velveteen will this winter take the place of the velveteen blondes that were so popular last winter. The vest may be of any material harmonising with the skirt. Short jackets fastened at the waist are the latest development of this convenient garment, and they are likely to be truch more generally worn during the winter than the conventional open Eton jacket, although this too will find many to patronise it. Another form of open jacket, very smart when made of velvet or velveteen, is open to the waist in front, but is secured to the plastron to make it quite tight atting, and ends in a basque, flat in front, but in very full flutings at the side and back, where it is rather longer than in front. Very large revere, a deep collar at the back, detached from the revers, large gigot sleeves, and buttons on either side of the waist in front, crim- plete a very fascinating little jAcket for indoor wear; with a waistcoat matching the skirt; it may be worn in the morninu, and it makes a pretty demi•toilette with a light-coloured silk skirt and vest of lace or chiffon. In some of these jackets there is a second ba, gas under the basque of the jacket, and rather longer; this !B also in very hall flutings, and in white, or a much lighter colour than the jacket, and matches the revere, collar, and oafs.</p>
        <p>Yours lovingly, JENNIE.</p>
