      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>SHIP CANAL</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL</p>
        <p>COTTON FOR MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>The Manchester Charterers, Limited, have the s.a. Sir Garnet Wolseley now loading at New Orleans for Man- chester direct. She will be followed by the s.s. Haile- dene, sailing from New Orleans about the 20th Df,c. The same Company are loading the steamer Loughrigv, Holme at Norfolk (Va.), to sail about the 15th Dec. Messrs. Lamport and Holt are berthing the s.s. Biela at New 'York, to sail about the end of the month for Manchester. The s.s. Sailor Prince, from Alexandria with co`ton, was due this week, and the Swedish Prince sailed at the beginning of the week from Alexandria, I also with cotton for Manchester direct. I ACROSS THE ATLANTIC IN Al 3 TON BOAT..</p>
        <p>Both Saturday and Sunday little groups of interested and speculative people could be seen assembled round the little yacht Nina which arrived with her "crew "of one at Latchford Locks, where she lay until Monday. The boat, which is a strongly built two-masted hulk,has a water line of only 401t.° and a tonnage of 13 tons. Taking the yacht all round, the Nina is not the sort of ' boat one would care to cross the Atlantic in. Many of us would prefer to cover the distar,ce between England and New York on the floating palaces of the White Star and other lines, and not trust oneself on a few frail planks to separate us from the dangers of mid-ocean. Yet there is one man who has had the courage to brave the terrors of the deep in the little craft, and that is the present owner and builder of the Nina, Adolph Frietch, a Russian Fin by birth. For many years this daring navigator has sailed schooners on the great lakes of America. The Wmt, which he built himself, took five mcnths to complete. Our representative called on Frietch at the Latchford Locks on Sunday morning, and in answer to a gentle knock at the cabin door found himself in the presence of a burly seafaring man, who looked quite capable of do- ing what he has done. The little cabin of the boat does not admit many persons,and seemed very unsuitable for along sea voyage. The owner of the boat was just finishing his morning meal before the gaze ef a curious band of sightseers. Particulars of the voyage were freely given. A start was made from Sandy Hook, New York, on August sth, when two tug boats and a band cheered the adventurer on his way. Frietch says he started in good spirits, and soon began to get reconciled to his unusual enterprise, which ended at Queenstown on September 1011. Ho had fairly good weather during the 34 days taken in accomplishing the trip, but in the log book on Angust 14th there is an entry which shows that the journey was anything but pleasant. A furious gale sprang up on that day, lasting all night. It must, as Frietch observes, have been a deary prospect to see the sun setting on the Atlantic with the angry waves pound- ing against such a small craft. On the log Frietch con- cludes a vivid description of the weather by explaining that having made all secure ho went down to the cabin where, after indulging in a mutton chop,he slept soundly during the night. Ile also adds that he felt glad that he was by himself, as it would have made him uneasy to have had a companion uneasy with fear. Frietch, who seemsto be a well educated man, says he passed the time away by reading novels and playing an accordion. He left on Monday on his way to Manchester, where the boat will be on exhibition for some days.</p>
        <p>A ship-chandler residing in Brooklyn, America, bears the high-souuding name of Mr. Westminster Abbey. A cod caught off Flan Thorough Head, not long back, was found to have fifty-nine fishholk4, all baited, in his stomach. .</p>
        <p>General Booth has engagemcmts to speak at over five hundred meetings while in the United States, and is to visit not less than seventy different cities. The Grand Duke Paul of Russia is the tallest man in Europe, touching the standard at seven feet nine inches. He is not, however, a pleasant giant, seeing that his hands are covered with hair and that he grinds his teeth in his sleep. The hearing of the St. Helens libel case, in which Councillor Isaac B. Rose sued Mr. Bernard A. Droom- goole, editor and proprietor of the St. Helens "Adver- tiser Newspaper," for damages. was concluded on Tuesday. The jury found for the plaintiff, damages £2OO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>HOUSEHOLD ACCIDENTS.</head>
        <p>HOUSEHOLD ACCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>These are occurring hourly. Either a child is burned or scalded, its finger cut or bruised, perhaps bitten by a dog, scratched by puss, or it may be a fall, a strain, or ono of the thousand vexatious troubles cropping upin the home, in the business, the stable, the hunting field, barracks, on shipboard, the playground, and in the Fretting world—from cycle to steeplechase. It is then that BOMOCEA shines. Apply in all cases freely, and in a very short time all pain is allayed and healing begins. Any nicht baby may have an attack of croup, and just a quarter teaspoonful given internally acts like a charm; also for rubbing the thr,at and chest HOMOCEA is usefu I. Again, what father or mother does not know what it is to be awakened by a child crying with the earache or toothache. Fill the ear with HOMOCEA, or rub the gums, and if the tooth is decayed, put HOMOCEA in, and the business is done.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. C. FEGAN, Writing from " The Boys' Home," 95, Southwark Street, London, S.E , Says: "I know no preparation like Homocea ' for general usefulness In an institution like this. I have thoroughly tested it by personal application, and amongst our boys, for all kinds of pain and accidents IT DOES ALL that is guaranteed to do, and we would not be without It here on any account. It is not only a wonderful lubricant, but strongly antiseptic, and relieves inflam- mation an I pain almost instantaneously. Personally I cannot, express my thankfulness for it. I have used it for all kinds of ailments during the last eight years here, and at sea, and in Canada. For stiffness, sprains, muscular rheumatism, sore throat, mosquito bites, &amp;c., It is a real boon, and no praise can be too high for it. No one need be afraid to use it for even the most tender cart, or even on raw flesh. I have frequently used it for ury eyesight with much benefit. I have always kept • ' HIPPACEA '</p>
        <p>(the veterinary pr-oap-a-ration) for stable Use. For sore backs, broken heels, &amp;c., it is a grand specific." Remember that HomocHA subdues inflammation and alleys irritation almo,t as soon as applied for it "TOUCHES THE SPOT.'</p>
        <p>All wholesale houses stock HOMOCEA. It can be obtained from Chemists and others at ls. 111. or 2s. 9d. per box, or will be sent by post for 1.. 3d. and 3s. from the wholesale agency,2l, Hamilton Square, Birkenhead.</p>
        <p>Sold in the neighbourhood by J. R. YOUNG, SANKEY- STREET, IvARRINGTON ; by the executors of the late ISAAC SPEAKMAN, BRIDGE-STREET PHARMACY, RUN- CORN; J. H. WESTON, DEVONSHIRE BUILDING. RUN- CORN; S. STRINGFELLOW 1, COLLEGE STREET, Si', TIETEN'S ; and by E. BOOTH, 5, CHESTER' BRLDGE, CREWE.</p>
