      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>ASTHMA CAN BE CURED.</head>
        <p>ASTHMA CAN BE CURED.</p>
        <p>A STARTLING STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>Asthma has long been classed amongst the incurable diseases. It has baffled the greatest skill of the medical profession. On the other hand it is remarkable that doctors should for so long a time have clung to a wholly inefficient method of treating it. Asthma is a spasmodic affection of the bronchial tubes of the lungs, and the usual course of treatment calls for the employment of various nauseating drugs which are taken into the stomach. Medicine so taken may in course of time reach the lungs and have a slight effect, just as every- thing taken into the stomach finally acts in some degree upon every part of the system, but the number of failures to cure Asthma in thii way demonstrate too clearly that such treatment is a mistaken one. The only true treatmeut of Asthma is by inhala- tion. By this means the remedy at once reaches the real seat of trouble. Within a comparatively recent period. Dr. R. Schiffmann, who has made the treatment of Asthma and kindred complaints the study of a lifetime, has prepared a remedy known as Schiff- man's Asthma Cure. With this remedy there is no wait- ing for results. Being inhaled it acts directly upon the mucus membrane of the air passages, relieving the spasm and constriction instantaneously, facilitating free expectoration, and insuring comfortable rest to those otherwise unable to sleep except in a chair. Briefly, it effects cures where medicines have failed. The great confidence of the proprietor in his remedy will be readily appreciated when he requests (as he now does) the pub- lisher of this paper to make public announcement of the following offer. Be will refund the money to any person who, having purchased a package of his chemist, finds on giving it a fair trial tLat it does not do as claimed for it. Most chemists have it on sale in two sizes, retailing at 2s. 6d. and 43. 6d. per package, or should any chemist not have it or decline to order it for a sufferer, a package will be sent, post free, on receipt of the amount by Dr.R. Schiffman, (U.S.) 34, T. H. Gray's Inn Road, London, W.C. A most convincing list of testimonials and pamphlets will be sent free to any address.</p>
