      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>"THAT LASS O' LOWRIE'S."</head>
        <p>"THAT LASS O' LOWRIE'S."</p>
        <p>Tune—" The Lass o' Gowrie."</p>
        <p>'Twuz on a scorchin' neet i' June, Sum wheer abeaut at full o'th moon, My Ailie in her wooden shoon, Coom o'er th' big broo fro Lowries ; Her honds wur cowd, her e'en wur red, Aw th' pure wur toozlet on her yed Et if hoo'd just jump'd eawt o' bed, That bonnie lass o' Lowrie's. Eh! but aw na'er geet such a start ! Wen into th' heause hoo made a dart,— Hen fair upset my appo cart, That lovely lass o' Lowrie's. Mi mother placed her on a cheer, An' ax't " Wot's th' matter wi' thi, dear— Its thee to speyk, an' me to hear, My darlin' lass o' Lowrie's." An' then hoo took her on her knee, An' sed " Wotever con it be That favvers so distressin' thee ? My bonnie lass o' Lowrie's, Come, tell mi freely, if theau con, An' if its owt abeaut a mon, Ascii try an' mek thi grief bnone, My charmin' lass o' Lowrie's." "Well, mother, ez yo want to know Wots causin' rue this grief an' woe, Its one that's near an' dear to yo— That's blighted th' lass o' Lowrie's. Aw've seen yore Tummy on a bench, A-coortin' wi' another wench, An' that's wot's gien my heart a wrench— .% swears this lass o' Lowrie's." A change coom o'er her then. by Gyow I An' aw ne'er heerd sitch a row, It fairly rattle't throo my now, That tung o'th' lass o' Lowrie's. Aw never thowt ez hoo cud use Sitch words ez villins, sceaundrels, foots! No Scriptur' inspirations thoose, That coom fro' th' lass o' Lowrie's. Then hoo begun to use her clogs Agen ml shins wi' savage jr,ge, An' played Owd Harry wi' ml "togs." Did that wild lass o' Lowrie's. At lung length, wen her wynt wur spent, An' aw her grief hed getten vent, Like to a fricken't hare hoo went, O'er that big broo to Lowrie's. Aw needn't say hoo's gen ml th' seek, An' aw con noather drink ner peck' • Aw fear aw's't turn a perfect wreck, Throo that sweet lass o' Lowrie's: Wile ez aw lie awake at neet, Fro' th' darkenin' to th' mornin' leet, Aw con but own it sarves ml reet— Aw've lost that lass o' Lowrie's. TEM 0' PEGS.</p>
        <p>Rose Bonk, 7th Dec., 1894. -.-.</p>
        <p>A panic occurred at a school children's entertainment in Belfast on Wednesday evening owing to the gas going out, and a number of .chilaren were in-hired in the crush.</p>
