      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Coun- cil was held on Tuesday in the Town Hall. The Mayor (Councillor J. Fairclough) presided, and there were also present, Aldermen Harrison, Sutton, Bolton, Francomb, Wright, Hutchinson ; Councillors Monks, Plumpton, Shaw Green, Baird, Hill, Skelton, Evans, R. T. Fair- dough, Roberts, Loagton, Muir, Bennett, Smethurst, Shillcock, Lee, Davies, Elepherd, Woods, Cannell ; Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Mr. J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), and Mr. T. Longdin (borough surveyor). THE LATE MR. VOISEY, J.P. The MAYOR said they had all heard with deep regret of the death of Mr. Voisey, and he moved the following; reselution :—" That this Council receives with great re- gret the announcement of the death of Mr. Lewis Voisey, Esq., J.P., formerly a member of this Council, and desires to express its sincere sympathy and condolence with Mrs; Voisey and the members of her family in their sad bereavement." Alderman SUTTON seconded the motion, and remarked that Mr. Voisey took a deep interest in everything con- cerning the welfare of Warrington. Councillor ROBERTS, in supporting it, said he quite agreed with all that had been said with regard to the late Mr. Voisey, who was always a most courteous and gentlemanly man in public and in private. His abilities were of the highest order in his profession, and he was a man who did good service to Warrington in more capa- cities than one. (Hear,hesr.) The town, as well as Mr. Voisey's family, had sufferod a greet loss by his death. Alderman gaRRISON and Councillor BENNETT also wished to join in the general expression of regret and sympathy that had been made with reference to the late Mr. Voisey. The resolution was unanimously agreed to. WATCH COMMITTEE.—COUNCILLOR ROBERTS' SPEECH AT THE WYCLIFFE P.S.A. The MAYOR moved and Alderman HARRISON seconded the confirmation ef this committee's minutes, whieh contained the following extract from a letter from the Rev. D. W. Roberts to the Home Secretary. "I maintain that the only difficulty in the way was this, that if they dealt effectually with certain houses it would have resulted • in bringing before the Warrington Bench of Magistrates some prominent Town Councillors," and also a letter from the Home Secretary to the Watch Committee, which appeared in our last issue. Councillor ROBERTS said he did not intend to move any amendment to the minutes, because if he did he should probably be told by the Town Clerk he was out of order. and he did not intend running the risk of being ruled out of order. But he wished to disassociate himself from any implied approbation of the letters which ap- peared on the minutes. If the whole of the correspond- ence had been puelished then there might have been subject matter for discussion on fair terms but as only part of the correspondence had been published he sub- mitted it was not fair to the gentleman implicated, and as one of the jury he could not give a verdict in favour of the plaintiffs until he heard the whole of the evi- dence. He thought it would have been much better to have left the correspondence out altogether or else have published the whole. Councillor SMETHURST endorsed what Cooncillor Roberts had said. He thought it was only fair that they should have had the full correspondence placed before them or otherwise left out. It was very unkind that any gentleman should be accused of what was suggested in the letter of the Rev. Mr. Roberts. Councillor SKELTON said he must enter his pro- test against the statement made by the Rev. Mr. Roberts. He submitted that all the correspondence in the world could not possibly change the meaning of the charge. The paragraph reflected upon every member of the Council. Speaking personally, he challenged the Rev.Mr.Roberts or anyone else to prove he had ever been guilty of gambling. The statement ought not to have been made unless Mr. Roberts was prepared to substantiate it by giving the names of the members to whom he was alluding. Councillor Woons asked if the Committee were pre- pared to take some action with regard to the accusation made 'by the Rev. D. W. Roberts. He thought Mr. Roberts' statement was a most insulting one. Of course, it all depended on who were prominent members of the Council. He supposed the most prominent were Coun- cillor Roberts and Councillor Green. (Laughter.) Councillor SHAW GREEN was surprised to be classed a- mong the prominent councillors. He could however say that he did not gamble, and therefore the Rev. D. W. Roberts' statement did not refer to him. He should like to see the whole of the letter published. If two or three lines were worth quoting, surely the whole of it was. He moved that the whole of the correspondence be published. Alderman SurroN said he was glad the subject had been brought before the Council, because it was too serious a matter as affecting the members of the Council to let it pass by unnoticed. The honour and character of several members of the Council were implicated to some extent by the .statement made by the Rev. Mr. Roberts. It was very clever on the part of Councillor Roberts to endeavour to make this statement something different to what it was, but with all his cleverness be would not be able to convince the Council that it was- something different to what it appeared on the agenda. He himself asked several members of the Council, and they emphatically denied geing to this gambling-house or at all events taking any part in gambling. The police had been prevented doing their duty by certain persons interfering and meddling with them. He thought they would all be satisfied with the reply of the Home Secretary to the letter sent by the Clerk to the borough magistrates. Alderman HARRISON characterised the Rev. D. W. _</p>
        <p>'Roberts' assertion as a very rash and bold one. As an -educated man he ought to have known how important it was and how careful he should have been unless he had the most satisfactory evidence in proof of his asser- tion. That evidence he did not appear to have had. But while the Rev. D. W. Roberts was a sinner in a small degree, he should like to ask them what they thought of Councillor Roberts, who went to Wycliffe -Church two or three weeks ago and delivered him- self— Councillor ROBERTS : I rise to a point of order. If my speech at Wycliffe P.S.A. is going to be discussed here I am prepared to give a replybut I ask whether the discussion is in order or not. Alderman HARRISON : I only introduce it because I consider it a foul, calumny. Councillor ROBERTS : You may consider it what you like ; I am prepared to stand by what I said. Alderman HARRISON : lam justified as a member of the Watch Committee in bringing this matter up and exposins it not only to the Town Council of Warrington but to the public who have been very much misled and very much astonished. I have a right by way of illustration to quote a few lines Mr. Roberts was pleased to deliver himself of in the Wycliffe Church. I await your decision, Mr. Mayor. The MAYOR : You are in order. Councillor ROBERTS ; May I ask for the right of reply. The MAYOR: Ido not think you have: The Town Clerk rules that you have no reply. Councillor ROBERTS : Am I to be .attacked in this manner and have no reply ? I shall be prepared to reply if you will allow me. The Tows CLERK : According to the Standing Orders you have no reply. Councillor PLumr-ros asked if the discussion was in order. The MAYOR : I rule it is in order. Of course, Councillor Roberts may rise to an explanation. • Alderman HARRISON then attempted to read a quota- tion; from the " Examiner" containing a report of Councillor Roberts' speech at Wycliffe Church, but as he had not got his spectacles he was unable to do so, and Alderman Sutton read it for him, as follows :—" This Council of ours had a Watch Committee He thought</p>
        <p>the term "Watch Committee" was entirely a misnomer as it was more of a publicans' prevetion committee. Instead of looking to tht interests of the public of War- rington it seemed to have been a committee purely and simply for the purpose of protecting These few specially favoured citizens. It was a grand jury for the purpose of hearing cases in private which should be dealt with by the magistrates, and where it could, it cut the bill. He would draw no cohclusions.but he would leave them to draw their own. It was a very remarkable thing when it was possible for that grand jury—the Watch Committee—to hear cases against publicans and then to cut the bill." He (Alderman Harrison) begged to state in the most emphatic manner that that was one of the most mendacious statements a man ever uttered. There was absolutely not one word of truth in it. He had been a member of the Watch Committee for many years. He had been its Chairman arid was now its deputy- chairman, and he declared there was no substratum of truth in the statement that the Committee either by itself or any of its members had ever sat in judgment upon a case which ought to have been taken before the magistrates. It had been broadly stated that the Watch Committee was simply a grand jury and that they sat to hear deliberately cases that ought to be heard before the magistrates. He asked them not to believe a word of it. It was absolutely untrue in every particular. The thing had arisen in this way ; formerly the chief con- stable and the magistrates' clerk conferred together and the chief-constable took the clerk's opinion before taking proceedings. About twelve months ago the magistrates clerk was ill, and Mr. Hunt had a csse with s3me pecu- liarly difficult features about it. He went to the Watch Committee about it. The committee considered the case, and advised him to adopt a certain course, which was followed. Afterwards when Mr,llunt found he had any difficult case to deal with, he used to come to the Watch Committee, and the Committee at last arranged that before Mr. Hunt to ,k a case before the magistrates and incurred expere-e, he should first submit the case to the Watch Committee. That was the whole head and front of their offending. They had never sat, as Coun- cillor Roberts said, solemnly at a table, heard evidence discused the pros and cons and then decided there was no case. Counciller Roberts said the committee en- deavoured "to cut the bill." Could they imagine any- thing more monstrous than for a public body to abuse its trust and position in the way suggested and prevent men who were breaking the law from being brought before the magistrates ? Mr. Roberts' statement would not beer the light of investigation. Mr. Hunt gave a fiat</p>
        <p>contradiction to some other statements made, and he distinctly said that the Watch Committee never acted in the way described. They had always acted in a way which would conduce to the credit of the town and to the proper administration of justice. Councillor LONGTON alluding to the extract from the Rev. D. W. Robert's letter said that the charge really bore upon every member of the Town Council. Was it necessary for ev,ervone to rise and declare their own innocency? (No ! No !) Councillor DAVIES said that he took it that the matter before the Council was a portion of a communication from the rev, gentleman to the Home Secretary. It seemed to touch the corns of a good many membors,and they all seemed to be " prominent " Town Councillors. (Laughter). He would like to ask the Mayor, as chair- man of the Watch Committee whether he had any objection to the whole correspondence being submitted La the Council. He thought it was unfair, as Councillor Shaw Green had said, that the Council should hear only one part of the story. It had been complained very strongly that the Watch Committee had heard one part of a story, and a portion of them being magistrates, they had gone and heard the story over again at the police court. He had no doubt—and he did not cast any reflection upon them—thar a portion of the committee went to the court biassed to a certain extent. He did not think it fair to the solicitor, to the prisoner, or to the other magistratas. Of course Alderman Harrison said the cases were not tried. The Watch Committee might not be a grand jury, but still they had heard a portion of the case. It was well-known that if a poor person was charged with any crime, he was taken down to the bridewell without bail, and then brought before the magistrates next morning. If he was a respectable person, he was reported to the Watch Committee, and the committee could say whether the case should go before the court or not. He could give names privately. Alderman SUTTON: They are all imaginary cases. Councillor DaviEs (addressing the Mayor) : You would have no objection to the correspondence being published. The MAYOR: I could not say that. Alderman SurroN : I have read it. Councillor SHAW GREEN: You are fortunate. Councillor DAVIES: (continuing) said that it was not fair play, because the letter referred to the whole Town Council. The Watch Committee were not the Only per- sons referred to, and it was absurd that they should be the only persons to see'it. The TOWN CLERK : That extract is the only part referring to the Town Council. • Councillor DAVIES said that he was not going to say that he had never made a bet. The last het he had made was with Councillor Roberts. (Loud laughter), He was also glad to say that he won. and that Councillor Roberts paid him. (Renewed laughter). But as re- garded betting in houses used for that purnose,he would be the last man in Warrington to do so. He intended to second Councillor Shaw Green's motion that the corres- pondence be published. The TOWN CLERK ruled that the motion could not be put to the Council. Councillor DAVIES reiterated that it was a reflection upon the whole Council. The MAYOR: I should treat it with contemot.</p>
        <p>Councillor DAVIES We might do that if we had the chance of seeing the letter. Alderman FRANCOMB said that the other portion of the correspondence was in connection, with the police authorities and it would not be wise to reveal it to the public. He denied that cases were tried before the Watch Committee, and the only CAW'S brought ',gore the committee were those against publicans, and if 111 r. Hunt. and the Town Clerk, and the members of the Watch Committee believed the case worthy of sending before the magistrates that was always done. No case sufficiently strong was ever kept back. He had been a member of the Watch Committ.e for ten or twelve years and had never known a publican to get off, if there was a chance of conviction at the police court.</p>
        <p>Alderman -BOLTON. as one who had been on the Watch Committee, and also belenc.,ine to the trade which cer- tain members of the Council were so fond of shrine'</p>
        <p>bricks at, said that as far as he knew, all cases agrsinst the publicans hsd been left to the discretion of the Chief Constable. If Mr. Hunt was in denht, as to whether there was a clear case, he came to the Town Clerk for advice, and the Watch Committee were then informed and took the responsibility of.cases. Councillor ROBERTS then rose to make a personal ex- planation. He said that he would not attempt to fTlow his friend Alderman Harrison in his remarkAhlv had</p>
        <p>tempered effusion. because he did not think he was and therefore he would not blame him for his exhibition of temper. All he had got to say was this. He mad. these statements upon the strenoth of information given by no less a person than their own Chief Constable. The Chief Constable had been quoted against him that night and he was not going to stand under the accusation of not being capable of speaking the truth unnn that matter, which was a public matter. Re would say in explanation of what he had already stated that the Chief Constable had told him privately, and had exmanlained to the magistrates sitting in committee that he had re- ceived certain instructions twelve months last o,•toher. that he should from that date onwards bring all cases</p>
        <p>against the publican, before the Watch Committee. and should act upon their instructions. Previous to then,he received no instructions of that character, and alvrars took action up-m those eases on the advice of the ma7ig- trate's clerk. He would tell them that he took nritec- Con upon that, and he repeated that- hP was nos- czpins),, to have the Chief Constable or anybody else quoted aalinst him without givin7 his authority. He e,laimed protec- tion because the ChiefeConstahle had said delihnra,f•elv</p>
        <p>, ..„ that he had been f4tered by the aetinn of the vntrh Committee. He had therefore nothing withclraw.and nothing to repeat. Alderman HARRISON. —Mr. Hunt unequiyocably denies it. _ .</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS.—Tin can donv it all it tin FINANCE COMMITTEE.—RETIREMENT OF ALDERMAN HOLMF.S.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>_l.-...</p>
        <p>-----</p>
        <p>-I-</p>
        <p>inco me or profits for the last three years ha been, but his household and personal expenses amounted to £l5O a year. The net liabilities amounted to £472 15s. 7d. and the assets to £514 17s. 10d.—In reply to Mr. A. G. Potter, the assistant official receiver, the bankrupt said that he left Warrington on Oct. 15th and went to Liverpool. He went there because he did not wish to see his things sold, lie did nothing in Liverpool ; and then went to Birkenhead for a few hours, and then to Cardiff. Be tried there to find 'e place to commence business again sometime, as he heard that trade was very good in Cardiff, He staved in that place one week, and then went to Newport, Monmouth- shire, where he stayed four days. He was there that period of time bec tuse a gentleman promised him a job but he was disappointed, arid went from Newport to Hereford, and thence to Ledbury, where he was with friends. He was brooght back to Warrington by his niece. He took £lO with him when he left Warrington, which he realized by the sale of a pony to Mr. Kenyon. When he got to Herefordshire, he had no money left. He had tried to find his books since the sale, when they had almost all disappeared, but had not applied to the auctioneer or the Sheriff's officer. His books did not show all his business transactions, and did not show how much he was worth. He had been rather intemperate lately, and had had domestic trouble. On October 2nd, he was asked by Messrs. Davies and Forshaw to sign a deed of assignment, but he did not do so because he was , drinking. With regard to the pony, he did not think of going away when he sold it ,at the end of September. He got £l2 for it, of which he spend £2 before he went away. He did not spend more than £2 in drinking dur- ing the fortnight before he went away. He had been or the spree more or less, since July 28. He did not go away for the deliberate object of avoiding his creditors. He did not leave his address with anyone, and bad no idea of going to Cardiff. He was sober when Ise left We rri ngt on . He first heard of trade being good in Car- diff at Birkenhead. He intended to come back the same night to Warrington, and had no idea of staying away as long as he did.—The hearing was adjourned for a fortnight.</p>
      </div>
