        <head>E KAIVIii NEP, SATURDAY DECEMBER 8 1894.</head>
        <p>E KAIVIii NEP, SATURDAY DECEMBER 8 1894.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR seconded the passing of the minutes and they were carried unanimously. STREET IMPROVEMENT COMMITTEE. AldErman SUTTON moved and Alderman WRIGHT seconded the adoption of this committee's minutes. The former said that by the purchase of two cottages in Cambria Place a very good passage would be made.— The minutes were passed unanimously. HEALTH COMMITTEE.—COUNCILLOR SHAW OBJECTS. Alderman SUTTON proposed and Councillor ROBERTS seconded this committee's minutes. Councillor SHAW, as a member of the Health Commit- tee objected to the appointment of Councillor Roberts as deputy chairman of the committee. Tha MAYOR ruled him out of order, but Councillor Shaw'persisted, when the Mayor again told him to sit down, and the minutes were passel unanimously. .±,NITARY WORKS COMMITTEE. This committee's minutes, which mainly consisted of the appointment of officers, were passed on the motion of Councillor MONKS, seconded by Councillor SH ILLCOCK. PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE. Alderman WRIGHT in moving the adoption of the minutes said that the Padgate drainage system wasgoing on quite satisfactory. Councillor BAIRD seconded the minutes which were passed unanimously. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE. The minutes were proposed for adoption by Councillor SHAW G us, a,ll seconded by Councillor SHILLCOCK. Councillor WOODS said that the Education Department had passed the plans for St. George's Mission, and the town would now be free from the horrors of a School Board. (Laughter.) musEmkt ETC., COMMITTEE. This committees minutes contained the follow. ing reccornmendation "The General Purposes Comi- mittee has resolved that, on the request of the Museum Committee, a suitable temporary build- ing be purchased for the reception of large objects for which there is no space in the Museum, and that such building be erected on the vacant space adjoining the Art Gallery. The Committee has also decided to give notice to terminate the tenancy of the vacant piece of land at the wet side of the Art Gallery. and also the following lists of new books:—Fish wick's History of Lancashire, Stubh's Lectures on History, Lethahv's Lead-work. Smith's Man ,the Primeval Savage, Dofferin's Poems and Songs, O'Rell's John Bull and Co.. Le esllienne's Religion of a L,tera,ry Man, Chambers' Elocution, Astlev's Fifty Years of mr-Lifn,Lugard's Rise of our East African Empire, Sladon's Japs at Home, Rusoell's Hidden Chain, Cholmondelev's_Diana Tempest, Steele's; Clove Pink. Eiwarde's Adventuress.Bla,tchford's Son of the Forge,Weynaan's Under the Red Robe. Black's Highland Cousins, Blackmore's Perlycross, Caine's Manxman, Caldwell's Sermon at Newnarke, Watt's Dictionary of Chemistry, v. 4, Parkes' Manual of Prac- tical Hygiene, Teale's Dangers to Health, Dent's Making of Birmingham. Picturesque Palestine, Hornung's Un hidden Guest. Clegg's David's Loom, Twain's Tom Saw- yer Abroad, Haddon's Handbook to Local Governmen- Act. 1894,Cumming's Wanderings in China, Swinhurne,, Studies in Prose and Poetry. Parliamentary Papers, (En- dowedCharities,UnitedStatesTariff,Educationßeport,&amp;c.</p>
        <p>In his house there could not be better gas to be obtained Anyone could come and see it. Alderman BOLTON: The next time my gas is like last Saturday, I'll come. (Lawhter.) Councillor FAIRCLOUGH said that it was a strange thing that complaints were made so often. He was sure that something was wrong in the fittings, and if persons would not send to the gas works to have them attended, they must take the consequences. They ought to go to the gas works to complain, and not come to the Town Council. The minutes were tl, &gt;n, passed unanimously.</p>
        <p>RIVERS COMMITTEE— :RADERS AND THE SHIP CANAL. Councillor ROBERTS n •ved the adoption of the minutes, and gave a long expla Ltion as to the present position of Warrington with r.i:.;ard to the Ship Canal. He asked why the traders did not enter the Council and thus take an active interest in Ship Canal matters. Instead of doing so they had stood aloof from the Council tel years. As long as traders cho4e to remain outside the Council they must take the responsibility of what the Council did. (Hear, hear). In not one single instance had the Corporation given anything to the Ship Canal Company. It was most unfair that they should be attacked, in the manner they had been by an anonymous writer who knew the truth and concealed it, or who did not know the truth and made no attempt to discover it. (Hear, hear. Alderman SUTTON said the traders bad taken very little interest in Ship Canal matters, Councillor HEPHERD said the Canal Company had been subject to a fine of £5O a day from the Ist of July to the Ist of October, in consequence of net keeping up their undertaking with the Corporation as to water rights, and that penalty had been taken off by the Com- mittee without the consent of the Council. Councillor DAVIES said they were more interested in the Ship Canal than any other town in the kingdom. They had a free river to Liverpool, and they could meet the Ship Canal with a stronger hand than any other Corporation. He thought it was a mistake in giving up the fines. He felt that the committee had been inactive. Within two months of the opening of the canal the firm with which the Mayor was connected had to bring an action against the Canal Company, and in his opinion the Warrington Corporation ought to have been plaintiffs as well. Councillor SMETHURST said the Canal company guaranteed a certain height of water in th river. By the removal of the bar at Liverpool the tide came in and Went out quicker than before, and he did not believe they would be able t) keep the quantity of water they wanted for the canal. Councillor Roberts, in replying to the debate denied that the committee had been inactive, and promised that everything which was done by the committee should be with the full consent of the Council. The minutes were agreed to. PROPOSED ALTERATION OF THE TOWN HALL. Councillor R. T. FAIRCLOUGH moved the following resolution of which he had given notice :—" That the Town Hall, Park and Baths Committee consider and report to the Council whether the present buildings of lofts and stables can be conveniently adapted for offices, connected by a covered way with the Town Hall." After a short discussion, the resolution was passed, and the proceedings of the Council terminated.</p>
        <p>—The followine gifts are reported and the thanks of the Committee were ordered to be sent to the donor : Fraffmentnf Roman Amphora, Nina at Wildersnnol ; and the account hook of the Constable of Dareshury from 1712 to 1741, from Mr. S. F. Widdrington,Per. Mr. Rohert Davieq ; fraument, of sword-scabbard dug tip in Sankey- street, from Mr. A. Mounfield ; snecimens of leather and tannin. materials (36), from Messrs. E. and J. Richard- son ; Water Vole and Water Shrew, from Mr, G. Mnun- field ; Bronze Fibulae (5) from Wilderspool ; and an iron nadlock, from Mr. R. Kendrick ; " Proceeding's of th. Warrinetnn Literary and Philosnnhical Society for 1893-4," from the society ; "The New Encirclnpedia ," by 1,11, 2 v., from Mr. J. Auckland ; Parliamentary Paners (13), from Mr. R. Piernoint, M.P. " CnllPctiAn of the</p>
        <p>Writinva of llncyar Tfavdoroz. 1700." and " Beautiful linuge9,," by Haweia, frnrn Mr. Inaenh Clare. Councillor ROBERTS in movinq the adoption of the</p>
        <p>minutes. said that more room was required for exhibits in the Museum. There were articles recovered from the river diversion of considerable local inl-ereat. It was urnbable that if they had the increased seff)mmodation. they wr,,ild have further gifts of that character before lone,. There was also increa sea accmmolation required for a class in modelling in the S^hool of Art, as it was necessary that this should he encouraged. Councillor MONKS S.nnnlei the adoption of the</p>
        <p>minutes which were carried pnanimnpsly. • CEMETERY COMMITTEE. ThiS Cle)MMittP'S minptpa, which anneared in (pr last icapp, were passed pnanimnpalv on thp motion of Alder- man TIARRISON.!Sr.ennripd hv enpncillor Amt. TOWN FALL AND MARRETS COMMITTEES. The mirmtes of these C,nmmittppa which alan appeared in ()Pr last iaspe were passed, thp former, on the motion of Alderman BOLTON, Secneded by Ceuncillnr CANNELL,. and the latter on the mntinn of Alderman FluTcruNsori, qpnonderl by emArillnr ITEPFIERD. GAS COMMITTEE. —CONFLTCTING REPORTS UPON TITE STATE OF THE GAS. Conncillnr Fainctoport in moving the adoption of the _ _</p>
        <p>minutes of this rnmmittpr4 saifi that hp wnnld give an xnlanat in n to Alderman Harrison who stated that he. had to ffe, tl bed some time ago heeanse thP gas was not asit should be. The rearnn was because a joint in the pines broke whereby a quantity of air cot into the mains, and conl,i not he atono.d for sonic time. Tt was a matter which could not he helped, and could not he fore,,een. Conncillor FIRPHERD seconded the adoption of the</p>
        <p>minutea, and exnlained that twelve mnntha ac2o, Fairelontrh tv.cam. chairman nf the Rivera Com- mittee, and he therefore requeqted tha Ga,a Committee to am-mint him (Councillor 11,,nherril to the chairmanahin. Nnw that Councillor Fairelnnqh had regigned the e, hair-</p>
        <p>manship M thn Rivers CommitteP, thp Gas Committee wPrP nnanimnns in Plectinq him chairman. Cm- illor Worms said that it v7a.s satisfactory to hr</p>
        <p>Councillor SHNNV GREEN in moving the adoption of the ''iunci at last, an explanation of something connected with the Finance Committee's minutes, said that Alderman Holmes' name was not. upon the committee that year.. qas works. This was the first explanatiou he had ever heard. It was a unique record in the hitory Af any Cornoratinn, Fair- that one memher should for 33 years nonsenutively have Alderman HAnnisov said that while Councillor Fair- clnugh's explanation was satisfactory as far as it went, been the Chairman of the Finance or any other commit- he must. say that there was still no material improve- tee. In speaking of Al derman IFlolmes' retirement from ment in the illuminatinr rower of the gas. that position, it would he well to show the changes in that department sinrehe had first-occunied ,hat rositinn. A MEMBER: The gas is all right here. The borough debt 33 years ago was -c!46,200, and th ( Alderman HAnntsow :It is no great shakes here. debt on March 25th lest. .was £579.427. The hornnohn Lang Alderman WRIGHT: It is only halfenek. receipts then were £1.7.215, now they were £165737. The MAYOR sail that he thnueth Pre was something That was an evidence of the enormous and rapid growth wron g with the fittings in Alderman Harrison's house of the borough.</p>
        <p>Eczema, Rabies, Sunburn, all Skin Affections, Stiff Joints, Twinges in the back, Toothache, Chilblains, Lumbago, Strained Muscles, Rheumatism of years standing can be got rid of at once with</p>
        <p>another were decided upon he should wish the wearing of it to be enforced.—The &amp;fendants were dismissed on condition that they wore the badges until further notice. —Mr. Hunt said these men had in a great measure been mixed up in the charges against the pro- prietor'. The proprietors held the . men re- sponsihle. for the wearing of the badges and postin ; up of the list of fares.</p>
