      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>'BUS DRIVERS AND THEIR BADGES.</head>
        <p>'BUS DRIVERS AND THEIR BADGES.</p>
        <p>PROSECUTIONS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court.on Monday morning, be- fore the Mayor (chairman) and other magistrates, the following 17 'bus and cab drivers were charged that, on the 26th November last in Warrington, being the licensed drivers of hackney carriages, they unlawfully while driving, failed to wear attached to their outer clothing their badges :—Edward Holt, 277, Knutsford- read ; Benjamin Millichap,477,Knutsford-road ; William Simmons, 48, Wilderapool-road ; Joseph Clooney, 127, Knutsford-roa,d ; John McClinton, 77, New-road ; Thos. Ruane, 16, King-st. ; John Johnson,s, Ellesmere-street ; Wm. Cliffe, 350, Knutsford-road ; Peter Baguley, 3, Miller-st. ; Thomas Maguire, 80, Winwick-at. ; Edward Goulding, 71. Mersey-st. ;Joseph Williams, 3, Wharf-st. ; John Heavy, 37, Orford-st. ; Frank Wilson, 68, New- road ; Percy Woods, 15, Albert-at. ;John Greenough, 31, Golborne-st. ; Patrick McCormick, 31, Orford-st.—Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, appeared for the defendants, and on their behalf pleaded not guilty.—Mr. Hunt (chief constable) prosecuted, and said that the charge was brought against these men very unwillingly, but it was necessary that something should be done. Inspector Bo- wen had warned them all about not wearing their badges and not having the list of fares. All these men had been seen without their badges. Ha added that a large number of 'busdrivers had a strong objection to wearing such a large badge when a smaller one wauld have an- swered all purposes in the narrow streets of Warringtion. These badges should be wcrn outside the coats of the drivers, but Mr. Hunt was led to be- lieve that all was not right, so he sent Inspector Spinks with Sergeant Cheney and four con- stables last Monday morning to look round. The result was that defendants were caught without badges.—Mr. Browne said that in the case of Woods, he was cleaning his carriage ane had taken his coat off with the badge on it.—lnspector Spinks : 'I his is the second time.—Mr. Browne (continuing) pointed out that the bye-laws were not complied with in the marking of the badges and h erefore thought that all the defendants should be dis- missed but the bench ruled otherwise. In some cases, Mr. Browne said, the men had their rugs pulled over their badges thus obscuring them. The men thought that the badges were much too large for Warrington, and he proposed that smaller ones should be made to fit on the men's hats where they would always be visible. When the drivers had their oilskins on it was impossible to fasten the badges outside.—P.C. Amson said that he had seen 11 drivers at the Central station without badge;.—P.C. Chrimes deposed to seeing eight at the same place without badges.—Five were seen by Sergeant Cheney without badges.—Alderman Harrison said the men had only to go to the Watch Committee and they would have the badges altered reasonably.— Mr. Hunt said that, though he had received complaints from different people about the irregular wearing of the badges, he did not wish to press the case.— Inspector Bowen said he had procured several kinds of badges from the Hackney Carriage Company and shown them to different drivers who had selected the one in question. He said that whenever he appeared out came the badges, but as soon as he was gone they were put in again.—Mr. Monks said that if the Watch Committee were appr ,ached they would modify the badges, and if</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST THE PROPRIETORS.</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST THE PROPRIETORS.</p>
        <p>The following persons were then charged with being proprietors of hackney carriages and unlawfully failing to have a list of fares in the carriages on the 28th Nov,mber :—Samuel Wilson. the Feathers Hotel; Wm. Russell, Suez-street; Mary Ellen Kiernan, Patten Arms ' Hotel; Edward Connor, Sankey-street; James Ken rick, manager of Warrington Carriage Co.—P.C. Amson deposed to seeing a cab belonging to Mrs. Kiernan of the " Patten Arms" without a list of fares inside. He also saw one belonging to the Lion Hotel with the same deficiency.—Mr.Browne said that the" fares" sometimes tore down the lists which were only of card. If, like in other towns, the lists were on metal plates they would not be destroyed so easily. It would take about an hour to deface those.—Alderman Harrison : I coula deface one in two minutes.—Mr. Monks: You are cleverer than other people. —Alderman Harrison: I can't help that.— These defendants were also dismissed. L A PROPRIETOR FINED. *ran, 30, Chorley-street, was charged with dri %b, not being a licensed driver and Edward Connor with employing him. Both pleaded not guilty. P.C. Turner said he saw Hatton driving a cab- without having a badge on, and on being questioned he said he was lilt licensed. Mr. Connor said Hatton was only driving the cab because the proper driver was away. Both were fined 2s. 6d.—Mr. Hunt added that these regulations would be looked after more severely in the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>ORDINATION SERVICE AT EMMANUEL CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ORDINATION SERVICE AT EMMANUEL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>An event which is not frequently witnessed in Warrington took place on Friday afternoon at Emmanuel Church, Bold-street. This was the ordination of a clergyman into the Reformed Church of England. The servio is similar to that of the Established Church, but in the Established Church the ceremony is always performed in a cathedral city. The minister to be ordained as a Presbytor was the RA,. T. W. Gladstone, of Eccles (Trinity Church).who was previously in deacons' orders. There was a fair attendance at the service. The pro- ceedings commenced with a hymn, and the reading of the litany by the Rev. E. T. Reed. The Rev. Dr. Bow- man, of Christ Church, Liscard, then preached an ex- cellent sermon from Timothy iv. 16. after which the candidate for ordination was presented by the Rev. Dr. Bowman. The ordination service was conducted in a very impressive manner by the Right Rev. Bishop Eldridge, the presiding bishop .of the Reformed Episco- pal Church. A communion service was then held, con- ducted by the Bishop, and the Rev. E. T. Reed, and the ceremony was then brought to a close. The members of the church afterwards sat down to a tea in the schoolroom, Arpley Station, and when the tables were cleared, a public meeting was held. at which there was a very good attendance. The Rev. E. T. Reed Presided and he was supported on the platform by Bishop Eldridge and Messrs. Gandy, Allen and Leah After devotional exercises, the choir sang Vie anthem " As the hart panteth. The CHAIRMAN in the course of an excellent address,he said he was glad that a division which occurred in the Reformed Church had been brought to a happy close in May last. This was due to a great extent to Bishop Eldridge. It was a very important event as the battle of the future in the Christian churches would be a battle between saneflotalism and non-sacedotalism. This was really the battle of the Reformation over again. The Christian ministry was not to stand between the sinner and the Saviour but to lead the sinner to the Saviour. Wherever the priestly idea prevailed they would find ignorance and not prosperity in commercial affairs, but where the opposite was the case, they found nrospeity. It was therefore of the highest importance that this should he armed. Cardinal Vaughan had recently said that the ministers in the Church of England were doing the Roman Catholic work in that church,and Bishop Bsle had recently referred to the same fact in very mournful term.. Though the Reformed Church was but a small church, he believed that God had raised them up to he an antidote to this sacerdotalism, and he hoped that it would proceed in its work of evangelising the country. Re believed that in their own church there were many signs to encourage them. The mernhErs of the senior and junior Christian Endeavour Society then sang a number of endeavour hymns. Bishop ELDRIDGE cave an able address upon "the rapid advance of sacredotalism in the Church of England and the need which exists for the Reformed Episcopal Church." The meeting el °Red with the usual votes of thanks. • FAILURE OF A WARRINGTON BUILDER.</p>
        <p>John Vaughen Lloyd, 72 Selby-street, off Lovely- lane, joiner and builder, appeared to undergo his Public examination at the Warrington Bankruptcy Court- on Friday. before Mr. Registrar Ridg way. Mr. Forshaw appeared on behalf of the Warrington Slate Company. Mr. A. Browne for Mr. R. A. Naylor, and Mr. E. A. White for Mr. Selby. The bankrupt alleged as the cause of tailure,the notice to quit given by the landlord of his hnsiness premises, and the actions brought by two of his creditors which stopped his busi- ness. Re statod that he commenced business as a joiner and builder in Warrington about 17 years ago, with a capital of P6O, his sayings. He alleged that all his recent books had bee' lost, hut he had kept a cash book, containing a complete record ef his business receipts and payments, exeent payments by cheque. He ad- mittei that his ledgers only disclosed accounts with his debtors, and did no, contain any capital account, or trading. and profit and loss accounts, or accounts of his trading expenses, and withdrawals for household and personal expenses ; that his books had not been balanced at any time during the last three years, and that he had not during that. time taken stock. He further stated that he first became aware that he could not pay his debts as they became due, out of his own monersiabout the end of Sr:pi-ember, and that he had pot contracted any dehts with a knowledge of in- solvoncy. He was unable to state what his</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>MAYOR'S "AT HOME."</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S "AT HOME."</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Councillor James Fairclough) held his first " aChome,'" on Wednesday afternoon at the Town Hall. The Council Chamber and Hall were brilliantly decorated with plants and art muslin. The musical part of the proceedings was efficiently undertaken by Mr. Pearson's band, and the duties of caterer by Mr. Powys, the hallkeeper. The fol- lowing is the full list of the visitors : . _ _ _</p>
