d Mrs C H. Unsworth, Mr and Mrs J B. Venn Mr and Mrs George, Venn, Mrs. White. Mr and Mrs John, White. Mr C. White, the Misses. White, Mr H B. White. Mr. G F F. Whi'e, Mr and Mrs E A, White, the Misses, Wrisrht, Aid, and Mrs, Watkirs, Dr and Mrs, Watkins, the Misses, Ws tkins (Bewsey), Watkins, Miss, Whittle, Mr and Mrs 3 Lyon. Whittle Mr and Mrs N (Stn •k- -ton Heath), Whittle, Mr J W, Whittle, Miss (Stafford Rd), Wilson. Mr end Mrs Stanton, Wilson. Mrs' Wilson, Miss Woodcock, Mrnnd Mrs W H. Westwell, Miss.Westhrook, and Mrs IT J, Woods, Connelllor and Mrs, Woods, Mrs Alfred, Woods, Mr and Mrs WS, Woods. Mr and Mrs Herbert, Woods, Mr anoi Mrs E W, Woods, Mr C 11, Woods. Mr and Mrs J., Woods, Miss A M. Willis, Rev F W and Mrs. Willeocks Rey E and Mrs, Willcoeks, Miss, Watkins, Bey Owen, Warnock, Rev James, Wild, Rev H Walker, Miss, Warbur- ton, the Misses, Waring, Mr and Mrs Wm, Walton, Mr and Mrs John, Williamson, Mrs, Williams, Mrs J P Wenham, Miss, Willett, Mr A. J, Willett, Miss Edith, Ward, Mr 2nd Mrs, Ward. Miss. YOnge, Rev 3 and Mrs, Young, Mr and Mrs J R. Young.Mr and Mrs J C H.</p>
        <p>The Irish judges who opened the various commissions on Tuesday and on Monday made pointed reference to the gratifying condition of. the country as shown by the small number of cases sent tor trial. James Canham Read, dock-house clerk, was hanged at Chelmsford, on Tuesday, for the murder of Florence Dennis at Southend on the 24th June. The prisrmer died without admitting his guilt, and in a letter which he ' wrote to hi, brother on Sunday he protested his inno- cence ; but this letter di,:poses of the statement he made from the dock after conviction—that he was forty miles from Southend at the time of the murder,—for he therein informed his brother that four persons living in South Church-road,Southend.could answer for his being in bed on the night of June 24.</p>
