        <p>BOARD OF GUARDIANS ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>There has been considerable acqvity in political circles this week in connection with the forthcom- ing elections to the Board of Guardians. The Liberal general council met on Monday evening to decide upon their course of action, and as a result a letter was directed to be sent by Councillor Roberts to the Conservative Association to the effect that, as there seemed to be a growing desire in the town that women should be specially repre- sented upon the Board of Guardians, and that there were matters of great importance in connection with the administration of poor law in regard to which women had special knowledge, the Conserva- tive and Liberal Associations should each nominate a lady guardian, and that in any event the two seats in question should be offered to such ladies as the respective associations might choose, with- out the risk or expense of a contest.. The meeting also suggested the desirability of a compromise. The Committee meeting of the Conservative Association was held during the sent week, and it was resolved " that no action be t.ken by the Conservative Association with rega,d to Parish Council and guardians' elections unless the Liberal party showed signs of .making the elections a party question, in which case it will become incumbert upon the Conservatives to fight every ward." A discussion also arose with regard to the suggested lady guardians, and the general feeling was not in favour of the proposal. A proposed compromise whereby the Conservatives receive six, and the Liberals four seats was favourably commented upon. It will be remembered that some time ago, two candidates were chosen by the Trades Council. viz. Mr. T. Malkin for the Bewsey ward, and Mr. Lang for the St. John's ward. It is understood that should the suggested compromise break down,these candidates will receive the support of the Libetal party. In the meantime, the Conservative members of the Board of Guardians are taking matters in their own hands, and they held a meeting on Thursday night, Mr. S. Davies occupying the chair, there being also present, Messrs. Shaw, Muir, Aukland, A. J. Jolley, A. Jolley. Pilling, Dobson etc. A letter was also read from Mr. R. Heaton, regretting his inability to be present. The following gentlemen were chosen as candidates at the forthcoming elec- tions: Messrs. A. J. Jolley, W. J. Shaw, J. Hill, G. Pilling, S. Davies, J. Muir, A. Jolley, and R. Heaton. Messrs. Longton, Gill, .Aukland, and Dobson are not seeking re-election. The candidates were not assigned to the various wards, although Mr. W. Y. Shaw announces his intention of fighting the Fair% field ward.</p>
        <p>In Penketh, Mr. J. Dignum is expected to be, elected wittout oppesiton to the District Council. Mr. Thompson, of the white lead works is ex- pected to be a candidate for the representation of Little Sankey. The date of nominators closes on Wednesday next, and the poll if any will take place on Monday, December 17.</p>
      </div>
