        <p>THAT now familiar word "Corn- The Board of Guardians. promise " has been heard in con- nection with the coming election of Guardians. So far as Warrington is con- cerned there has been little or no dis- position in either party to fight the contest on political lines. There is no doubt a good deal to be said at the present time in favour of a mutual agreement. We are on the eve of a new depar- ture in the administration of poor-law relief. Boards of Guardians have hitherto been elected on an old-fashioned property franchise, but in the future they will be controlled by a much more democratic class of voters. The new forces have not yet, however, had time to finally range themselves, and there was no reason, apart from personal ambitions, why on this occasion an arrangement should not be come to. The proposal of the Liberals to allow the Conservatives to retain six of the ten seats was an eminently fair and even goner' ous one. There was much to be said,too,in favour of the suggestion that each party should nominate one lady member,but there was nothing surprising in the fact that the Conservatives did not look upon it with approval. The Tory party finds women useful as organised canvassers, but it is not prepared to bestow upon them any of the hon- ours of public office.</p>
        <p>THE Watch Committee did not The Watch Committee, come well out of the discussion in the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening. In fact, the latest statements with regard to the fashion in which the Commit- tee interprets its duties are in some respects so serious, that we cannot think for a moment that the subject will be allowed to drop without fur- ther explanations. It is now perfectly clear that the Committee does intervene between the police and the magistrates in all offences against the licensing laws. This was admitted even by Alderman HARRISON, whose over-drawn indigna- tion at the critics of the Committee was supremely ridiculous in view of the fact that he practically admitted the truth of the charges which these said critics have made. As far as the relations between the Committee and the police are concerned, the only difference between Coun- cillor ROBERTS—who was the special object of , Alderman HARRISON'S attack—and Alderman HARRISON himself was that the statement of the Councillor was as clear and precise as could be desired, while that of the Alderman was vague and inconclusive. According to Alderman HARRI- SON, the interference of the Commitee with the 'police which interference, it is agai n worth noting, he fully admitted—originated in a rather peculiar way. It appears that ' formerly when the Chief Constable wished for advice as to the legal bearings of a case which he thought of bringing before the magistrates he went to the Magistrates' Clerk. This, clearly, was an eminently proper and judicious course to take. But some time ago Mr. WHITE, the Magis- trates' Clerk, was unfortunately laid up with a temporary illness, and at that time the Chief Con- stable had "a difficult case to deal with." Natur- ally one would have thought that, under these circumstances, the Chief Constable would have gone to the Town Clerk or some other lawyer for enlightenment and guidance„ but, "No," says Alderman HARRISON, he came to the Watch Com- mittee—a Committee which is composed of lay- men who know as much or as little about legal forms and technicalities as any other body of lay- men. This, explains Alderman HARRISON, was the innocent origin of the " interference " of the Watch Committee with the police and their cases against the publicans. It will be seen that this explanation still leaves one rather material point to be explained. If the Committee kindly under- took to give legal advice to the Chief Con- stable when the Magistrates' Clerk was ill, why did it persist in giving its decisions upon publicans' cases when the Magistrates' Clerk was again well? But, as a nsatter of fact, Alderman HARRISON'S story is altogether unsatis- fying, and perhaps the best that can be said of it is that it is a plausible defence of an indefensible, procedure. Oa the other hand, Councillor ROBERTS' statement of the origin of the Com- mittees' interference is concise and positive. Ac- cording to Mr. R,oBEwrs,the Chief Constable " has told him privately and has complained to the magistrates sitting in Committee that certain in structions were given to him twelve months ago last October that he should from that date always bring cases of alleged offences by publicans before the Watch Committee, and act upon their instruc- tions. Previous to that he had received no instructions of that character, and always took action after advising with the Magistrates' Clerk." This, we take it, disposes once for all of the plea that the intervention of the Watch Committee</p>
        <p>came about originally more ny accident than by design, and that there is no deliberate purpose in it. Another important test is the action of the Committee with regard to other cases than tkose against publicans. If we rightly understand the remarks of Alderman Francomb, the. Committee does not interfere in other cases. That being so, the question at once arises, why do they not interfere? If the pur- pose of the committee be simply to give guidance to the CHIEF CONSTABLE and to take responsibility off his shoulders, why should they not have all other cases,except those of very minor importance, brought before them? There are prosecutions undertaken by the police which involve greater responsibility than those for oflences against the licensing laws, and which result in much more serious consequences to the accused. If the CHIEF CONSTABLE ever needs support it will be, we should think, in connaction with such cases as these, yet in these cases he does not, it seems, receive it. The committee gives the whole of its attention to the charges against publicans. Why is this? What is the inference to be drawn from it? The liquor interest is a very powerful one id Warrington, but we can hardly think that it has been taken under the special protection of the Watch Committee. Yet, taking all the circum- stances into account, and all that has been said the Committee is clearly under an obligation to free itself from even the possibility of such a charge. Mere outbursts of simulated indignation in the Council Chamber will not do. Deeds speak louder than words. If the committee has no special regard for the owners and occupiers of licensed houses, let it deal with licensing offences exactly as it deals with all other offences against the law. Nothing short of this will be sufficient to clear the municipal government of the town from a very serious stigma.</p>
      </div>
