      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY. DECEMBER 8. 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY. DECEMBER 8. 1894.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S BIBLE CLASS, RUNCORN. Under the presidency of Mr. B. liniveton, a well- attended meeting of this bible class was held on Sunday afternoon. Mr. B. Crosby delivered the address for the afternoon and spoke upon "The influence of will upon character." An organ solo ivas most efficiently performed by Mr. W. Cooke, and Mr. Crosby exceelingly well sang a solo entitled " The Sands or Life." COLLIER'S FRESH IRISH WIRY BUTTER, 10d. per lb. excellent.—Advt. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>QUESTION OF A GRAVING DOCK AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF A GRAVING DOCK AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>HOW THE MATTER STANDS.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Runcorn Commissioners on Thursday, Mr. T. Lea asked that the communication from the Manchester Ship Canal Company with regard to the graving dock question be read.—Mr. Taylor approved of the suggestion, and Mr. Handley believed that when the committee's alternative proposals were made known, everyone in the town would be satisfied with them.—Mr. Lightburn said they were unable to go before the electors and say whether they would accept the proposals of the Shin Canal Company or not. He did not think any member of the Board was prepared to say that he would accept the proposal of the Ship Canal Corapany.—Mr. Bisbrown thought the proposals should be read.—Mr. Imison was of the opinion that the rate- payers ought to have an account of what the Canal Company had proposed. There was no doubt that it was a question in which the success of the town was bound up. It was the only question which seemed to have created any amount cf interest. The electors were looking t 3 them for some intelligent expression of their , views on the subject. I The Law Clerk then read the following letter from Mr. , Collier, of the Manchester Ship Canal Company :- 1 " Manchester Ship Canal Offices, ath October, 1894. I Dear Sir,—l desire to refer to the meetings which I have I recently had with several of your Commissioners, and to commit to writing the last proposal which I bad the I pleasure to submit to them. The matter is understood to be for the present without prejudice, and any provi- sional agreement which may be come to is subject to ratification by my directors and by your Commissioners. The Company's proposals are (1) The Runcorn Improve- mens Commissioners to waive and forego all claim to the provision by the Company of the graving dock named in clause 2, sub-section A of the agreement of Ist September, 1893. (2) All other provision in the said agreement to stand and have effect as though this varia- tion had not been made. (3) The Company to amend and put into working order the ship-repairing slip late in the occupation of Messrs. Brundrit and Hayes, at the Old Quay,Runeorn. The said slip-way to be made with Y ways (hitherto it has only had 5) and so that it can be used for vessels of 150 ft. in length. Steam power tl be provided for working the carriages on the said ways. All other proper and necessary machinery, fittings and appliances for drawing vessels up the said slip-way to be provided in connection therewith. (4) The small graving dock near the south end of the bonded ware- house to be maintained in its present condition, and for</p>
        <p>_ its present purposes as a graving dock. (5) The public to be allowed to regulate for the use of the said slip-way and graving dock named in paragraphs 3 and 4 hereof in turn with the Company or others in the order they may enter in a register to he provided for the purpose. The rates to be charged to the public for such use shall in each case be of each vessel 203. for the first- day and 10s. per day thereafter. (6) The Company to erect at their own expense over the Bridgewater canal, at Halton-road wharf, a footbridge. The fabric of the bridge to be provided by, and at the expense of the Commissioners but the abutments and all other works to be provided by the Company. (7.) The Coln- pans, to make at their own expense deep water berths with 24 feet of water alongside, and of a total lineal ex- tent of 400 feet, in a position (to be determined by the Company) between Runcorn Bridge and Weston Point. I am to desire that the Commissioners will give the pro- posals their best consideration, and in so doing I would wish to repeat that I believe that what is now proposed would he of much greater benefit to the town and trade of Runcorn than the provision of a graving dock as was originally contemplated." The document is signed by Mr. W. H. Collier, and addressed to Mr. E. Marshall, secretor; of the Commissioners. Mr. Wiswall said the letter should read "six ways instead of four" not " seven ways instead of five."—Mr. Handley suggested that the counter proposals drawn up by the Law Clerk for the General Purposes Committee oheuld also be read.—Mr.lmison said it should he known that no answer, had been sent by the Board to the Ship Canal Company in reply to the proposal. He was in favour of the Committee's resolutions on the matter being read.—Mr. Lightburn said he took it that the 'object of the proposition was that the public should know how they stood in the matter, but he did not see how it was to beef any use to tern, as the proposals had not been submitted to the Canal Company. There were some members of the Committee who objected to the alternative proposals and were in favour of a grav- ing dock pure and simple.—The Chairman said there was a fair majority of the Committee in favour of the alter- native proposals, and he thought they should come before the public.—ln reply to Mr. Hankinson, the Law Clerk said the provision of a graving dock by the Shin Canal Company was quite a recent act.—Mr. Pritchard said the proposals of the Committee would have been dealt with by the Board, but considering that they were disselving, it was thought desirable to postpone the matter until the new council was elected. The ratepayers should understand that it might be found necessary to make considerable modifi- cations in the committee's proposals —Mr. Bishrown said the committee considered the matter carefully, and there was no doubt but what the recommendations of the committee would be sent to the company with per- fect unanimity.</p>
        <p>The original agreement with the Ship Canal Company and the Board respecting the provision of a graving dock was then read by the Law Clerk and also the re- commendations of the General Purposes Commit- tee, which was prepared to advise the Com- missioners to adopt the following recommendations. (1) Under no circumstances should the right to a gray- ing dock he absolutely waived or cancelled. But the time for the making should be extended for five years. (2) The committee were willing to extend the time for the construction of a wharf mentioned in the agreement of 1890. (3) The slip to he made so as to accommodate three averaged- sized vessels abreast. (4) Proposal of Canal Company agreed to. (5) As the rates in other places for small craft are much lower than those asked, the following table was mugaested : Vessels of 150 tons register and upwards, 205. first day, 10 per day there- e'er. Teasels of 100 tons and under 150 tons, 15s. first day, and 7. per day thereafter. Vessels of 50 tons and under 100 tons, and unregistered vessels, 103. first day and 5;. per day thereafter. These rates to include steam Dower, etc. (6) With regard to the bridge over the Bridgewater Canal at Halton-road the Com- mi,tee pointed out that the fabric had already been purchased by private individuals. (7) The 24. ft depth to be at low water. The layby to be on the Run- corn side of the pumping engine and below the Runcorn bridge and to be not less than 600lt. in length. A tip for the loading of coal and salt to be provided soins- where between the railway bridge and DAamere dock. (8). All the new works now agreed to be done to be cornpletei by June 24th, 1895.</p>
        <p>llud=on's is the Soap to buy, As everybody knows— It quickly drives the dirt awa.,, But won't wear out the c'uth33 h—Ado .</p>
