      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>POLICE INSPECTION.</head>
        <p>POLICE INSPECTION.</p>
        <p>The members of the constabulary in Runcorn division were on Tuesday inspected by Colonel Hamersley (Chief Constable of Cheshire). The police force included Superintendent Atherton, Inspector Hicks, six sergeants, and 23 constables. The books, clothing, &amp;c., were examined and found to be satis- factory.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF A TANNERY FOREMAN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Mr. Joseph Ridgway. of Canal-street, N5,,h0 for 21 years had acted as foreman at nightield Tannery, and of which Mr. Posnett is manager, were interred in Runcorn Cemetery, on Sunday afternoon. The deceased was greatly esteemed, and over 60 of the workmen at the tannery walked in procession at the funeral, as a mark of respect. Mr. Posnett headed the proces- sion. The funeral service was to have been con- ducted by the Rev. J. Simpson, but as the rev. gentleman was indisposed, Mr. Slade performed the duty. COLLIER'S sell everything for the smallest possible "profits. Advt.</p>
        <p>MILL-BROW WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN. _ _</p>
        <p>The Band of Hope choir connected with this Sun- day school gave a service of song entitled, "The Man who Spoiled the Music" (Rev. Mark Guy Pearce), in the schoolroom, on Sunday. There was a good attendance, and the proceedings were pre- sided over by Mr. J. R. Frith. The choir exceed- ingly well sang the various pieces, and were under the conductorship of Mr. James Moorfiebil.. The connective readings were read by Mr. S. Pope. Mr. George Johnson was the organist. The collections taken were on behalf of the Band of Hope funds. COLLIER'S sell the best quality goods at lowest possible prices. A dvt.</p>
        <p>AN INCORRIGIBLE WORKHOUSE BOY IT RUNCORN</p>
        <p>POL/CS COURT.</p>
        <p>William Henry Harley, 13 years of age, was charged at Runcorn Police Court on Friday, before Messrs, P. Speakman and F. J. Norman, with steal- ing a cap, the previous day. It appears that the prisoner and another boy absconded from the work- house et Dutton the pftvious moraiag, without their caps. On meeting two boys they appropri- ated their caps, and proceeded in the direction of Runzorn. P.C. Griffiths followed the two run- aways, and succeeded in capturing, and taking them back to the workhouse. Mr. Cunnah (work- house maste") informed the Bench that the prisoner was unmanageable. He was ordered to be sent to Brad well Reformatory.</p>
        <p>THE LATE CAPTAIN SHAW.—THR FUNERAL. The remains of the late Captain Shaw were laid to rest in Runcorn Cemetery on Sunday afternoon. The body had been conveyed from Liverpool on a hopper,and thence to his late residence in Lowlands- road. A large number of friends walked in pro- cession to the Cemetery. Amongst them were captains Brown, Jones, Owen, H. Hill, Joynson, Worrall, J. Millinger, T. Millinger, Bettley, Davies, Peacooke, Bone, McPhail, Pye, Parr, and Water- worth, Messrs. Shaw (Seamen's Institute) Philip Speakman, J.P.,C.C., R. Abel, J. Horsefield, Parson, J. W. Timperley, Gittens, Darlington, Coward, White, Jones, Davies, Peacocke, jun., Roberts, Done, Green, F. Lewis, J. Millington, Carter, Cooper, Foster, and Hindley. The funeral service was con- ducted by the Rev. T. Bramall, Primitive Metho- dist minister.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>MEETING_OF THE SONS OF TE3IPERANCE.</head>
        <p>MEETING_OF THE SONS OF TE3IPERANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. E. Sutton presided at the fortnightly meeting of this order on Saturday evening. An adjourned discussion took place on a paper, read at a previous meeting, "On the evils of drink." The crux of the discussion was on the moderation. It was statel by the speakers that the only absolutely sate course was total abstinence. That of all the myriads who had gone down to drunkards' graves, not one purposely sought his fate: but found it through the trap-door of moderate drinking. The following officers and members took part in the debate : Messrs. Thomas J. Holt, T. Hatton, David Smith, Samuel M. Temperley, Edward Key, John Jones, John Williamson, Thomas Cooke, and Daniel Bisbrown. Mr. Holt replied to the discussion, and the meeting closed. There was a large attendance of members. COLLIER'S is. BUTTER is the gem in the market. —Advt. FORTHCOMING SCHOLARS' CONCERT. The scholars attending Greenway-road Board Schools. Runcorn,are to give a miacellaneous enter- tainment in the Public Hall on Thursday and Fri- day evenings next. The entertainment will include the performance of an operetta entitled" Mr. No- body." Mrs. Tomkinson, of Willington Hall, Tar- porley, will tin Thursday evening, distribute the prizes awarded to the scholars, and will also deliver a short address. Dr. McDougall will be the chair- man. On Friday evening in addition to the chil- dren's performance, Mr. Wilson Blackledge, twill appear in his laughable character sketches. - ACCIDENT AT BOTTOM LOCKS, RUNCORN. _ _</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon, Thomas Sheldon, 25 years of age, residing in Duke's-fields, while en- gaged at work on the gangtree over the canal at Bottom Locks,met with a serious accident. A lurry left the rails and this caused him to be thrown over the side, alighting on his back on the flags, about 20ft. below. He was conveyed home,and has been attended to by Dr. Mangan, who found him suffer- ing from injuries to the elbows and lower portion of the spine. He is progressing favourably.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>DROWNING FATALITY AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>DROWNING FATALITY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Worsley, coroner, held an inquest at the Burial Board offices on Monday morning, touching the death of James Russell, of Leven-street, Liver- pool, who was drowned at Runcorn on Friday evening. The first witness called was Margaret Russell, wife of the deceased, who identified the body as that of her husband, who she said, was 49 years of age. He was chief engineer on the steamer "Herbert,"—William Shaw, missionary of the Seamen's Institute, stated that he was at the docks at the time of the fatality. He heard a splash in the water and cries for help, but on going near to the spot he was unable to hear or see anything. Several persons came upon the scene with grappl- ing irons and witness assisted in the work until the body was recovered at 8.5 p.m. on Friday. The body was found within a yard or two of the lock gates, over which the deceased should have passed in going to the town. He had taken the wrong turn and fallen into the water. Deceased had been on the ship a fortnight and witness believed deceased had been in Runcorn on a previous occasion. On the same night,within five or six minutes of the body being pulled out, one of the lock tenter.' who knew every inch of the ground, fell into the lock, but was rescued. The place was well lighted, and at the spot there were chains, but witness could not say whether they were in position at the time. It was ppesible that the chain might have been unhooked. The accident was without doubt due to the density of the fog.—lnspector Hicks stated that he saw the mate of the vessel on the night in question and his story corroborated the statement made by Mr. Shaw. They had endeavoured to get the mate but the " Herbert " had gone from Runcorn to Liver- pool and thence to Preston. The steamer was engaged in the coasting trade. A verdict of "Accidentally drowned," was returned. Those who had assiqed to recover the body and convey it to the mortuary on the Mersey Mission Institute Ambulance were thanked for their services. The Coroner and jury expressed their sympathy with the bereaved relatives. RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Board was held in the Board-room on Tuesday evening. Mr. J. Prit- chard presided, and there were also present, Rev. Dr. Gastaldi ; Mr. E. Imison, and Mr. G. Moss (clerk).—The minutes of the School Attendance Committee showed a decrease of 198 scholars, and 57 in the average attendance as compared with the corresponding month last year. The percentage of attendance was 84 as against 81 last November, showing an improvement in the regularity of at- tendance.—A very fine magic lantern of the newest type with cylinders and other appliances was on view in the Board-room. The Chairman explained that it. was a present to the Board by Mr. Brunner, M.P. The lantern which had been in use at the schools for several years was unsuitable and out of repair, and the teachers thought it advis- able to try and obtain a new one. On applying to Mr. Brunner for a subscription towards the cost, he generously offered to present a new lantern to the Board. He had supervised the selection, and had sent a lantern far superior to the old one. Ile had also sent new cylinders for the gases, and a screen to be hung up in the school, the total cost being about £4O. The Chairman moved a cordial vote of thanks to Mr. Brunner for his gift. Mr. Imison seconded, and the vote was carried unani- mou,ly.—Applications for several assistant teachers for advance of salary were referred to the School Managealm: c...oncluded the busi- ness.</p>
        <p>~ , ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHAPEL, RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHAPEL, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In cennection with the Greenway:roa—d-IVilitive Methodist Chapel, annivrrsary services were held on Sunday, when sermons were preached, morning and evening by the Rev. T. Brantall. Suitable hymns and anthems were sung. There were large congregations at both services, more especially at the evening service. The collections were on be- half of the trust funds and were in advance of last year.</p>
        <p>- COLLIER'S BLACK BLENDED TEA, 20. per quarter lb, Ada</p>
        <p>FORTHCOMING SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from an advertisement in another column, there will be a sale of work in connection with Camden Wesleyan Chapel, Runcorn, on Wed- nesday, Thursday, and Friday, the 12th, 13th, and 14th inst. Mr. John Simpson will open the sale on the opening day, and Messrs. P. Speakmaa, j.p., C.C., and C. R. H. Harrison will respectively per- form the opening ceremony on the two following days. Numerous attractions will be provided. COLLIER'S BREAD is the best money can buy.--elciet. THE RUNCORN SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>The choir of this institute gave a very interest- ing entertainment at Weston on Monday evening. The proceeds were in aid of the cleaning and fur- nishing of the reading room at Weston. Mr. Ford was the chairman and there was a very large attendance. The various items were most credit- ably given by the choir. On Tuesday evening the usual weekly entertainment was provided in the Institute, when Mr. Shaw presided. There was a large attendance. BETHESDA LITERARY AND SOCIAL CLASS, , RUNCORN. "Gambling" was the subject of a paper read be- fore the members of the above-named class onTues- day evening, by Mr. George Holt, under the presi- dency of the Rev. J. Simpson. The essayist in the course of an excellently prepared paper, referred to the increasing prev.alence of the gambling evil, and of how the usages of the times pandered to it. As instances he spoke of the influence the telegraph and Press exerted in this direction. He also re- ferred to the efforts made by the Anti-Gambling League,and urged his bearers to use their influence in order to induce the Government to discontinue the transmitting of betting information by tele- graph. Considerable discussion was aroused, in which the Chairman, Mr. E. Worrell, and Mr. J. Jones took part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>N.S:P.C.C.-M ERTING AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>N.S:P.C.C.-M ERTING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A meeting was held in the Seamen's Institute on Tuesday afternoon, for the purpose of considering the question of the formation of a local branch of the National Society for the Prevention ol Cruelty to Children. Lady Broke presided, and there were also present the Revs. Canon Maitland Wood, Dr. Gastaldi, T. Brackenbury, W. George, G. D. Wray, and T. W. Vale, Mrs. Alexander, of Alvanley, and Mrs. F. Norman. It was resolved on the proposi- tion of Lady Brooke to adopt the constitution of the N.S.P.C.C., and undertake to work in conformity with its spirit and rules, and it was further resolved to make application to the Central Com- mittee f•r incorporation as a district committee of the Warrington and district branch of the society. On the motion of the Rev. G. D. Wray, seconded by Dr. Gastaldi,it was resolved that the area sh3uld be so much of the Runcorn Union as was not already affiliated with the Warrington, Northwich and Chester branches. A committee was appointed with power to add to their number, Lady Brooke being appointed secretary. On the motion of the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood it was decided that Mr. G. W. Allen be the district correspondent. HALTON-ROAD WESLEYAN CHAPI4L--FOREIGN MDRIONARY ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>Anniversary services on behalf of Wesleyan Foreign Missions in connection with Halton-road Wesleyan Chapel were held on Sunday when ser- mons were preached. At the morning service, the Rev. W. Lile Stone was the preacher, and in the evening the Rev. W. George preached. There were good congregations at both services.—A public mis- sionary meeting was held on Tuesday evening when the chair was occupied by Mr. James B. Frith, who was supported on the platform by the Rev. E. Smith, of Manchester, Rev. 'l. Brackenbury, and Rev. W. George. Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P., F. Wiswall, S. Pope, J. M. Frith, and E. Taylor (circuit secretary). The ley. W. George submittei the general report of the society and Mr. Pope gave a statement of what had been done in the Sunday schools. This latter statement showed that the Juvenile Christmas offerings amounted to .£5, and £3l6s. 6d. had been collected in the Sunday schools which was an advance on the amount realised twelve months ago.—Several addresses on mission- ary work were delivered. A collection at the meet- ing, and at the services on Sunday realised .£ll. Votes of thanks to the Chairman and speakers were passed on the motion of Mr. Taylor, seconded by Mr. Wiswall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>HALF-HOUR TALKS IN RUNCORN LIBRARY.</head>
        <p>HALF-HOUR TALKS IN RUNCORN LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, M.A., delivered the first of a series of half-hour lectures to the public on books and authors, to be given by local gentlemen, in Runcorn Free Public Library on Friday evening. The lecturer was introduced by Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., who said that the Library Cora- naittee were prepared to encourage the readers in every way, and if still further greater facilities were demanded the Committee would be equal to the occasion.—The Rev. Canon Maitland Wood then delivered an admirable half-hour's address on the subject, "A few hints to readers." In the course of his address the rev, gentleman advised his audience to avoid reading in a groove. If they got out of the groove they would be astonished to find how much interesting and valuable information they would meet with in very unlikely books. There should be no " Hobson's choice" about books. They were not, however, to let their taste altogether dic- tate their reading, but let their reading govern their taste. They should not label books as dry because they did not happen to like them. The lecturer strongly advised students to become familiar with famous books. Such books, both old and new, held their place in general esteem by the consent of scholars in all ages. In order to make it easy to understand the classics, of the ancients and foreign and English classics several series of small books had been prepared and published to expound and illustrate tikem. Such little books broke up the great works, and what seemA difficult became comparatively easy. A vote of thanks was passed to the lecturer on the motion of Mr. J. G. Hutton.</p>
        <p>1111•11 C"... aair,..111W.4</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NURSING CLASSES.</p>
        <p>Miss Ward, of Crewe, conducted an examination in Camden School, Runcorn, on Friday afternoon, of eighteen candidates who have been attending the nursing classes in the town. The result of the examination, it is expected, will be known in about six weeks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN BOATING CLUB.</head>
        <p>THE RUNCORN BOATING CLUB.</p>
        <p>This club held its final meeting of the season on Wednesday night in the Cocoa-rooms, lligh-st. Mr. W. Milliner (secretary) read his report in which he stated that the club had been a success. He hoped, however, that their financial position would be improved in the following year. Votes of thanks were accorded the Committee, and the secretary for their services, on the motion of Mr. J. A. Smith, seconded by Mr. D. Coventry and supported by Mr. F. Aber.</p>
        <p>THE WORK OF THE DISTRICT NURSE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>From October 25th to November 25th there have been 37 cases on the books—seven of them being chronic, 17 medical, six surgical, and seven typhoid. Of these three have died, and four are convalescent. Thirty-two patients have received occasional visits. The number of visits paid to cases totals 287, and the number of occasional visits 50.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S BAND OF HOPE, RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S BAND OF HOPE, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A well-attended meeting of this Band of Hope was held on Wednesday evening, wLen the chair was occupied by Mr. D. Bisbrown. A number of recitations were given by W. Kirkham, E. beach, B. Jackson, K Read, J. McLeod, Nellie Streets, L. Hopley, M. Gatley and J. Harrop. Dialogues and glees wera also given. Songs were contributed by Miss S. P. Hayes, Mrs. Temperley and Mr. Thomas. Mr. J. Kirkham delivered a short address. HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Orford and Farnworth Harriers held an inter-club race at Farnworth on Saturday. Although Orford were without Grounds, Bradley, and Mather, they managed to win easily by twenty points. G. Wright of the home team was first man in. The Orford team have only lost one inter-club race since they were formed three years ago, that being with Sefton (the Liverpool and District champions), who are coming to War- rington on Saturday next, December 15th, when a tight race may be expected. The following is the order of Saturday last :</p>
        <p>ORFORD,</p>
        <p>FiRywDRTH. J Price J Hinchliffe F Woods 4 J Bouch 7 J McCue J B trnett ' 8 Ashton .1' Harrison..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>PARISH COUNCILS. (CONTINUED FROM PAGE 6). FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>PARISH COUNCILS. (CONTINUED FROM PAGE 6). FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>The first Parish Meeting was held in the Girls' School- room, Fredsham, on Tuesday evening, Mr. Charles E. Linaker presiding. There was a large company present. A large batch of nolninati3n papers numbering 61, was presented. The candidates were questioned. The fol- lowing are the candidates, with the number of votes each received at the show of hands :—Messrs. Edmund Ashworth, 62 votes; William Andrews, 61; W. Booth, 88; W. E. Burton, a ; John Barrow, 59; P. Booth, 72; Jossph Corker, 90; T. Davies, 14; G. Davies, 2; H. M. Davies, 37; C. A. Dawson, 20; James Edwards, 27; John Allis, 38; T. Earlam, 108; John Forster. 30; John Garner, 3; Henry E. Heywood, 107; James Illidge, 16 ; J. G. Kydd, 30; H. Lewis, 35; H. G. Linaker, 50; A. Linaker, 28; H. McWaters, 26 ; P. Riley, 24 ; T. A. Rigby, 84; R. Renton, 24 ; E. Rhodes, 17 : Geo. Rodgers, 83; W. D. RingroFe, 45; W. E. Shore, 85; G• Sharrock, 36; S. Sharp, 18' Thomas J. Selby, 11; A. Thomas, 50; H. Tiley, 50 ; John Tomalin, 23 ; J. M. Williams, 103; J. J. Wilkinson, 43; and W. Youd, 73. Messrs. James B. Holland and J. W. Rothwell withdrew from the centest. After the Chairman had read out the names of the candidates who had had the highest num- ber of votes, Mr. Wm. Jones demanded a poll en behalf of Mr. John Ellis, and the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>FRODSCIAM LORDSHIP.</head>
        <p>FRODSCIAM LORDSHIP.</p>
        <p>The meeting of this parish was held in the Beys' School, Frodsham, on Tuesday evening, when Mr. John Pollard presided. There were a large number of electors present. Twenty-seven nomination papers were handed in, and the following votes were taken :—Messrs. R. Bate 41, Joseph Barrington, sen., 30, Rev. H. B. Blogg, 10. Messrs. A. Bowden 18, T. B. Carter 44, T. Davies 26, J. H. Davies 43, J. Dobson 40, T. Ellison 24, T. Fletcher 31, H. Frankish 13, N. Greening 11, T. Hulse, 58, J. H. Hargreaves 24, T. Lewis 60. George Okell 49, James Percival 47, Rev. R. Renton 25. Messrs. C. Rey- nolds 42, T. A. Rigby 23, R. Shepherd 44,George Turner 57, H. Nay 40, A. Thomas, J.P., 24, S. Vernon 27, J. M. Williams 23. and W. Walker 29. The Chairman an- announced the result after which Mr. J. H. Hargreaves demanded a poll.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EARLESTO W-N AND N EWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTO W-N AND N EWTON.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Newton, on Monday morning, be- fore Mr. 1. J. Gillespie, Michael Condannon, labourer, was charged with bbing drunk and disorderly in Earle- strPet, E ' arlestown on Saturday night, and was fined 53. and cos's.-0a en Roberts, collier, Grafton-st., was also charged with a similar offence on Sunday morning, and was fined 2. 6d. and costs. MISSIONARY MEETING AT BIIRTONWOOD.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting in connection with the Wesleyan Home and Foreign Missions was held in the Wesleyan Chapel, Iturtonwood, on Tuesday evening. Mr. J. Gooding of Earlestown, presided, but there was only a poor attendance. Suitable addresses were given by the Chairman, the Rev. J. Bartley, of Earlestown. and Rev. J. T. Waddy, of St. Helens, and at the close a collection was taken on behalf of the missionary funds.</p>
        <p>DROWNED IN THN SANKEY- CANAL.</p>
        <p>An inquest on the body of the man,identified as Chas. Ganger, tripe dresser, of Ulverston, which was found floating in the Sankey Canal, at Earlestown, under cir- cumstances reported in our last issue, was held at the Ship Inn,on Friday. by Mr. F. A.Jones, deputy coroner. Evidence of identification was given, and no further particulars as to the man's movements after he left his lodgings being forthcoming, a verdict of "Found drowned" was returned. GOOD TEMPLA.R ENTERTAINMENT. An entertainment in aid of the Hope of Earlestown lodge of Good Templars was given in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, on Saturday evening. Bro. W. Porter. P.D.C.T., of Liverpool. presided, and by him and Bro. E. Boreland, EI.D., of Liverpool, interesting addressess were given on Temperance matters. An excellent pro- gramme of songs, recitations, and instrumental selections was given before a fairly large audience by the follow- ing, :—Messrs. J. Nichols, W. Wilson, T. Clare, jun., J. Huddleston, J. Miller, and G. Clarke; Misses Roberts, Higgins and Sudworth. The various items were accom- panied by Miss Sod worth and Mr. W. Willis. WESLEYAN HOME AND FOREIGN MISSION.</p>
        <p>The annual missionary services in connection with the above were held on Sundoy in the Wesleyan Chapel, Earlestown. The pulpit was occupied morning and evening by the Rev. H. S. Stndford of St. Helens, who delivered sermons appropriate to the occasion. At the evening service the choir sang the anthem, " Seek ye the Lord," Mr. F. P. Clare taking the solo. Mr. J. K. Barr also sang the solo," Great in the Lord." In the afternoon an address on missionary work was delivered to the scholars in the upper schoolroom by the Rev. Mr. Sand- ford. Collections were made in behalf of the mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>STONING TEE BIIRTONWOOD VICARAGE.</head>
        <p>STONING TEE BIIRTONWOOD VICARAGE.</p>
        <p>What was at first aupposed to be an outrage on the new Vicarage at Burtonwood, the residence of the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, was reported to the police, the first report being to the effect that several windows in the place had been broken by stones. On looking into the matter the police elicited the fact that a man returning home at midnight in an intoxicated condition, was seized with a atidden inspiration, to assault the building. He accordingly commenced an attack, and succeeded in hurling a stone through one of the bedroom windows, causing much alarm amongst the resident. The man on being interrogated by the police could give no explanation of his conduct but stoutly assorted that he had no vindictive intention. It is understood that further proceedings will be taken. GOOD TEMPLARY (EBENEZER LODGE). _</p>
        <p>The Earleatown Ebenezer Lodge of Good Tempters held its usual session on Tuesday evening in the Co- operative Hall. Bro. A. Morris, CT.. presided. Good attendance of members. One candidate was propGsed for membership. Bro. J. Taylor read digest of No. 1 Convention to be held at fyldesley, December 15th. Committees were appointed to make arrangements for coming New Year's tea party. The programme for the evening was "Topical talks," the secretary being responsible. The subject was well taken up by the members, after which solos were given by Sister Day and Bro. J. Taylor. NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS CRICKET CLUB.</p>
        <p>At the annual concert given at the Town Hall,Newton. on Saturday evening, in aid of the funds of this club, there was a good attendance. Each portion of the pro- gramme was opened by a glee, the first being. "The Fairest Flower," and the second, "In this hour of softened splendour." Mr. George W. Kidd sang " The Island of Dreams," and "My Sweetheart when a Boy," in a manner which gained for him unstinted applause, and for which he was encored. Cowen's song, " The Promise of Life," was given with due effect by Mrs. White, and the same lady gave a aood interpretation of " the world went very well then," for which she was encored. Mr. W. Kay's fine bass voice was heard to advantage in the songs, " 'lhe Yeoman's Wedding," and " Elie Storm Fiend," and he well-merited the enthusias- tic recall which was demanded for each. Miss Bothwell was also heartily encored for her excellent rendition of " The Kerry Dance," and " The Bonnie Banks of Loch Lomond." Mr. W. H. Smith's rendering of the songs, " The light of the stars," and " Golden Lilies," received much favour frOm the audience. The songs, " Last Night," and "0 fair dove, 0 fond dove," submitted by Miss Madge Roberts, gained the approbation of the audience, as did the violin solos," Gavotte," and "Irish Melodies." by Miss Ada Aires'. Humorous songs were given duriLg tlie evening by Mr. S. Peace. 'Mr. P. Fairclough was the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>abbertisements not Cia66lfttit.</head>
        <p>abbertisements not Cia66lfttit.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN ELECTION. cIiNTRAL WARD.</p>
        <p>LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— After only twelve months' experience as a Commis- sioner. and suddenly having my indentures cancelled, and being turned out of office, accompanied by all the old hands. I now appeal to you to elect me on the District Council to finish my apprenticeship, There is no doubt that Run'orn at the present time needs stimula, ing. Trade is depres-ed. the condition of affairs could hardly be worse, yet in spite of this I have every faith in the future of our town, provided we get whst belongs to us, and our future rights firmly guarded.</p>
        <p>At the pre-ent time we have -a Perfect right to a Graving Dock 300 feet long, the Ferry across the river, a Bridge acroA toe Canal at Halton Road Crane, lower Water Rates, better lighted Streets, the prevention of Hawking by non- ratepayers.</p>
        <p>Of course in the present state of affairs,Baths and Recreation Grounds are out of the question, but as son as suitable I shall press for their provision, They are absolutely nc.se3- sary. as the Ruucorn people are cooped up in a town which is worse off than the Workhouse as regards means of cleanli- ness and recr ation.</p>
        <p>Manyrmatters of vital importance aro continually cropping up in the course of service whici require faithful and disinter- ested action, and if you elect me :o the Di,trict Council for the Central Ward I will serve your interests willingly and straightforwardly, and to the best of my ability.</p>
        <p>Yours truly, WALTER COLLIER.</p>
        <p>6, Church-street, Runcorn</p>
        <p>WANTED. General SERVANT, with good character, re- ! (limed. nurse kept, —App,y Mrs,REID, 46 Lansdowne- road, near Withington Station. Manchester,</p>
        <p>QPORTING-NAPS (good) sent overnight, 6 days for 12 kJ stamps.—BURLINGTON AGENCY, 21, Heddcfn-st., London, TIT</p>
        <p>WANTED Situation as GROWL Gardener: well up in both branches: good oriver with single or pair, good references : married : age 29 height 61t, ; weight llst.—Ad- dress C. BOUND, Brook-lane, Alderley Edge.</p>
        <p>HOUSEMAID Wanted where anothergirl is kept, must have good chlracter, ft.—Address Mrs. BALMALD 24, Padiharn-road. Burnley.</p>
        <p>wANTED, experienced GENERAL ; 3 in family; under- take all duties; assistan.:e n w tshing ; age ?.0 t) 25 ; state wages; good references required —M., 13, Mount Terrace, Manchester-road, Bury, Lancashire.</p>
        <p>SIT UATION WANTED as Housekeeper, to one or two gentlemen; good cook; mid Ile aged; can be highly recommend-d. Address M. W., 13, Matlock Place, Welbeck- street, Manchester.</p>
        <p>FOR IMMEDIATE DISPOSAL. The stork in trade and goodwill of an old established Grocer's!business having an off Spirit, Wine and Beer License attached thereto and now carried on at 116. Liverpool-road, Cadishead.—Apply to Mrs. RILEY on the premises.</p>
        <p>I\TH W Four Wheeled VAN. suit Biker or Confectioner 11 Yarmouth CART, two spring LITR UM to carry two end four Tons. Any description of Vehicle built to Order and Design.—WILLIAM H.RAToN.Wheelwright and Ladder Works, Clayton, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>The ordinary meeting of this Board was held in the Board-room on Thursday morning under the presidency of Mr. S Taylor, J.P. There was also present Mesrs. F- Wiswall, R. Abel, H. J. Grine, W. Handley, W. Collier, E. Imison, J. Pritchard, J. Dobson, .1. Meadowcroft, T. Lea, D. Bisbrown. W. Hankinson, J. W. Lightburn, Mr. • F. A. Lake (law clerk), Mr. E. Marshall (secretary), and Mr. M. Barker (surveyor). THE HEALTH OF THE TOWN. Dr. McDougall,in his report, c;°,, id the number of deaths registered during the past month amounted to 22, one of which was an inquest case. The 22 deaths showed an average death rate for the 12 months of 13-02, and ex- clusive of the inquest cases of 12-06 of the estimated Population. Thirty-six deaths were reeistered in the corresponding month of 1893.—The report was con- sidered very satisfactory.</p>
        <p>HIGHWAYS AND BUILDINGS COMMITTER. It was recommended by this committee that applica- tion be made to the Local Government Beard far their sanction to a loan of £620 for 60 years for sewerage works in Highland-road. Moughland-road and the Hollo- way, instead of for £5OO as asked for in acrnrdance with resolution passed on the 4th October, 1894. That the tender of Mr. William Moore for the private street works to be dene in Norfolk-street. Grosvenor-street, Sewell. street and Hankey-street be acceired. That the plan of Mr. John Wilson for the enlargement 'lf house in Oxford- road be approved. That the plan of Mr. Walter Collier for the erection of an open cart shed at the rear oc premises in Church-street be approved. That the plan of Mr. Henry White for the erection of a small scullery at the rear of cottage in White-street be approved.—Mr. Wiswall in moving that the minutes of the committee be approved, added a further minute that three sepa- rate tenders for the supply of 500 tone of unbroken Penmaenmawr stone be accepted. He stated that ow- ing to the number of unemployed in the town, it was advisable to find work for some of them. The commit- tee had previously bought the stone ready broken, but they now proposed to buy it in lumps and break it at Runcorn.—Mr. Bisbrown said they hoped to find work for a number of men by jobs of this kind. The minutes were passed.</p>
        <p>HEALTH COMMITTEE. This Committee recommended that the plan of Mr.Esrp for the abatement sof the nuisance at premises in Hal- ton-road be approved.—That the plan of the executors of the late C. Hazlehurst for the abatement of a nuisance in Stone-st. be approved subject to alterations.—That the plan of the executors of the late Wm. Rigby for the abatement of nuisances at Spring Gardens be approved. The Sanitary Inspector having reported that there is not sufficient water closet or privy accommodation and that a nuisance exists arising from the dilapidated condition of ashpit and privies at the schools connected with the Ellesmare-st. Chapel,in Sutherland-st.,it is recommended that a notice be served on the trustees of the Chapel under Section 38 of the Public Health Act, 1875, to make good the deficiency and abate the nuisance within 28 days of the service of the notice by removing the exist- ing ashpit and privies and by providing two water closets for females and one water closet and a urinal for males with a division wall between the two sets of closets and by constructing a new dry ashpit with con- creted bottom and covered over, and that in the event of non-compliance with such notice this Board do thi required work and charge the costs thereof to the said trustees. These minutes were passed on the motiml of Mr. Imison, seconded by Mr. T. Lea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NEW TRCIINICLL INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>THE NEW TRCIINICLL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>The Technical Instruction Committee reconsmended that a sum of 225 be paid to Mr. James Wilding for hie services as clerk of works in connectien with the erection of the Technical Institute, and that the Institute be insured for the sum of £3,500 (£3,000 for building,£soo for furniture.)—ln moving the adoption of these minutes, in referring to the first recommenda- tion he said wnen the contract for the Insti- tute was let it was necessary to have a clerk of works, and the architect, Mr. Bowden accepted Mr. Wilding (the assistant surveyor) as clerk of works. In the ordinary way it would have cost £75. He was very glad to say that Mr. Wilding had given every satisfaction.—Mr. Collier spoke against the grant of £25 to Mr. Wilding, stating that he had done most of the work in time which he had been paid for.—Mr. Lightburn and Mr. Imison each spoke in support of the motion, aid Mr. Bisbrown against. On a vote being taken the resolutions were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>FINANCII.</head>
        <p>FINANCII.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee recommended that the offer of Miss Ann Ravenseroft to lend the sum of £4OO for seven years at 3i per cent., and of the Loyal British Fleet Lodge, St. Helene, for £3OO for five years at per cent. for private street works be accepted, and that the sail of the board be affixed to the mortgage. That ap- plication be made to the Local Government Board for their sanction to a loan of £1,450 for 80 years for Cie building of the Technical Distitute instead of for £1,250 as asked fer ia accordance with resolution passed ou the sth July, 1894. That the tender of Mr. James Gres* for repainting, &amp;c. the board room for the sum of £4 18s. be accepted.---Mr. Lightburn in moving the con- firmation of the minutes said the Committee had also got an offer from the Morning Star of Oddfellows Lodge, Runcorn, for the loan of £l,OOO. The minutes were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>THH PURCHASH OF THE WATERWORKS.</head>
        <p>THH PURCHASH OF THE WATERWORKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Meadowcroft gave a short stat-ment of the first years working of the water works under the Board. He said the amount paid for the water works property was £67.500, and also £5Ol 183. 11d, for stock on hand. The law c,sts were £535, and commiseion £862, and the cost of the Act was £936, and with other items their capital account represented £70,640. The result of the year's working, after placing the sum of £528 to the sinking fund, showed a loss of £27 12s. 9,1. The total receipts for the rear amoon:-ed to £4,511 123. 6d. and the expenditure £4,485 Bs. BJ. They were therefore just' on the right side so far as actual expenditure and receipts were concerned. But in the expenditure £lll ss. sd. had been expended in cpvering the boilers, which would not be incurred in the coming year. The price of coal had also been higher than they expec••• ' •Ild be in the coming year. As that would be hi sion of saving a word upon the water qur• • ~, congratulate the Board on the course the irk r-t: •r.I to the Waterworks. So far a, the action B au was concerned, no one would question the step they took. He thanked them for the many courtesies he had received whilst a member of the Board.—Mr. Insison said he was truly sorry when he heard that Mr. Meadow- croft had fully made up his mind not to seek re-election on the Board. Mr. Meadowcroft had acted, he was sure, to the utmost of his ability for the benefit of the town, and had proved himself a most useful member of the Board.—Mr. Lightburn also added his testimony to the great advantage Mr. Meadowcroft had been to the Board. The ratepayers hardly knew how much they lost by his leaving them.—Mr. Meadowcroft thanked the speakers for the remarks they had made.</p>
        <p>THE PROI4OTION OF FIR JOHN RIGBY.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Sir John Rigby. now Lord Justice of appeal thanking the members tor the cordial vote of congratulation on his recent promotion. The report of the proceedings on tha graving Ll;c.'a question will be found in another column.</p>
        <p>. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>HOW TO DEFEAT OUR ENEIIIES.</p>
        <p>Everything. that tends to weaken our constitution and render it liable to disease is an enemy of mankind, and everything that tends to withstand the attacks of these enemies and to restore health and strength to the human system is a true friend. Among the most prominent ani most dreadful, like so many generals in the attacking forces of the great enemy of mankind—death—may be mentioned the following :—lndigestion, liver complaint, nervous disorders, and chest affections, while the differ- ent symptoms by which these diseases manifest them- selves may be regarded as so many sub-officers in the ranks of the enemy. When anyone of these attacks us we may be certain that some of the others are close at hand. The stomach, liver, lungs, nerves, and blood, are por- tions of the body that the enemy is very prone to assail, and if he succeeds in maintaining his position for any length of time in any one of them, and is not quickly expelled, he soon extends his pernicious dominions t) other parts of the body. When the stomach is dis- ordered,we may be certain that the enemy in some form or other has gained a foothold there, and if the food Me not properly dige,ted, the blood does not receive a suffi- ciency of the vital particulars necessary to diffuse through all parts of the system in order to afford proper nourishment to the body. In our climate, affections of the chest are very prevalent in the form of colds,coughs, bronchitis, asthma, Sze. By the insidious attacks of these various enemies of man, thousands are annually laid low and brought to a premature grave. This need not be the case. The enemies can be defeated and ousted. Make a determined stand against their attacks. Seek the aid of true and tried friends to eject them when they have taken possession of any part of the body. The best friend in such eases, and the one most certain to assist you in fepelling the enemy under any of the above guises in Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. It is a natural remedY, being entirely vegetable, each dose con- taining a suitable quantity of Quinine, together with the active principles of the following well-known medicinal herbs :—Sarsaparilla, Gentian, Burdock, Saffron, Laven- der, and Dandelion, combined in most happy propor- tions, and concentrated in a pure state, as well as being Scientifically prepared to li.e suitable to all ages, .and at all seasons of the year. Above all see that you get the right article, with the name GWILYM EVANS' QUININE Rrrritas on Stamp, Label, and Bottle. Refuse all imitations, and insist upon having NOTHIItiG BUT GWILYIL EVANS' QUININE BITTERS.</p>
        <p>THE VEGETABLE TONIC. Prices : Bottles, 2. 9J.; double sly., 4s. 6d. Sample bottles, is. lid. To be had of all Chemists, or direct from the Proprietors : QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING CO., LTD., LLANELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>WIFE ASSAULT.—FALSE SWEARING BY A WITNESS. At the Borough Police Court on Monday morning be- fore the Mayor (chairman) and other magistrates Wil- liam Smith, 12 Buckley-street, was charged with assault- ing his wife, Harriet Smith, in Winwick-road on Satur- day night, and also with assaulting P.C. Ryiatt whilst in the execution of his duty at the same dine and place, —P.C. Rvlatt said that on the night in question he was in Winwick-street when he heard a scream of "murder" and on proceeding in the direction of the sounds he saw the defendant kicking his wife who was on the ground. Be separated them when the defendant butted him in the face with his head. On the way to the police station defendant was very rough,kieking and biting the officer who had to call P.C. Dela.hay to his assistance.— P.C. Delahay deposed to seeing the defendant struggling with the last witness in a violent manner.—Mrs. Smith, wife of defendant, appeared with a swollen face and said that her husband did not strike her at all but P.C. Rylatt struck her over the eye with his fist and pushed her away. Her husband had never struck her since they were married. Mr. Hunt then called P.C. Hollingworth who deposed that a few weeks back he was in Win wick- road when the woman came to him and complained of her husband kicking her and knocking the baby out of 'her arms.—Sergeant Taylor also gave evidence showing that the man had fought with his wife.—Mr. Monks: This is a case of false swearing ; is not she liable for perjury. The magistrates clerk : Hn. evidence is value- less.—Defendant was fined 10s. and costs of 14 days hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>MINOR. CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR. CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT. Mol.7DAY.—Before the Mayor (Councillor Jas. Fairclough, in the chair), Alderman Barrison, Mr. F. Monks, and Mr. J. . Hayward. EXTENSION OF TIME.—John Davies was granted an ex- tension of time up to 12 o'clor,k on Tuesday night, the occasion of the Swimming Club dinner at the Baths Hotel. DRITNIC ANI;DISORDERLY.—ThP following persons were dealt with for this offence :—Wm. Lythgoe, labotirer. Rose and Crown-St., one month's hard labour; Saml. Slater, filecutter, 34. Barry-st., 2.5. 6d. ; Sarah Grant, no settled abode, 2s. 61 ; John Wm. Woodacre, 6, Black- hurst-st. , and also charged with assaulting P.C.'s Evans and Clarke, ss. and costs. DOG WITHOrT A LICENSE.—Thnmas Bate,s9, Longford- •st.,was charged with keeping a dog without a license on the 9th November. Sergeant Cheney deposed to seeing the defendant in possession of the dog, and on inquiry found that he had no license. Defendant on proving that the dog was not his, was dismissed. USING OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—Kate Hinds, 8, Hill-st., was fined ss. and costs for using obscene language to the annoyance of passengers on the 26th November</p>
        <p>last. STREET OBSTRUCTION.—John Casey was charged with leaving a horse and lurry an unreasonable time in Bridge-St., on the 27th November. P.C. Sankey proved the case, and said that the vehicle was in the street an bour and a half. Defendant, who did not appear, was fined 10s.. including costs. CHI3INEYS ON FIRE.—For allowing their chimneys to be on fire Richard Perslow, 239, Winwick-road, and James Gilooly, Wainwright's-yard, were each fined is. TtrESDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), Dr. T. S. Smith. and Mr. E. J. Guest. ScHoot ATTENDANCE CASE9.—On the prosecution of Nr. G. Riley (school attendance officer) the following persons were dealt with for not sending their children regularly to school :—Samuel Beckett, forg,ema.n, Hay- dock-street, ss. ; John Dutton, Silver-square. ss. ; James Heyes, Silver-square, ss. ; John Baguley, School-brow, ; Charles Higginson, Ellesmere-street, 2s. 6;1. ; Wm. Ryder. Ernest-street, 24. 63. ; Ellen Carter, Friars'-green. 25.—Elizabeth Giffiths was also summoned, and an order was made for her daughter to attend Fairfield School. 'WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough (in the chair), and Mr. J. H. Fleming. ' ANOTHER FAMILY DISAGREVMENT.—,IO4eph Ireland was char.ed with having on the 3rd December assaulted and beaten his wife Mary Ireland.—Mrs. Ireland, comnlain- ant, stated that defendant had dragged her by the hair cf her head on the Monday. Be had also broken her shoulder twice—Evidence was brought by defendant to 'prove that his wife was drunk at the time and he did not 'strike her at all.—Mr. Bunt said the defendant and complainant got drunk alternately, so the case was dismissed. DRUNK AND DISORDETILY.—Ma ry Grimes was charged with 1-eing drunk and disorderly in Frnghall-lane, on Tuesday night. P.C. Jones said defendant was using bad language and singing lewd songs. Defendant denied the latter charge and created some amusement •by saying she was singing "Sitting on a style. Mary " or The emigrant's farewell. The bench showed their appreciation of her musical efforts by sending her to practise for 14 days in gaol. THURSDAY.—Before Alderman Sutton. DESERTER —William Wilson was charged with being a -deserter from Her Majesty's 7th Royal Fusiliers at Aldershot since 1892. Lance Corporal Foy proved the case and defendant was remanded to await an escort. The officers' application for the usual reward was acceded to.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDAY.—Before Mr. Charles Reynolds (chairman) and Mr. F. J. Norman. TRANsrra or LICENSEs.—The following transfers of licenses ware granted : Castle Hotel, Halton, from John Simpson to Ellen Simpson ; Old Bridgewater Arms, Waterloo-road, from Thomas Heath Barton to John Royle Hughes Garnett; Mariners Hotel. Percival-lane, irOT., Joseph Hall to John Elwards ; Ship Inn. Heath- -road, from Philip Davies to .Tames Hunt ; Shamrock Tavern, Regent-street,from James Hunt to David Shaw; • Camden Hotel, Surrey-street, from Elizabeth Hancock to John Fleming ; Sloop Inn, Lock-street, from Sarah Ann Jones to Thomas Plummer ; Friendship Tavern, Fryer-street, from Thomas Finegan to Samuel Spann. ift REGULAR SCHOOL ATTENDANCE.—For neglecting te send their children regularly to school the follo.wing were dealt with : William Germany, South-road,Weston Point (two cases) 10a.; James Foster, Clifton,adjourned ; Rueben Pickering, Weston Point, ss. ; Stephen Perry. South-road, Weston Point, sa. The Chairman remarked that in the first ease ronsiderino• that it was the first offence of the defendant, he was of the opinion that Mr. • Cooper, the attendanee officer, should have only prose- cuterl in one case instead of two. Also, where there had been 90 attendances out of 117, as was the case with the last defendant,he thought that the committee should not bare prosecuted. The 27 absents might possibly he explained No NAME ON CA RT. —Alfrerl Jones, timber renrchant, Halton-road, was summoned for having on the 27th Nov- ember at Runcorn allowed a certain harry to he used in Irwell-lane without haying his name painted on it. He was ordered to par the costs. Mr. A. Illirgyrorth, M.P., has definitely decided not to again enn,st West Bradford, and a c.mmittee has been appointed to. consider the choice of a successor. It. is expected that Mr. H B. Priestman, a member of a Brad- ford family, will he the seleeted candidate. One of them has died and others are in danger. William Edward Wri,ht, teacher. H,,thatn, Hull, was ordered at Y,,rk AqsiV'S on Tuesday to he detained dur- ing her MA-sty's pleasure for attempting to cut his Pife'S throat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>PARISH COUNCILS.</p>
        <p>CLIF FON. Under the oresidencv of Mr. Joseph Stokes, the parish meeting was held at Clifton on Tuesday evening. It was proposed by Mr. William Wood, seconded by Mr. Peter Massey, that they make application to the County Council to establish a parish council for Clifton. After some discussion the motion was carried. Mr. Joseph Stokes was selected for the position of .district councillor.</p>
        <p>SUTTON.</p>
        <p>The parish meeting at -Sutton was held at the Aston Arms, when the chair was occupied by Mr. A. Rigby. The candidates nominated were :—George Aitken, John Bate, William Bidsbury, William Lowe, James McWaters, Thomas Rigby, Thomas Turner, Edwin Wallington, Alfred Lomas, Thomas Okell, and Walter Taylor. In order to prevent a contest the three latter gentlemen retired.</p>
        <p>NORTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. L. Evans presided-at-the parish meeting in this township. There were only eight candidates proposed for the eight councillors required, therefore the follow- ing were appointed without contest: John Acton, Clement Baguley, Charles Banner, Sir Richard Marcus Brooke, John Henry Horton, Thomas Horton, Charles Percival and Thomas Percival. WESTON POINT. The parish meeting at this village held in the Church schoolroom on Tuesday evening was well attended. Mr. Robert Cookson was unanimously appointed chairman. Nine candidates were nominated for the five vacancies. They were as follows :—Rev. David Saunders Bowen, Vicarage, Richard Robert Clarke, Clarke's Row, Wm. Frederick Clarke, Clarke's Rw, Thomas John Grounds, Hotel manager, John Jeffs, farmer, John Moores, Brine Works, Frederick Myers, Weston Point Lock, Louis Rosenberg, Weaver Villa, Samuel Savage, Weston Point- road. None of the candidates withdrew their names, and the chairman took a vote by show of hands, when it was found that Mr. Moores headed the list with 49 votes, closely followed by Mr. Richard Robert Clarke with 45 votes. The three next highest in the list were Samuel Savage (37 votes), Frederick Myers (33 votes), and L. Rosenberg (28 votes). After the reading of the figures Mr. Thos. Jeffs demanded a poll which will accordingly be taken. • H ALT O'N The parish meeting was held in the Girls' School, Halton, on Tuesday evening, to elect a chairman and twelve parish councillors. There was a large attend- ance of parochial electors as well as "spectators." Mr. Gamon was appointed chairman. The following nominations were then handed in, with the respective proposer and seconder, Ackerley John, Astmore, farmer; Asithury Joseph, Prospect Farm, farmer; Barrow Samuel, Haltan, cooper; BaqUleV J. M., Halton Brook, bank clerk; Birch Charles, Norton Arms Hotel, Halton, licensed victualler; Blain Samuel. Halton, assistant schoolmaster ; Fryer Alfred, Brook Terrace, Halton, engine fitter s Gregory Thomas, Halton-road, publican; 'Hancock Job, Hilton, Waterworks engine driver; Hol- lingworth George Scott, Astmoor Wharf, Halton-road, warehouseman ; Ronghton John Burgess, Fern Bank, Halton-road, chemical works storekeeper; Hughes Robert, Halton-road, tanner's foreman; Jeacock Jno. Samuel, Halton Common, schoolmaster; Looker James, Prospect Cottages, Halton, tanner's labourer; Leath. -wood George, ivy Cottage. Halton, tanner's labourer; McCulloch William, Holly Bank, Halton Brook, mechani- cal engineer; Moors Walter, Mersey View, Halton ; Man- ning Jas., Halton. manager; Pritchard Jos., Brook Place, Haltort•roa4, watchmaker and jeweller; Povey Alfred, Halton Hill, tanner's labourer; Randell James, Brook Terrace, Halton, farmer; Reynolds Henry, Norton Lodge, Norton, tanner: Rowe Hugh, Halter), grocer Stokes ;resent', Clifton. farmer; Stubbs Jersenh, Brook Dale, Halton Brook; Wray George Daniel, Halton Hill House, clerk in orders. An opportunity was then given for asking the candidates questions, and a considerable time was spent in heckling those nominated. The queries were principally directed to the Rev. G. D. Wray and Mr. Reynolds. and had reference to charities. They both expressed their willingness for the fullest informa- tion being obtained. Mr. Reynolds was not desirous to convert tha Halton Library to a public library, as it was intended for the benefit of the clergymen. He was afraid the hooks would not prove very interesting to the general public% they being in Hebrew, Greek a.r.d Latin. Mr. Pritchard thought the Chesshyre charity had not been carried out in the true spirit in which it was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>given. The vnting was taken with the following result : Ackerler 47, AstburY 61, Barrow 54, BaguleY 43. Birch 42, Blain 38. Fryer 49. Gregory, 22. Hancock 32, Hollins!. worth 32, Horn7hton 45. Hughes 60, .Teacock 62. Looker 70. Lea.thwnnd 60, McCullnck 62, Moors 34, Manning 54, Pritchard 58, Po7ey 49. Randell 41, Reynolds 53, Rowe 63, Stokes 95, Stubbs 48, Wray 52. The Chairman declared the twelve gentlemen who had received the highest number of votes to have been elected by the meeting. A demand for a poll was made by Mr. Blain on behalf of the vicar. WESTON. The first meeting of the ratepayera under the New Local Government Act. for the nurnose of electing eiyht councillors for the Weston ward of the Weston Township Parish Council was held in the Church schoolroom on Tuesday evening. There WA. a very la,qc, attendance. On the proposition of the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood M.A., seconded by Mr. E. Aaron. Mr. Thomas Handley J.P., was unanimously voted to the chair. Mr. Handley thanked the audience very much for eleeting him to the honourable position of chairman of the first meeting under the new system of Gavernment, which would no doubt he handed down for ayes to come He announced that the first lansinesa was the handinc, in of nnininatian papers of cani'idates of the Weston Ward of the Parish Conncil.—lmmediately a large number of ratepayers came forward and handed in papers. The Chairman stated that there were eight scat to be filled for the Weston ward, and that each parochial elector could only vote for eight natnes. Persons who were not voters had, therefore, better retire to the rear of the room : and a number took this advice. Fifteen minutes as presc.rihed in the Ant were now allowed for the handing in of further nominations and the chairman and assistant overseer (Mr. James C. Collier) careray eKamin‘A each parr and ascertained that it fulfilled the requirements of the law. This occunied altogether half an hour. The Chairman then announced that he had received 30 nomination papers and 13 of these were dilnlinate4, leaving. 17 persons nominated. The C,hairm in read the list of nominations as follows : —Ed via rd Aaron, Mersev View, Weston, gentleman; Dani Basnett. Heath-road, Weston, grocer ; John Rail U, Victoria-rnal, Ronnorn, insurance agent: Wm. Dtanhabin, Old Hall. Farm, Weston. farmer : James- Ford, Sen., Weston, farmer; James Ford, jun.. Mersey view, Weston, farm manager: Arthur G enrze Gnest lVferser-Yiew,Weston,manae•er of Stone Qqarr;p, ; 7h,„ Griffiths, Weston, farmer ; Wm. Griffiths. Clifton Hall, farmer ; John Grlffiths. The Grane.e. Weston, fanner ; Richard Robt. N. Nisbet. Ford Bank-terrine. %Weston, foreman cooper ; Thomas Wm. Pielrerinc!, Colliers-row, Weston, titnAteener ; Thomas Toward Sill. Weston Villa. mana?er of Chemical Works : J.Jntin Ellis Sweet- man. WPcl.on (via .."manr ; JATTI.4 Tudor. gel th-road. Weston, Chemical Works foreman : John Vose. Quarry' Bank fintel. Henth-roaa. beerhouse-keeper ; Rev. Alfred , Maitland Wood, The Vicarage, Runcorn, Vicar of Run corn.</p>
      </div>
