      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 8, '1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 8, '1894.</p>
        <p>After some discussion Canon Wood retired from the, room during the voting and the other candidates fol- lowed his example,with the exception of nossrs.Thomas and William Griffiths, the latter remarking that he wanted things honest, upright and square. The Chair- man now took a vote by show of hands with the follow- ing result :—Aaron 13, Basnett 46*, Ball 40*, Dunbabin 22, Ford sen. 27*, Ford jnn. 18. Guest 27*, Griffiths (Thos.) 28*, Griffiths (Wm.) 23, Griffiths (John) 20, Nisbet 13, Pickering 15, Sill 26*, Sweetman 38*, Tudor 26*, Vose 26*, and Wood 10. Messrs. Sill, Tudor and Vose were equal and. the Chairman gave his casting vote for the two former. The eight selected are shown by an asterisk. Mr. Nisbet demanded a poll. Mr. Wm. I Griffiths : Whose expense will it be at? The Chairman: The ratepayers. Mr. Griffith : I should let it stand as it is then. (Laughter.) After the lapse of the requisite ten minutes, the Chairman announced that poll would take place on Saturday, the 15th December, from 12 noon to 8 p.m. He then declared the meeting at an end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>GEO. G-. BUSSEY &amp; CO. are the leading makers o requisites for Football, Hockey. Golf, Baseball, Gymnasia, and all Sports and Games. Illlustrated catalownes may be obtained from the local dealers, or the Sports Manufactory Peckham, London. Trade &lt; C.C.D &lt;t-. Mark.</p>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON v. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>There is an old superstition that it is unlucky to meet a funeral when you are carrying out an undertaking which you desire should be successful. Whether there is any foundation in fact for such a superstition we do not know, but we do know that the Warrington first team met a funeral on their way to the Leigh foot- ball grouuti on Saturday and—lost. Probably they would have lost in any case, but the fact that they met a funeral and then lost shows that cases may occur in these.advanced days which might be quoted in support of the old superstition. Warrington had only a moderate team, among the absentees being Nevins. Boscow, Buckley, and Cunningham. Travelling to Leigh on the London and North-Western Railway is a very tedious process, as all who have been on the journey will know, so the committee very wisely arranged that the team should go by 'bus. There were two disadvantages even attending this arrangement, and these were that there was a delay in starting, and consequently a late arrival at Leigh, 'and, secondly, the reporter who accompanied the team was left behind at Croft on the return journey, an event which would not have occurred had the team gone by train. In the summer time Croft is a pretty place no doubt, and is larg7ly patronised by pic-nic parties, &amp;c., but on a cold winter's night, when it is im- possible to see a ditch on the road side till you find your- self in it, there is only one attractive spot at Croft, and that is where the reporter was left behind. But even this spot lost its attractiveness, when he found that it was necessary to walk six miles along th%dark, dismal country roads to Warrington. Mr. Peter Maginn pro- vided a capital turnout and gave 'great satisfaction by the way in which he handled the reins. The journey to Leigh was performed in an hour and twenty minutes, there being no stoppages on the road. How long the re- turn journey occupied the writer cannot say, as he only got as far as Croft. The game at Leigh was well con- tested, and it would have been a great pity to have robbed the home team of a victory. The Leigh specta- tors were very enthusiastic. and it is doubtful whether the Warrington team would not have been mobbed had they happened to win. This was Leigh's first match after being suspended ten weeks for professionalism,and the Leigh spectators may perhaps be excused for giving vent to their feelings in the way they did when we con- sider that they had been pent up for ten weeks. Leigh put their best team on the field, the only absentee beinZ Wilding. whose place was taken by McMasters. Owing to the late arrival of the visitors, it was only possible to play two twenty-fives, and in the latter half it was al- most impossible t-) see the hall, it had become so dark. Early in the game Leigh entered the Warrington quarters, and the ball getting loose from a scrimmage. Leigh dribbled across the field. Very few of the War- rington players appeared to know where the hall had got to, and Anderton got over near the corner, the place kick failing. This was the only score during the game. Leigh played well all through, hut the W,=.rrington de- fence was excellent, otherwise they would have piled up a big score. The teams were : Warrington : Full back, Fleesom ; three-quarter backs, Foden, Potts, Burton. and Moores half backs, Bate and Clark ' • forwards,Turner, Berry, Taylor, Houghton, Bailey, Dakln, Thorniley,and Andrews. Leigh : Full hank, Cook; three.quarter backs, Guest, Anderton, Wallwork, and MeMasters ; half backs. Ewan and Shovelton ; forwards. Smith, Cheetham, Franle,Boardman, Eccleston, Williams, Pem- berton, and Shaw.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON RANGERS 2ND V. WARRINGTON This match match took place on the Rangers' ground on Saturday. The game was fast and interesting, but the Rangers' held the upper hand, and would not be denied, and after some tine passing by the forwards, Plinsten got the ball and dodged in under the posts. The whistle sounded for half time with the score 4 tries to nil in favour of the homesters. The second half was of a scrambling nature, the home team having all the play, in which Bailey scored 3, and Plinston 1. A one-sided game thus ended in a win for the Rangers by 8 tries to nothing. RUNCORN V. LIVERPOOL OLD Boys—Flayed at Runcorn on Saturday, the fog interfering with the match. Run- corn had six men away at Durham assisting the Cheshire county team. Owing to the fog the Old Boys were late, and only a, very short game was played. W. Faulkner started for Runcorn, the visitors returning to the centre. After a series of scrimmages, Runcorn got in the Old Boys' half, but Harrison cleared nicely. J. Faulkner got hold from a scrimmage, and passing between Light- foot, Richardson, and Abrams, took play to the visi- tors' half, when Lighfoot, a minute after, got over be- hind the posts W. Faulkner kicking the goal. From tile re-start, Walker charged the kick, and dribbled well up. The homesters now broke away, and passing be- ween W. Faulkner, Lightfoot, and Richardson, ended in the latter scoring. Lightfoot took the kick at goal, but the ball fell short. Score at half-time :—Runcorn, one goal one try ; Old Boys, nil. Final result—Runcorn, one goal four tries ; Old Boys, nil. WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. RUNCORN RECREATION.— This match was played on the Liverpool-road ground on Saturday, in dull, damp weather before a good muster of spectators. The Saints were poorly represented. After a series of scrimmages at half way Warburton transferred play to the visitors' territory. W. Bennett secnring passed to Flampson, and a long kick gave Lees- wood trouble. which he just managed to cl,a.r. From a scrimmage Warburton got possession, p, Bennett, and he to Ilainpson, who,11(14( cl through the thick of the visitors and scored a grand try, the same player landing a goal. Resuming, Lawless returned well to centre, and a smart passing movement, started by Warburton and taken part in by O'brien and Hampson, again called upon the visitors to defend. A combined rush by the Runcorn forwards called upon Roug,hley to save. Runcorn kept up the pressure, the forwards rushing the ball to the Saints' lines, but they could not break through the splendid defence of the homesters. The Saints found relief by a kick, and play raged at centre for some time, when a bout of passing by the Runcorn backs was checked by the accurate:tackling of Hampson. From a rush by the Saints' forwards O'Brien secured and was making good headwa7 when be was well tackled by Worrall. Leeswood gave relief by a smart run and kick, and half-time arrived with the Saints leading by goal to nil. Swift commenced the second half, and no return being made the homesters pressed. From a scrim- mage Hampson accepted a pass from W. Bennett, threaded his way through his opponents, and scored his second try. which was not improved. Leeswood kicked well and Hearne secured, but was tackled on the line when looking dangerous, and Runcorn experienced hard lines in not scoring, Darkness setting in the players could hardly be distinguished, and the Saints ran out victors by one goal one try to nil.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIA</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>WLRRINGTON " A " v. LEIS "A."—Leigh's first en- counter after their two month's suspension was the match with Warrington on the Wildersponl ground on Saturday. The weather was not the hest suited to foot- ball but still the two teams contrived to have a lively time of it as lone as the match lasted. The teams were composed as follows :—Warrington : Knight-, full back t Weave, Lawless, Edelsten, and Foster, three-quarters ; Flster and Bennett, half-hacks ; Fairhurst, Handles, Wilson, Hill, Dobson, Saunders, Ha mpson, and Burrows, forwards. Leigh : Cunliffe, full back ; Page, Davies, Shovelton, Davis, three-quarter;; Officer and Farrinv- ton, half hacks: Roberts, Hall, Latham, Phoenix, C. Hindley, J. HindleY, A. McMaster, and Cleworth, for- wards. Owing to the lateness of the Leigh men War- rington were unable to kick off until four o'clock—one hour after the time announced. Hardly had the game got going before Lawless for Warrington broke loose and after a quick run scored the first try of the match amid an enthusiastic burst of applause from the spectators, who were massed in fairly large numbers. Warrington still continued to press their opponents and numerous scrimmages and line-out; took place. Another oprinrtunity presenting itself Foster took advantage, and after an almost unimpeded run scored another try for Warrington which ,a as immediately con- verted into a goal. From scrimmages Leigh were pena- lised for off-s;de play three times in succession, and free kicks granted to Warrinton, hut nothing resulted. After a short bout of give and take play another scrimmage resulted ; this time Warrington were penalised for the same offence, and the free kick by Leigh went very near the home goal. Only a short interval had elapsed when Foster again getting possession after a brilliant run son- needed in planing the hall behind the line,Randles neatly converting. Half time was then called after 33 min ntes' Play with the score r.ading Warrington 2 ',oafs 1 try, Leigh nil. Oa restarting Lawless Was again to the fore with another try which was converted into a goal. A few more passes were made and then,nwing to the dark- ness, time was called with the score Warrington 3 goals 1 try, Leigh nil. Thriighout the match it was very evi- dent that WarringtAn were the superior team, Leigh not once having a decent opoortnnity of scoring. RUNCORN 25D V. OLD BOYS.—Pla.Ved at Wooderoft. The hninesters started. Each side played up well, lint Runcnrn had slightly the hest of matters for some time. Eventually Myers ran in for the visitors on two separate occasions, and kicked a couple of goals. The Old Boys invaded the Thincorn quarters on two or three occasions, hot the visitors had most of the play. Final result,— Runcorn " A " 3 goals 4 tries, Old Boys " A " nil.</p>
        <p>---, WARRINGTON ASSOCIATION V. MELLING.—This match took place at Warrington on Saturday. There was a very fair number of spectators present. Warrington kicked of and soon forced their opponents back, and by some smart passing by the forwards almost scored. After some good play T. Mather shot a beautiful goal. On the restart the visitors by capital combination increased their score, the point being gained by the inside left shooting through after the ball had rebounded off the goalkeeper. Half time was then called with the scores equal. Melling kicked off in the second half, and were beginning to show much better form, but Warrington had the best of matters, and Connor getting the ball down the field passed to Smithson, who transferred to Jewkes, and the last-named player by a good shot suc- ceeded in placing his club in the ascendency. The game from now to the end was very interesting, each citadel being attacked in turn. The whistle sounded for time with the scores—Warrington Association, two goals ; Melling, one goal. WARRINGTON PRINTERS v. SANKEY C.LB..—Played on the Printers' ground on Saturday. The opening stages were in favour of Sankey, but the homesters woke up and transferred the play to the other end, where they had hard lines in front of goal. Good back play by the visitors kept theirscharge intact for a time, but a good run by the Printers' left wing ended in the ball being sent through. Half-time arrived with the score three to none in favour of the homesters. When time was called the Printers had won by four goals to nil. FARNWORTH V. PARR VICTORIA (ST. HELENS).—At Faroworth on Saturday, before a large attendance. The home team started, and the visitors quickly assumed the aggressive and scored two goals under five minutes. Farnworth warmed to their work, and good play by the forwards enabled George Randle to notch the first point for the villagers. From the centre of the home team • made strenuous efforts to draw level, which they did just on half t,me. Ashcroft landed the ball in the net shortly after tke restart for Farnworth, who ran out winners by four goals to three. HOWLEY WANDERERS V. REWSEY ROVERS.—At Howley. The visitors ki-Lkei off and had hard lines in not scoring in the first half. ' Second half, Howley kicked off and played well; shortly afterwarls a grand pass from Daintith to Whiteley ended in a goal. BewsPy kicked off and had hard lines in not scoring, and Howley took the ball down and Jones gave a shot which went through the goal and the time was up. Howley 2 goals, Bewsey nil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RESERVE V. Sr. HELENS L. &amp; N.W.R. RECREATION RESERVE.—At Runcorn on Saturday, before a good attendance. The Rees arrived with only ten men, but found an excellent substitute in Casson, of the home first eleven. Teams :—Runcorn; Goal, Hulse; backs, Parry and Green; half backs, Gleave, Clarke, and Bell ; forwards, Bailey. A. N. Other. Upton,Linton, and Burrows. St. Fleleas : Goal, Morris ; backs. Ken- yon and Phythian ; half backs, I. Davies, Casson, and Edwards ; forwards, A. Davies, Fairhurst, Worrell. Scott, and Flavell. Worrell started, and after some give and take play the visitors scored three goals in quick sucession, Flavell (2) and Davies. After a while Runcorn played up pluckily against odds, and Bailey scored. Half time: Recreation, three; Runcorn, one. Upton started the second half, and play was taken to the Rees' goal, where Upton scored. After this Runcorn played very hard to draw level, and after repeated shots Burrows put the ball right through and equalized amid great excitement. Towards the close the Runcorn forwards went for the goal with the ball at their toes. Linton scoring. Runcorn thus won by four goals to Recreation three. NEWTON RESERVE V. WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S.—This match was played on the Meanes ground on Saturday last. The home team were well represented, but St. Mary's brought only a weak team, being short of four of their regular players. At half-time the Newton forwards were leading by 4 goals to nil when the whistle sounded. In the second portion the home team• had matters all their own way, the custodian not being called upon ,and ' scoring four more goals won a pleasant though one-sided game by 8 goals to nil. CHESHIRE MAGPIES V. WARRINGTON S. ELPFEINS.— Played at Chester. The visitors kicked off, and scored in the first few minuo-es. Play was of a very give and take form and half-time arrived without any further scoring. In the second half the Magpies scored three times and won by three to one. ALL SAINTS' V. PARISH Crrtracrr.—Played on the ground of the latter and resulted in a win for the Saints by five goal to nil. The goals being- scored by S. Muckley two, Hinchliffe two, Gandy one.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. WARRINGTON AN!) DISTRICT AMATEUR LEAGUE FfRqT DIVISION. Results up to Saturday last Goals</p>
