ire, West Ditto. Cumberland, Wigan,Manchester, 6-0 P.m. Peterborough, and East Lincoln- o extra fe shire 6-15 p.m Town Delivery Preston, West Cumberland, Liver- Dit O. 9_30 p.m. pool, Birkenhead, Ormskirk, Isle of Man, &amp; Glasgow 10-30 p.m. Night Mail Despatch. All Parts London, Midlands, S.-Eastern &amp; 11-15 p.m. South Counties. &amp; Foreign ...... 10-45 p.m.</p>
        <p>Letter 0 to the ttor.</p>
        <p>PASTEURISNI AND HYDROPHOBIA.</p>
        <p>Sir.—The discovery of a "remedy for hydrophobia by Pasteur is claimed by the pro-vivisectionists as a " triumph" that should give a quietus to the "silly agitation "against a necessary and beneficial research. Many are their claims; but the case for the practice which they seek to build on the Pasteur inoculations for hydrophobia is a most dismal failure—the most dismal of all. Is it not a fact that up to the present time not less than 268 persons have died after undergoing this treatment. either because it failed to protect them, or the treatment itself produced paralytic rabies? Only two or three months ago Pasteur failed to protect a little English lad who, on the recommendation of a medical man, was sent to him and underwent his treatment, and now we have the case of Lieut. Malcolm Stevenson of the 93rd Highlanders who recently died at Birkenhead "under most remarkable circumstances." Accord- ing to the press reports Mr. Stevenson, while with his regiment in India, was bitten by a dog which, on exa- mination, was found to be mad. He was thereupon sent off to the Pasteur Institute, Paris, where he under- went the usual course of treatment, from whence he came to Birkenhead, apparently " quite cured," and looking remarkably well. He, however, was taken ill again, and died "three days later from a peculiar for in of hydrophobia." What are these circumstances? The public has not been apprised and warned of the dangers pertaining to Pasteur's inoculations ; and that this "pecu- liar form of hydrophobia," far from being "remarkable," is nothing more or less than paralytic rabies, a new disease, and a terrible one, too, for the introduction of which Pasteur himself is alone responsible (why not label the disease with the name of its distinguished in- ventor?) This charge is male upon the authority of the late Prof. Peter, Profs Billroth and von Frisch, Dr. Lutaud, Prof. Jules Guerin, Dr. Dulles, Prof. Collin. the late Dr. Constantine James, Prof. Augusto Michelacci, Profs Amorose and Renzi, and many others. Prof. Peter, in a letter to Dr. Dolan, the editor of the " Provincial Medical Journal, said : . . . " the mortality from hydrophobia in France has been augmented since Pasteur began his work, and not only is the mortality increases, but cases of paralytic rabies have been introduced by later inoculations." Commenting on eleven cases which happened within a period of twelve weeks, Dr. Lutaud says :—" Thus eleven cases of paralytic rabies happen- ing in less than three months what can we conclude from the facts if not that the new treatment has considerably , augmented the danger from hydrophobia, and that in ' eleven cases at least he has introduced the malady into men who had not got it 7 M. Pasteur does not cure hydrophobia—he gives it." I might quote many English authorities also in support of the charge first brought against Pasteur by Prof. Peter in an address to the Aca- demy, that by his inoculations he has introduced into human beings paralytic rabies • but it is needless : the charge, which has been repeatedly made, has never .yet been contested, either by Pastrur or by any of his sup- porters. Sines, then, Pasteur's treatment, besides being useless, is worse than useless, in that it sometimes kills of itself, may I point out that the alternative treatment is the Turkish baths according to the method of the late Dr. Buisson, which has proved efficacious, is in accord- ance with natural laws, and is easily obtained 7 Why _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>is the system advocated by Dr. nuisson so persistently ignored by the medical faculty (it is absplutely no treat- ment for the disease ; why does it so persistently ignore it F). I have one solution : as it calls for no sacrifice of animal life, with the cruelties attendant thereon, it is not to the interest of those in search of Fame with a big F, along with large salaries, which this age of medicine by research provides.—l am, yours faithfully. JOSEPH COLLINSON. Fi-ont street, Wolsingham, Co. Durham 1894.</p>
      </div>
