      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>INGRATITUDE OF THE SEMINAL CONTROVERSIALISTS.</head>
        <p>THE INGRATITUDE OF THE SEMINAL CONTROVERSIALISTS.</p>
        <p>write to sympathise with Mr. Peers and to criticise his "more light on the seed problem. I must say that he has done his very best to acquire truth and to publish it. In doing this he has made an attempt to elevate man and to make this world a better place to live in ; for " it is truth and only truth that can make us free." Therefore the pursuit of truth is at once noble and ennobling. Experience teaches us that error has never built a bridge, a boat, eaed a pain, or cultivated a flower. Error is the name of that " devil who goeth about like a roaring lion seeking whom he may devour." Whilst truth like " mercy is twice blesFed ; it btesseth him that gives and him that takes." The wise man said " buy truth and sell it not," and it is the price we must pay which makes the transaction so difficult, viz., our old beliefs. Dear old things that have been planted in our minds with such loving care and tended with great solicitude. And then. 0 ! how we have hugged it till our existence and it have become interwoven.</p>
        <p>Now, sir, you see our condition. We believe that seed dies, that it must die in order to produce a plant, also that nothing Cali be truer than this, and to us it is the very best philosophy We have hugged it till our ex- istence and it have become interwoven. Nor 'can we part from it easily. On the other hand, Mr. J. Peers says that we are in the wrong—that we believe a lie which he has been kind enough to lautih at and to ridicule. Yet his arguments have been very few and somewhat mild. Then he quotes Huxley and several others, all great men, which of course ought to convince us of the error of our ways, but somehow they do nothing of the sort. lam afraid that we are not so teachable as we ought to be. "This is bad, but 1 worse remains behind," for we are charged with the sin of ingratitude. When Mr. Peers has laboured "to lay the bare truth before us," have we not laboured to lay the bare truth before hlm ? Then, on no side is there room for many thanks. Again, Mr. Peers speaks of "a demonstration" "a positive demonctra,tion." This demands our serious consideration. Unfortunately this demonstration is in a damaged condition A demonstration is a public and not a private affair. He has spoken of an experiment in the following terms" Where now he the Goliaths and Gama- liels of this immortal subject ?" Can it be that the one small pebble of " fact " from the sling of experiment has smitten the "mighty ones" of orthodoxy to judicious silence?" There is one thing we must bear in mind; David slung his "pebble" at Goliath in the face of two contending armies—there was no lack of witnesses. Had</p>
        <p>this been done in a back garden some persons might have entertained doubts and would have said that the idea of that boy killing that monster with an oiince of stone: Why it was preposterous! But David killed him in the most undeniable manner possible. And if Mr.. Peers wishes to demonstaate that a grain of wheat con- tained a living form and that this is the order of nature, he must publish the process in such a manner as will enable your readers to conduct the experiment for them- selves, and so demonstrate the truth of his assertions. Please note: I do not for a moment think that he is try- ing to mislead. Far from that. But are we sure that we understand each other. What I want to know is this. Be says in yours of November 10th : "To thic end I dissected the germ or embryo from many grains of wheat." How did he make Vie invisible appear to sight? Then he goes on to say—" and grew them successfully while the remainder of the seed remained and still remains unchanged." This is far beyond the reach of my poor mind. Does Mr. Peers mean to say that wheat in the granary contain an embryo, "that the germ dies and is revived." This is positively positive nonsense, for there is no revival needed, nor am I "determined to slay the dead." Again, "Now the question hinges very much upon the definition as to what constitutes vegetal life) and vegetal death. A plant (why, bless my soul, it is SEED we are talking about, not plants) to be truly said to be living must possess at least two of these three powers—assimilation, growth, and reproduction. Wheat flour possesses none of these powers. I should think not ; nor wheaten flour either, but why flour at all? Does flour mean the dead part of the seed? If so, I may remark that flour is a product of art, seed is a product of nature. lam really afraid Mr. Peers has plants and seed hopelessly mixed. To me plants and seed are diverse. For in- stance, plants possess the power of assimilation,growth, and reproduction. Seed possesses latent life, and some- thing when pulverised becomes flour. Seed does not grow, and is unchangeable. But when seed is sown in the earth a great change comes over it, and it rapidly looses the nature of seed and assumes that of a plant. The earths the air, the light, the sun, all assist in bring- ing about this great and glorious transformation. The residue of the seed is converted into sugar, which the plant assimilates. Thus, the latent life of the seed be- comes the active life of the plant. Unquestionably the seed dies and is consumed by the plant.—Yours truly. FELIX SAMUELLS.</p>
        <p>The Queen of Madagascar has issued a manifesto ex- horting her subjects to resist the French invaders, and the enthusiasm among the Hovas is very great. BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of this class was held on Wednes day evening, Mr. Simister in the chair. There was a very good attendance. A paper was read by Mr. F. Stubbs on " Self made man." A good discussion followed in which a large number took part.</p>
      </div>
