      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>PRAYING FOR MAX O'RELL.</head>
        <p>PRAYING FOR MAX O'RELL.</p>
        <p>Max O'Rell, in his latest book, " John Bull and C 0.," tells this story about himself :—I had just returned to the hotel after having given a lecture on the Scotch at the town hall. I was half undressed, when there came a knock at my bedroom door. It was a waiter bearing a card ; the Presbyterian minister of the town wished tO see me at once on a very urgent matter. I bid the waiter show the reverend gentleman up. A man of about 50, in the usual black ecclesiastical coat and white cravat,and holding a soft felt hat, appeared in the doorway, wearing a sad face. I recognised him as one of my audience that evening. " Excuse my costume," I began ; 't but you wished to speak to me on urgent business, and I thought best not to make you wait." "There is nothing the matter with your dress," he broke In; "this is not an affair of the body, but of the soul. I have come to pray for you." I was taken a little by surprise, and felt a trifle , discountenanced, but I quickly regained my composure. ' " Why, certainly," I said ; " with the greatest pleasure, if it can make you happy." He knelt, put his elbows on the bed, buried his head in his hands, and began: "Lord this man whom thou seest near me is not a sinful man; • he is suffering from the evil of the century ;he has not • been touched by Thy grace; he is a stranger, come from a country where religion is turned to ridicule. Grant that his travels through our godly lands may bring him into the narrow way that leads to everlasting life." The prayer, most of which I spare you, lasted at least ten minutes. When he had finished, my visitor rose, and held out his hand. I shook it. "And now," said I, "allow me to pray for you." He signified consent by a movement of the hand. I did not go • on my hnees, but with all the fervour that is in me I cried : " Lord, this man whom thou seest beside me not a sinful man. Have mercy upon him for he is a Pharisee, who doubts not for one moment, and that without knowing me. that he is better than I. Thou who has sent in vain Thy Son on earth to cast out the Pharisees, let Thy grace descend upon this one ; teach him that the foremost Christian virtue is charity, and that the greatest charity is that which teaches us that we are no better than our brethren. The man is blinded by pride; convince him, open his eyes, pity him, and forgive him, as I forgive him. Amen." I looked at my good Presbyterian. He war rooted to the floor, amazement written on his face. I once more took his hand and shook it. " And now," said I," we are quits. Good-night." He went away somewhat abashed, pocketing the mild reproof. To THU PoeicE.—Notice has been sent to the police in the larger market towns, respecting a fraud carried on by street vendors. As is well-known, the late extraor- ilinary cures in this district have attracted widespread attention to the medicine by which they were effected, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and it appears that street and market-place vendors have been detected fraudulently selling large quantities of loose pills under Dr. Williams' name. It cannot be too widely known that Dr. Williams' Pills are sold only in wooden boxes, enclosed in a pink wrapper, bearing the full name Dr. Williams' Pills for Pale People, and anythine else offered either by shopkeepers or others, as Dr. Williams' is a fraud, for the detection of which the manufacturers will pay a cash reward. Should any doubt exist, the best plan is to send the price in t-,amps, without any- thing extra for postage (2s. 9d.-13s. 9d. for six boxes) to Dr. William s' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn Viaduct, London. The genuine Pills are never sold loose, or by the dozen or hundred under any circum- stances, and no tradesman can put up "the same formula" as some pretend. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are a proved cure for rheumatism, neuralgia, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, St. Vitus' dance, nervous headache, , scrofula, chronic erysipelas, &amp;c. They are not purgative, but restore pale and sallow complexions to health, and • are specific for female troubles, while in men they effect a radical cure in all icases from worry, over-work, or excess of any nature.</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>SCOTCHMAN'ST SELF-CONTROL.</head>
        <p>A SCOTCHMAN'ST SELF-CONTROL.</p>
        <p>Charles Mathews used to tell a good story in support of the truth of the remark anent a Scotchman, a joke, aud a surgical operation. When " starring "in Edin- burgh, his landlord, who seldom attended any other public meeting save the "Kirk," asked Mathews if he would oblige him with •• a pass for the playhoose." This favour being readily granted, the " guid man" donned his cheerful black suit, and witnessed Mathew's two great performances, Sir Charles Goldstream in "Used Up," and Plummer in "Cool as a Cucumber," both downright side-splitters. Meeting his landlord on the stairs as he proceeded to his own room after the performance, Mathews was cordially greeted by that gentleman, of whom he then inquired how he had enjoyed the entertainment. " Aweel," said the North- erner, "it pleased me yara much, ye ken, and I con- seeder you played unco naturally ; but, heigh, moo, I'd a hard matter to keep frae laughing."</p>
        <p>SOVEREIGN REMEDIES FOR SKIN ERUPTIONS To Cure Eczema, Skin Eruptions, Irritation, Pim- ples, Blotches. Ulcerated Bad Legs and Sores, use • lUL.I,LIVICK'S SKIS' OINTMENT AND</p>
        <p>LOOD TABLETS.</p>
        <p>WORLD-WIDE REPUTATION.</p>
        <p>Either ointment or Tablet s . ls. lid., from all Chemists post ee 15. 31.. from MARTIN. Charnigt, Southamptc.,n. BOOTS, Cash Chemists, Warrington. Agents SPNAKLIAN 27, Bridge-st., ituncorn.</p>
        <p>NEWTON BOARD OF IMPROVEMENT</p>
        <p>COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of this Authority, which was held at the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Mon- day, there were present, Messrs. G. Ratcliffe, P. MacMahon, P. Thompson, J. Smith,W. Coltingwood, E. T. Evans, T. Forster, J. E. Worsley (law clerk) and R. Brierley (clerk and surveyor). Mr. J. W. Emmett being unable to attend, Mr. G. Ratcliffe was elected to the chair, on the motion of Mr. MacMahon seconded by r. Smith.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC WORKS AND SANITARY COMMITTEE. At a meeting of this committee held November 19, counsel's opinion was read upon the. rights of tolls on Newton Common, and it was resolved that it be further .considered, when the Law Clerk was present. The Surveyor reported a breach of the building bye-laws by John Lea at his buildings near the crossing in Crow-lane, and it was resolved to take legal proceedings at once against him, and that the justices be urged to enforce the penaltles. The Sur.vevor read his correspondence with Miss M. Winstanley respecting her deviations from plans submitted for alterations to premises in High-st. It was resolved that she be requested to comply with the plans and submit plans for additional premises within three days, and that she have notice to remove the door-step encroaching on High-street, within 42 hours, failing which the Surveyor order his workmen to remove it. It was also resolved that the correspond- ence with Mr. J. Crampton and adjoining property- owners in Wargrave New-road be submitted to the law clerk to take such proceedings he may consider desir- able. The proceedings of a sub-committee of Authori- ties in the Warrington and Leigh unions re provision of I a joint smallpox hospital were submitted and approved. The following plans were submitted and passed : Thos. Platt and E. T. Evans, for alterations to shops in Market-street, Mr. Mills conversion of lock-up-shop to a dwelling in Cross-lane ; new workshop of Mr. Beatty, builder in Wargrave New-road.—The adoption of the minutes was moved by Mr. Ratcliffe, seconded by Mr. MacMahon. The law clerk said that a summons had been taken against Lea, bat when the case was brought before the magistrates, the case was adjourned for a month, owing to a technical point raised at the trial.— The minutes were unanimously passed.</p>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE. The minutes of the meeting of this committee held on November 19, showed that the manager's report had been read, and that the following was the carbonizing account for the mouth was fuel used 398 tons, 10 cwts. ; and the gas made, 4,003, 900 feet, being an average of 10,047 feet per ton of fuel used. Mr. Swarm was instructed to give a detailed account on the water mains requiring renewal, with an estimate of the cost.—Mr. Smith suggested that the illuminating power of the gas shonld be given each month.—Mr. Brierley was instructed to attend to this suggestion, and the minutes were passed unanimously on the motion of Mr. Ratcliffe, seconded by Mr. MacMahon.</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE. At a meeting of this committee held on November 19, it was resolved that Mr. Brierley call upon the surveyor of taxes at Warrington, to revise the assessments in the gas and water departments. Accounts amounting to £699 13s. 7d. were recommended for payment —The minutes were passed on the motion of Mr. Smith, seconded by Mr. MacMahon.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE. This Committee reported that they had instructed Mr. Ball, the school attendance officer to inquire from Mr. Taylor, schcolinaster of Haydock, if he intended to receive into his school again, after the examinations, the Children from Newton Common. If not, Mr. Ball was to see that the children go to some other school.—The minutes were passed on the motion of MacMahon, seconded by Mr. Evans. HOURS FOR POLLING AT THE DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Collingwood asked what reply had beeo received from the County Council with regard to the application for extension of hours of polling at the district council elections from ten o'clock in the morning to eight in the evening.—Mr. Brierley replied that a notice had been received fixing the polling from twelve to eight.</p>
        <p>THE CLOSING OF THE BOARD'S LABOURS. The Chairman observed that that was the last meetir g of the Board, arid that therefore it would be fit to preacn the funeral sermon upon it. (Laughter.)—Mr. Brierley said that it would be necessary to call the Board ti- gether again to pass some accounts and wind up the business of the Board before the new District Council came into power.—A long discussion arose as to the best date for the purpose. The day after the elections was mentioned. but the members expressed themselves doubtful of getting a quorum of that date. Eventually it was agreed to meet on Thursday, December. 22.</p>
        <p>The Japanese have had the misfortune to lose, for the present, their brilliant commander-in-chief, Marshal Yamagata. RP ha a broken flown in health, and is re- turning from Manchuria to Japan. In washing. MATCHLESS CLEANS ER is a perfect Treasure saves much work awl time, to use it s a pleasure.—ADVT</p>
        <p>The latest addition to the long list of noblemen who I,ave entered business is the Marquis of Lorne, who has joined a firm or house decorators, and will not only be a partner, but will take an active share in the business as a designer. The Duke of Argyll, it is well known, is one of our poorest dukes, and Lord Lorne is far from well off. it is quite as much, so his friends river, with the desire to have some aeive interest in the life that Lord Lorne has taken to business.</p>
      </div>
