      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ELECTION Ar RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE ELECTION Ar RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am uotifled of several nominations for the Council, and while appreciating the honour two serious considerations compel me to withdraw. The first: An anxiety not to imperil the return of those old Commis- sioners who in the past have done their best with the machinery at their disposal. Their experience will be invaluable in getting the new constitution into working order, and if in the future they fail "the whirligig of time will bring its revenges." The second and most powerful, is a desire to avoid the expense and illfeeling of a contested election at a time when the town is on the verge of starvation. The winter is on us, there is no work, generous tradesmen who in the past have delighted to be charitable,now feel the pinch themselves. The ranks of those who "can and will help," are thinned. Why in-..pair their efficiency by the distraction of an election? Under such painful circumstances no young candidate can suffer by withdrawing, and divert- ing his energy and cash into the channel of charity. Those inspired by ambition, will by so doing, ensure a satisfaction more substantial than a seat in the Council, while those who think that "duty bids them fight," may well reflect that in this case there is no principle at stake.—Yours faithfully, FREDK. JCHN NORMAN, a Lyndhurst, Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>F ROD;TAM.</head>
        <p>F ROD;TAM.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO AN ORGANIST.—In the Boys' Scheol, Overton, on Monday evening, several handsome gifts were handed to Mr. Thomas Riley, in acknowledgment of long and valued services. The recipient for 50 years had been associated with Frodsham Parish Church, and for 38 of them had ably performed the duties of organ- ist. The tribute from the parishioners consisted of a beautiful silver watch, bearing the following inscrip- tion :—" Presented together with a purse of 20 guineas by the parishioners of Frodsham to Mr. Thomas Riley, after 38 years' service as organist of the Parish Church. September 23rd, 1894." The gift from the adult members of the church choir took the form of a hand- some marble clock,bearing asuitabl3 inscriptioa,in which it was stated that it was given to him on resigning the position of organist after the period named above. Another present consisted of a " gift cake," contributed by Mrs. Garratt, on which were the words "Long life to Mr. Riley." The proceedings were under the presidency, of the Rev. H. B. Brogg, and the guests included Colonel Ashton, Mr., Mrs., and the Misses C. E. Linaker, Mr., Mrs., and the Misses Timmins, Mr. and Mrs. William Davies, Mrs. and Misses Garrett, Mr., Mrs., and Miss Riley, the Misses Wright, Mrs. J. Davies, Messrs. R. V. Milner, J. Austin, H, Tiley, W. Youd, J. W. Rothwell T. Lewis, H. E. Heywood, E. G. Steward. and Jahn Pollard.—The Chairman made the presentations, refer- ring to Mr. Riley's long and valuable services as organist. Colonel Ashton, Mr. C. E. Linaker, and Mr. Peter Nick- son also spoke. Mr. Riley acknowledged the gifts in suitable terms. A programme of vocal and instrumental rrwir cra,, afterwards ,enno thrni)ol,.</p>
        <p>BIRDS, ANIMALS AND FISH STUFFED, and mounted in Glass Cases of any design. HEADS and HORNS mounted also for Sale. Birds made into Hand Screens or Fire Screens. Dealer in British and Foreign Birds and Animals' Skins, Glass Shades. Stags' Heads. C4binets Store Boxes, Nets, &amp;c., for Butterfly and Egg Collectors, —B. C00x.”.1 Naturalist: and Furrier, 21, Renshaw-street, Liverpool MIMI</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <p>_ • _ -</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. DECEMBER 8, 1894.</p>
        <p>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Vesy keen interest is being taken in the contests for seats on the new District Councils, in Earlestown and Newton. As is well known the district has been divided into five wards, and these councillors are required for each ward, making a total of 15 councillors. Wednes- day was the last day for sending in nominations, and when the stipulated time had expired 95 nomination papers had been handed in, nominating 32 candidates. Of course the nominated have until Friday to decide whether they will go to the poll or not, but so far as our information goes at the time of writing, there will be no withdrawals. Below will be found a list of the candi- dates nominated in each ward, with the place at which the votes will be recorded : CROW-LANE WARD . —William Beatty, Brookfield House, Wargrave new road, builder and coatractor • Charles Bell Ford Borron , Wargrave-road, New ton-le:W Mows, glass manufacturer; Benjamin Corlett, 84, Cross-lane, Earlestown, timber merchant ; Samuel Duncalf, 8, Market-st., Earlestown, grocer ; Henry Hibbert, B,High- st., Newton-le-Willows, butcher and licensed victualler : William Mills, 78, Cross-lane, Earlestown, auctioneer and valuer ; James Smith, Brookside, Church-st., Newton-le-Willows, merchant ' • Joseph Stringer, 112, Market-st., hairdresser; Peter Thompson, 1, Haydock- st., provision dealer. Mr. H. Hibbert was disqualfied owing to the address of the seconder of his nomination not being given as stated in the overseers list of voters. VIADUCT WA RD. —James Watson Emmett, Earle Cottage, engineer, Patrick McMahon, 10, Markat-at., draper ; Thomas Ormand, Crow-lane, accountant; William Valentine, 34, Haydock-st., physician; Mathew Winstanley, 41, and 43, Legh-st., butcher and furniture dealer. NEWTON WARD. —John Simpson, 61, High-st.,Newton; gentleman; Robert Stone, Newton Park, colliery pro- prietor ; Robert Stuart, 36, High-st., Newton, solicitor; Adam Syme, Park-road, Newton, gentleman; Frederick James Wood, Woodleigh House, High-st., Newton, manager. TOWN HALL WARD.—Evan Thomas Evans, Park-road, Newton, draper, &amp;c.; Thomas Forster, Rams Head Hotel, Earlestown,licensed victualler; William Gilmour, 306, Earle-st., joiner; Eugene Walter Whittenbury- Kaye, Cross-lane, Earlestown, clerk in holy orders ;- John Nesbit Paterson, Lismore House,Cross-lame, Earles- town, medical practitioner; John lienry Profit, 128, Market-st., clerk in Her Majesty's Customs; George Ratcliffe, 75, Sankey-st„,. gentleman. WARGRAVE WARD.—Wm. Collingwood, Mount Villa, Wargrave-road, general manager to Vulcan Foundry ; Wm. Davies, 54, Wargrave-road! blacksmith; Charles James Mouncey, 16, Havdock-st., 04•4-p-‘,O practitioner Ezra Clement Pryor, 173, Wargrav Inland Re- venue officer ; John Slee, Wargrave-road, engineer ; Wm. Thom(p)son, jun., Larkfield House, Sankey Sugar Works, assistant manager. POLLING STATIONS. Newton Ward : Magistrates'</p>
        <p>Court, Newton. Town Hall; Crow-lane ward: Wes- leyan Schools, Earlestown ; Town Hall ward: Earles- town Town Hall; Viaduct ward: Earlestown District School (2 booths) ; Wargrave ward, Wargrave Schools.</p>
        <p>WARORAVE WARD.</p>
        <p>In connection with the candidature of Messrs. Coiling-</p>
        <p>wood. Slee and Thompson for Wargrave ward, a mee-ting was held in the Vulcan schoolroom, on Monday evening. Mr. James Wright presided, and he was ac- companied on the platform by Rev. James Carson, Messrs. Collingwoed. Thompson, Catterall,Owbrige, and Haselden.—The Chairman said that in connection with that contest there had been a good deal of feeling and criticism introduced which if properly looked into might have been left out altogether. It was not a question of classes against the masses, it was a question of the general weal, the general good for one and all.—Mr. Collingwood said that since their last meeting the position of affairs had considerably developed. One of the principal objections that he could see his apponents had to himself and colleagues was that they represented a party, and that they had been shoved into the contest by the Conservative club. He wished them to clearly understand that neither he nor his colleague belonged to the club, nor had any connection with it. He gave them to understand that he was not coming forward as the</p>
        <p>representative of any party, and he found out on en- quiry that his committee was made up of different shades of opinion. He held that party politics had nothing whatever to do with the government of a town- ship of that sort. What they wanted were men who would do their work properly and . put party politics behind them altogether. They must not think he was running down the labour side. He held that the labour question was the most important of the day, but he felt sure that it was connected with party polities, that it had no connection with the duties appertaining to a district councillor. As one of the objections to his candidature, he heard incidentally the other night that he had committed a very grave act; he had discharged a workingman, a very old servant, dismissed him summarily for smoking. That was absolutely false, and in justice Co himself, he Should deny that he knew absolutely nothing about it, until it was brought to his notice. He was a little disgusted at people descending to personalities of that sort to further themselves. He was also called over the coals by one of his opponents for not attending as many meetings as he ought to have done. He admitted he had uot attended very well, although he had been present at a good number of committee meetings, but the meetings were held in the afternoon at their most busy time at the Vulcan, and he should be one to favour the meetings being held at a more convenient hour. They really wanted representative men as councillors for that ward, and he hoped they would do their best to carry out the wishes of the Committee. (Applause.)—Mr. W. Thomp- son next addressed the meeting, and said the Wargrave ward, with industries of its own, could not be properly' represented from without. Was it not to their interest that the various industries in the ward should be retained. He could say with regard to one works, it was purely out of feeling for the labour that must inevit- ably have been stranded, that the calamity of total clos- ing was averted te give the managers time to see if they could not improve matters, and so go en, and yet they said employers took no interest in their employes. It ought not to be forgotten that capital could take to itself wings and fly where the labour could not follow. They wanted practical mem, men who knew the value of money and how to spend it to the best advantage. It had been said they were representatives of a political party, but to that he gave a m )st emphatic denial.— Mr. -Catterall, in moving a resolution to further the In- terests and return the three gentlemen as representatives I of Wargrave Ward on the Council, said that practically speaking the candidates were workingmen. Referring to the remarks of Dr. Mouncey with respect to diseased meat, he said if Dr. Mouncey knew that diseased meat was sold on Earlestown market, he should not have al- lowed such a thing to go on year after year, when he could very easily have exposed it, and whatever claim he had on the confidence of the people of that ward, he had forfeited it by keeping such a secret to himself.— Mr. Ha,selden seconded the motion, and Mr. Owbridge spoke in support of it. On the motion being put to the meeting the resolution was carried unanimously.—A vote of thanks to tho Chairman terminated the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY. —Before Major.P!c.kmere (chairman) and</p>
        <p>Mr. John White.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY—Peter Moss. Sankey, 53.; Wm. Clancy, Sankey. 53. and costs; George Welsby, Poulton, 103. and costs. ALLEGED ASSAULT.—AIfred Faulkner. Croft, was charged with assaulting and beating Ed. Millett and Mary Walsh on the 24th November at Cratt. Both cases were settled by permission of the bench, the former by payment of 153 61. and the latter by payment of 123. FURIOUS RIDING.—Henry Tomlinson was charged with having on the 30:h Nov. in the highway at Padgate, furiously ridden a h,sne.—Sergeant Barnes deposed that on the above date he was in Padgate-lane when he saw defendant trying to ride the horse through a hedge.— Defendant was ordered to pay the costs. DOGS AT LARGE. —Ann Simms, Burtonwoocl was fined 23. 61. for allowing her dog to be at large on the 25th November and Thomas Unsworth for the same offence at Poulton on the 30th Nov. was tined 5. A WARNING TO PENICETH POACHERS.—JATI Travis and Richard Sankey were charged, that en the 2004 Nov. last in Ponketh,he committed trespass in search of game on certain land in the occupation of Mr. William Ditch- fiAd.—Mr. J. P. Mearns, solicitor, St. Helens, prosecuted on behalf of the tenant of Penketh Hall Farn3.—The Bench fined Travis 103. and costs, and Sankey (who had been previously cautioned) 153. and costs.—An advo- cate's fee for 103. was allowed Mr. Mearns. A SUSPICIOUS CHARACTER.—WiIIiam Harrison was charged with being on the 25th November at Newton, in a yard belonging to Thomas Ince for an unlawful par- pose. Prisoner pleaded not guilty.—Patrick Grafton. labourer, 33 Graftun-st . Earlestown, said that on the 25th of last month he was at a window of his house which overlooks Mr. Ince's yard when he saw the Prisoner get over the wall and drop into the yard. He then sent for the police.—lnspector J. Morgan, &amp;tries- town, deposed that about half-past one on the Sunday he was informed of the occurrence, and on proceeding to the yard found prisoner under a lot of straw and sacks. To get there prisoner had had to climb over a 7ft. wall. Mr. Ice's premises were accessible from the place where prisoner was.—Prisoner said that he lived and worked in Runcorn, and he had got into the yard because he was drunk and did nct wish to go out. He also said that he had been in the whole of the day.— Prisoner was sent to gaol for one month's hard labour.' AFTER HIS CHRISTMAS GOOSE.—Joseph Naylor was charged with stealing two geese value 165., the property nf James Molyneawx, farmer, Crow-lane, Newton.— Prisoner pleaded guilty, and was also charged with as- saulting John 011iver at 6.45 on the same day and place. —The magistrates, after hearing the evidence sentenced prisoner to two months hard labour.</p>
