      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>ELECTION OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Nominations have been received at the office of Mr. J. C. Sutton, clerk to the Board of Guardians, for all the wards of Earlestown and Haydock. There may still be a change in the list of candidates.for the persons nomin- ated may withdraw up to four o'clock to-day (Friday). The following is the full list of the+ candidates nomin- ated, one member being required for each ward : NEWTON IN MAKERFIELD.</p>
        <p>NEWTON WARD.—Henry Hibbert, Blue Bell Hotel, licensed victualler. TOWN HALL WARD.—Thomas Robert Matthews, The Vicarage, Market-st., clerk in holy orders; George Rat- cliffe, 75, Sankev-st., Earlestown, gentleman. CROW' LANE WARD.—John Nesbitt Patterson, Lismore House, Cross-lane, Earlestown' medical practitioner. WARGRAYR WARD.—Edward Laigh Bent, Vulcan, Vulcan Foundry, grocer; Thomas Unsworth, 51, Cross- lane, photographer. VIADUCT WARD.—John Clarke, 15 and 17, Bridge-st.. Earlestown, ironmonger; James Henry Forster, 47 and 49, Houghton-st., Earlestown, wine and beer dealer; Joseph Eckersley, 1, Regent-st., Earlestown, grocer and provision dealer. HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>—-- - WEST WARD.—Thomas Andrewg. Church-road, Hay- dock, grocer; Thomas Jackson, 47, Cross-lane, Earles- town. salesman; John Henry Ridette, The Manse,Black- brook-road, Haydock, congregational minister. EAST WARD.—William Balharry, The Beeches, Hay- dock, farmer ; William Moore, near the Roman Catholic Chapel. Haydock, clerk in Holy Orders. CENTRAL WARD. —Jac,b Billiage, near Cottage Hospi- tal, Haydock, insurance agent ; John Norcross, Rose- terzace, Haydock, collier; John Turton, near Ram's Head, Haydock. gentleman._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>UNITED IiErHODIST FREE CHURCH,</head>
        <p>UNITED IiErHODIST FREE CHURCH,</p>
        <p>FANCY FAIR AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>For the purpose of reducing a debt of £370 on Biles- mere-st.United Methodist Free Church, Runcorn,a fancy fair was opened on Wednesday afternoon, in the school- room, Sutherland-st. There was a large attendance when Mr. S. Taylor, J.F., performed the opening cere- mony. The members of the girls' class connected with the school have had the scheme in hand for over two years, during which period they have collected an in- numerable number of useful and ornamental goods. The heavily-laden stalls bore evidence of this on Wednesday afternoon, the various articles being neatly displayed. The committee in charge of the arrangements was corn- paged of the following ladies :—Misses Ashbrooke, Carr, Clare, Dean, Guy, Holford, Moores, Newall, Nield, Paul, M. Paul, Rayner, Royle, J. Boyle, Rutter, Stafford, M. Stafford, Stubbs, E. Stubbs, Scragg, Smith, B. Smith, and White. Mrs. Gandy was secretary and Mrs.Newall treasurer for the ladies' sewing meeting. The general secretaries were Messrs. W. C. Cooke, W. Newall, and J. Turnbull. Mr. J. Rayner was secretary. After devotional exercises, the pastor of the church, the Rev. J. Stafford, briefly stated the objects of the sale of work,mentioning that the debt was owing on the chapel and chapel-keeper's house.—Mr. S. Taylor then declared the bazaar open in suitable ternss.—The Rev.T. Brackenbury nropQsad a vote of thanks to Mr. Taylor. —Mr. James Turnbull seconded the motion, and it was carried with acclamation. The sale of goods was then commenced, and the first day's takings amounted to £5O 14s. 4id. Entertainments were provided during the evening. On Thursday the fancy fair was again opened, when Mr. T. Rayner, of Marston. near North- wich, performed the opening ceremony. He expressed the hope that the friends would realise their fullest ex- pectations. A vote of thanks was accorded Mr. Rayner on the motion of Mr. John Halford seconded by Mr. Wesley Cooke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>ELECTIONS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE ELECTIONS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>THE NOMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>The election of Urban District Councillors for Rnn- corn, which is to take plare on Saturday week, the 15th inst., will no doubt be one of the most memorable contests witnessed in Runcorn for local honours. As will be seen from the lists of nominated candidates given below quite a host of local men have been brought forward. But whether they will all go to the poll or not will be known to-day (Friday) which is the last day for withdrawals. At present there seems every likelihood of a contest in each of the six wards. The following are the nominatiorta which have been handed in to the returning officer (Mr. Lake) : HALTON WARD.—Frith John Ribert, Belle Vue,Halton- read ; Taylor Samuel. Rydal Mount, Halton-road, lime merchant; Wiswall Francia, 10, Stanley Villas, civil engineer ; Norman Frederick John, Lyndhurst, Higher Runcorn. manager of chemical works; Abel Richard Tower Flouse, Greenway-road. rarrier. HEATH WARD.-Speakman Philip, The Mount, Higher Runcorn, merchant; Clarke John, 25 High-st., signal- man ; Hayes John, 9 Chaucer-st., master painter; Imi- son Edwin, Heatherlea, pawnbroker; Pritchard James, The Elms, Higher Runcorn, ship owner; Norman Frederick John, Lyndhurst, Weston-road, manager of chemical works.</p>
        <p>NEWTO%N WARD.—Bisbrown Daniel, 40 Greenway- road, timekeeper; Dobson James, Stanley Villas, Run- corn. chemist; Reynolds Thomas, 94, Shaw-street, general dealer ' • Gatley Ellis, 3, St. John-street, general secretary to the Upner Mersey Watermen and Porters Association; Gastaldi John Bantiste,Trwell-lane; Williams William Martin, Bridgewater Arms Hotel, Gilbert-street; Leach William, 20, Albert-street. gentleman ; Kirkham James, "Glencoe," 140, Greenway-road, gentleman: Wilson, John, 13, Oxford-read, architect and surveyor. BRIDGEWATER WARD.—lmison Edwin, Heat herlea, Higher Runcorn, pawnbroker; Simpson John Simeon, 122 Green way-road, Congregational minister; Key Edward, 34 Lord-st., foreman ; Kirkham George Henry, 3 High-st., fruiterer; Gatley Ellis, 3 St. John-st. ; Hewitt George, Riversdale, Trentk6 m-st., ship chand- ler; Abel Richard, Grecnway-road ; Tcrrie George, Balfour-st., gentleman. CENTRAL WARD.—ltinison Edwin, pawnbroker ; Kirk- ham Gen. Henry, 3, High-st., fruiterer ; Lea, Thom., 84 and 86, Greenwav-road, bak&amp;r and confectioner; Collier Walter, 6, Church.st., grocer ; Gastaldi John Baptiste.lrwell-lane ; Hank nson Wm., High-st., mineral water manufacturer; Carr Thos. Samuel, Norman-road, watchmaker and jeweller; Norman Alfred Robert, Linden': Lodge ; Holmes Luke. High-st., auctioneer. CENTRAL WARD.—Posnect Rcbert Harold 16, Stanley- villas, Greenwavd•oad, manager of Tannery; Grice Henry Joseph, 59, Bridge-st.,sadler and harness maker ; Gatley Ellis, 3, St. John-st. : Handley, Wm., Heath- road, Savings Bank actuary ; Kirkham Gen. Henry, 3, High-st., fruiterer; Hankinson Wm., Hlgh-st., mineral water maeufacturer ; Horsefield Jonathan, Canal-st., coal merchant.</p>
        <p>The nomination papers handed in for Luke Holmes, Central Ward, and Gore Torrie, Bridgewater Ward wpre declared invalid by the returning officer through informalities in filling Ur) of the papers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>MEXICAN CURE.</head>
        <p>MEXICAN CURE.</p>
        <p>T(NOWN AS REV. JOSEPH HOLMES' MEXI- IX Frescriptien, is the only gua-anteed Remedy e-,r all those who suffer from the errors of youth, nervous weakness, exhausted vitality, kidney, bladder. gravel, prost te and kindred complaints, and has stood the tat for 20 ears. Send a self-addressed stamped enve!al a to JOSE(' HOLNEFS' 'term dy Co., Blf,ornabury Bkonattbur square, Londe r, and get full particulars of this great Ramer re. of charge.</p>
        <p>A private meeting of the candidates for the various .wards was held in the Boardroom at the Town Hall on Thursday evening, when over 20 of the 36 candidates nominated were present. Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., occupied the chair. The business of the meeting was the con- sideration of matters relating to the coming contest. Efforts were made to affect a compromise but without success. It was suggested by one can- didate that those members of the old Board who desired to sit on the new Council should be allowed to do so unopposed, and the other candidates to contest the vacant seats. This, however, fell to the ground. Dr. Gastaldi and Mr. Irnison decided at the meeting to con- test Central ward, but some candidates are still un- decided as to which ward they intend to contest. The only person who has signified his intention of withdrawing from the contest is Mr. A. J. Norman. Mr. Bisbrown (Newtown Ward) as will 13,. seen from an advertisement in another column, will hold a meeting on Tuesday next when he intends to speak on the graving dock question. The addresses of the follow- ing candidates will be found in another column: New- town Ward, Messrs. B. Bisbrown, James Dobson, and James Kirkham; Heath Ward, Mr. Philip Speakman; Central Ward, Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. W. Collier and 1. Imison ; Halton Ward, Messrs. S. Taylor, F. Wiswall,and J. R. Frith; Mersey Ward, Mr. R. H. Posnett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>PENKETTr.</head>
        <p>PENKETTr.</p>
        <p>MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT ASSOCIATION.—A most interest- ing lecture was delivered on Wednesday night, before the members of thrt above association. The leeturer was Mr. J. W. Garnett, of Chester, and his subject was "Palestine," illustrated with limelight views. Mrs. R. Garnett was in the chair and there was a good attend- ance. Tn- lecturer gave a very interesting description of his travels in the Holy Land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WESTON.</head>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A PILOT.—A pilot, named Adam Cart- wright, of Marine Parade, Seacombe, was returning late on Saturday night to his steamer "Delano." lying at Weston Point dock, and bound from Manchester to Baltimore, when owing to the intense darkness he missed his footing, and fell from the dock wall on to the river beach. The tide was out at the time or otherwise he would almost certainly have been drowned. He was taken on board, and Dr. McLennan was sent for, and found that he was suffering from a fractured rib, severe, bruises, and shock. On Sunday, Cartwright was taken to his horns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>STOCKTOg HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTOg HEATH.</p>
        <p>THE HILL CLIFFS AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BAND OF HOPE.---The second meeting in connection with the above was held on Monday night in the Providence Chapel which was filled.. The President, the Rev. J. 8. Hughes, presided, and was supported by Mr. H. Eyes, Mr. Dallow, Mr. Spenceley and Mr. Mounfield. A very excellent programme was spendidly gone through. Recitations being given by Misses Edith Strange, Sarah Jane Worrell, Minnie Kenworthy, Annie Howard, Edith Houghton, Jennie Houghton, Ada Guest and Helena Grounds. A song was given by Miss Eva Grounds, and Misses Mounfield and Strange gave a dialogue. A dialogue was also given by Masters Val Drayson, Richard Turner and George Thompson, and recitations by Masters Richard Turner, Harry Davenport, George Moss, Harry Martin and John James Worrell. A violin trio was rendered by Mr. Potts,Miss Olivia Potts and Master William Devonport, which was encored, and a duet was given by Miss Potts and Master Devonport. An address was given by Mr. Spenceley. Hoyle's atelodies were used. Mr. A. B. Kenworthy presided at the organ. Thatitoute all concluded a capital and most successful entertaintrent.</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE YOUNG PEOPLE'S ASSOCIATION.—Ou Sat- urday night a limelight exhibition was given in the Baptist schc.olroom, in connection with the above Society and through the kindness of Mr. Frith, who presented on the screen a series of splendid views, principally of Scotland, but including Irish, Manx and Cheshire views. The entertainment was greatly appreciated by a large audience. The President of the Society, the Rev. J. S. Hughes, presided. Mr. E. J. Hall proposed and Mr. W. H. Bradford seconded a vote of thanks to Mr. Frith, which was most heartily accorded and after a similar expression of gratitude to Mr. Longland, the entertain- ment concluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE Cup COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>'IRS?</p>
        <p>CLASS,</p>
        <p>RESULTS UP TO DATE.</p>
        <p>Pld. Won. Lost. urn. G.. G.T.Pts, For Agst. Oldham Tyldesley Warrington .... St. Helens 5 ... 2 ... 3 ... 0 ... 3 6.. 2 4... 4 Rochdale Hornets swinton 5 ... 1 ... 3 ... 1 ... 1 3 3 6... 3 Broughton Rangers - 2 *Wigan *Salford 4 1 2 1 3 2... 1 6... 3 *Leigh</p>
        <p>*Leigh (on September 19). Salford (October 16) and Wigan (November 13), having been suspended for prefessionalism, will not take any part in the competitiox this season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A. AT THE Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>The usual Sunday afternoon meeting turned out very successful last week. Mr. Benjamin Farrar occupied the chair and gave an address on" Why workingmen hold aloof from churches" which aroused the interest of the audience in a great measure. A solo entitled "It is well " was given with good effect by Mr. J. Bennett, and it was fol- lowed by the sane singer's excellent rendition of "The Star of Bethlehem" (Stephen Adams). Messrs. Lunt and Stewart took part in the meeting and Mr. F. Hickman accompanied with his usual aLility.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>LIMELIGHT EXHIBITION.</head>
        <p>LIMELIGHT EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. W. Monks gave an interesting exhibition of limelight views to the members of the continua- tion school, at the People's College on Mondny night. The views thrown on the screen by Mr. Monks were illustrative of different scenes in Eng- land, and were rendered further interesting by Mr. Hall's lucid explanations. There was a good attend- ance.</p>
        <p>SALE OF WORK AT CAIRO-STREET, SCHOOLS. A sale of work in aid of the school extension fund was held in the above place on Wednesday afternoon. In the room which was prettily decnr- atc I. with flags, Chinese lanterns &amp;e., there were six attractive looking stalls containing wollen goods, clothing of different discriptions, toys, books and pictures, refreshments, and sweets. At one end of the room was to be observed a large and gaily decorated Christmas tree, and one of Edignn's latest phonographs was provided for the edification of those desirous of hearing admirable selections of popular songs and instrumental music. After tea the Christmas tree was lighted up by numerous fairy lamps which greatly entranced its attractive- ness. The following ladies presided over the sale Mrs. and Miss Long, Miss Houghton, Miss Wheeler: Mrs. Priestman,Mrs. J. R. Ynung,Mrs. F. W. Monks, Miss Monks, Miss Hill sn'l Mrs. Knowles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CHURCH P.S.A.</p>
        <p>It is very pleasant to state that the membership of the above still continues to increase and has now reached the creditable height of 1.155 members. The meetings are still a great success and the one on Sunday did not in any way fall below the average. Mr. Joseph Bunch made an efficient chairman and a very bright address was given by Mr. J. P. Sharp, of Fairfield. Manchester. Miss F. Poole was the soloist and the meeting was taken part in by Mr. Pollitt an Mr. R. H. Evans. CAIRO-ST. CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The special course of sermons "New Statements of Old Doctrines" were continued in the above place of worship •,n Sunday evening. The Rev. W. H. Drum- mond preached on "Personal Salvation," and took as his text "I am come that they might have life, and that they might have it more abundantly."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>HOLY TRINITY SALE OF WORK.</head>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY SALE OF WORK.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon a pale of work was opened which has the three-fold object of paying the annual contribution to the Pastoral Aid Society, the Church Missionary Society, and to parochial objects. The sale has now been conducted for 15 years by Mrs. Or. Preston. There was a large collection of useful and fancy articles arranged on three stalls, and also a refreshment stall. Mrs. Garratt, of Frodsham, declared the sale open in a few appropriate words. A vote of thanks was accorded Mrs. Garratt on the motion of Dr. Preston, seconded by Mr. Charles Timmis. During the evening entertainments were given.</p>
        <p>BRUNSWICK WESLEYAN MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A lecture was given In connection with this society on Wednesday evening by Mr. F. J. Nor- man. JP., on "An Easter visit to Italy," in which he graphically described come of the "beauty spots "of the country. The lecture was illustrated by numerous views, shown on the screen by means of an oxy-hydrogen lantern, manipulated by Mr. J. Robinson. On Thursday evening a large number of children assemblEd in the Brunswick schoolroom when t he various scenes were again shown,described by Mr. F. J. Norman, J.P.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>THE Three Most Inquisitive English Rivers—The Wye, the Wen, and the Ware. PHYSICIANS should not be afraid to cross the Ocean, because they are used to see sickness. MASTER OR MISSUS ?—Stranger: "Where's the master of this house ?" Irish Innkeeper: "Over there. She's weedin' the garden, sor." A BOY writing a composition on "Extremes," says that "we should endeavour to avoid extremes —especially those of wa,ps and bees." SOFTLEICH "There goes a drinker I feel sorry for. His wife actually drives him to the s Old Soak : "What's the matter with him? Can't he walk ?" "WHAT have you been a-doing !" asked a boy of his playmate, whom he saw coming out of the house with tears in his eyes. "I've been a-chasing a birch rod round my father." THE builder of a church recently, when the toast of his health was given, rather eui,..lnatically replied that he was "more fitted for the scaffold than the public platform." NOT PARTlCULAR.—Magistrate: "You're charged with begging. Ten shillings or fourteen days." Beggar (nonchalantly) "Well, I s'pose beggars mustn't be choosers. Whiclli ver you like !" HE DIDN'T FOLLOW. —Doctor : " Have you followed my prescription ? " Mr. Briggs: "No, I haven't; if I had, I should have broken my neck, for I threw it out of a third-floor window." HAVERLEY : "Would you call a man a liar who was in the habit of telling little harmless fibs ?" Austen: "It would depend on how much he weighed." "I SHOULDN'T care much about the fleas," said the thin, pale lodger in a boarding-house to his landlady, "but the fact is, ma'am, 1 hain't got the blood to spare." MAMIE : "I believe in women's rights." Gertie : "Then, you think every woman should have a vote." Mamie: "No ; but I think every woman should have a voter." "I NEVER pass that house that I do not see that pretty little woman on the lawn. She must spend most of her time there." "Yes," replied Brown, "she does; she's a grass widow." EDITOR: "See here! In one line you rhyme 'wind' with sinned,' and in the next line you rhyme 'wind' with 'find." Latter-day poet: "Oh, that's all right. The dictionaries allow both ways. HE: "how beautiful these flowers are I They remind me of you." She: "But they are artificial flowers. There is nothing real about them." He: " Yes ; I knew that as soon as I looked at them."</p>
        <p>A RECOMMENDATION.—Mistress (to lady's maid applying for a situation) : "Have you any recom- mendations from the family you lived with last ?" Maid: "Oh, certainly, madam. While I was there three of the daughters got married." A M —Friend: "That villain in your geW play is a masterpiece. Where did you get the character ?" Dramatist: "I imagined a man pos- loosed of all the varieties of wickedness which my lerife ascribes to me when she gets mad." A DOUBLE CRThia—The French satirist, Alphonse Karr, replying to an inquisitive blue. stocking who demanded his opinion of literary women, said, "They commit two crimes: increase the number of books and decrease the number of honsewives I " EXTRAORDINARY PRECAUTION.—HoteI Clerk: "Did you tell that old gentleman from the country that he must'nt blow out the gas, as I told you.?" Barney (new boy) : " Yis, sorr ; but it's ao afeard to thrust him Oi was, sorr, Oi blowed it &amp;mit mesilf, sorr." BRIGHT PROSTECTS.—Mrs. Craik : "I look so well in black that it seems almost a pity you can never live to enjoy seeing me wear it.' Craik (cheerfully) : "Don't worry about that my dear; I expect to outlive your mother." DANGEROUS MEDICINE. —Mrs. Moddlin (news- paper in hand) : "Here's a solemn warning for you, Daniel. A man in Kansastook six glasses of whisky and black pepper as a medicine and died in great agony." Mrs. Moddlin : "I don't wonder at that. Black pepper is mighty powerful stuff." DONE BROWN.—Little Willie was sent to hold a wet towel before the fire until it was dry, the other morning. The youngster obeyed with alacrity, and the mother went on with her house- hold duties until, a minute later, she was fairly electrified by Willie bawling out, "Mither, is't dry when it's brown ?" HE RECEIVED ORDERS.—An Irishman, having enlisted in a cavalry regiment, was undergoing all the horrors of learning to ride, when the horse threw him over its head. According to custom, the sergeant who was superintending the riding lesson called out: "Pat Murphy, did you receive orders to dismount ?" "I did, sorr," said Pat. "Where from ?" bawled the sergeant. "From hindquarters, sorr 1" WAIT TILL YOU'RE BIG ENOUGH I—The five- year-old son of a family the other day stood watch- ing his baby-brother, who was making a great noise over having his face washed. The little fellow at length lost his patience, and stamping his tiny foot, said, " ou think you have lots of trouble, but you don't know anything about it. Wait till you're big enough to get a lickin', and then you'll see—won't he, mamma?" COOK: "I must leave, mum, because I'm going tg get married to the milkman." Mistress: I'm very glad to hear it, Jane, and I hope when you're married that, out of gratitude for my kindness to you, you'll see that your husband doesn't water my milk, as he has been in the habit of doing 1" A WELL-KNOWN professor had taken his watch from his pock-t to mark the time he intended to boil an egg for his breakfast, when a friend enter- ing the room found him absorbed in somelabstruse ogloulation, with the egg in his hand, upon which he was intently looking, and the watch supplying its place in the saucepan of boiling water. SCHOOLMASTER (to boy of neglected education: "If I take two from five, what will be the result?" Boy of Neglected Education: " Dunno, sir." Schoolmaster: "Oh, you stupid boy I Now, if your father's apple tree had five apples upon it, and I took two of them away, what would be the result?" Boy of Neglected Education: "You'd be locked up for stealin', sir." A CLERGYMAN was much surprised one day at receiving a basket of potatoes from an old woman in his parish, with a message s tying that as he had remarked in his sermon on the previous Sunday that some "common tators " (commentators) did not agree with him, she had sent him some real good ones. " HABIT "is hard to overcome. If you take off the first letter it does not change " abit." If you take off another, you still have a " bit " left. If you take off still another, the whole of " it" remains. If you take another, it is not "t " totally used up. All of which shows that, if you wish to be rid of a " habit " you must throw it off altogether. A GHOSTLY I HREAT. —Some years ago a travel- ling troupe were playing " Hamlet " in Dublin. When the actor representing the Ghost came to the well-known passage, "I am thy father's ghost," a voice from the gallery responded : " Ah, allure, an' a mighty fine spirit ye make, too 1" To which the Ghost replied, All right, Hagarty ! I know yez, and I'll give yez such a batin' as yez never had in yore born days when I got yez out- side." It is needless to say this brought down the house."</p>
        <p>VERY PLAIN. —A waggish journalist, who is often merry over his personal plainness, tells this story of himself : " I went to a chemist the other day for a dose of morphine for a sick friend. The assistant objected to give it to me, evidently fearing that I intended suicide. Pshaw I' said I. Do I look like a man who would kill himself?' Gazing steadily at me a moment, he replied: 1 don't know. It seems to me ,if I looked like you I should be greatly tempted to kill myself !' " A SLIGHT DIFFERENCE.—" Goodness gracious, Jenks, what a sight you are !" "Just as I was leaving home to come to the club, my wife pelted me with flowers." "But that doesn't account for your bruised and battered appearance ?" "You see, she forgot to take them out of the pots !" A DEBATING SOCIETY has been discussing the question, "Is it worse to think you have reached the top when there is one stair more, than to think there is one stair more when you have reached the bottom ?" "Now," said the warden to the forger who had just arrived at the prison, "we'll set you to work. What can you do best?" "Well, if you'll give me a week's practke on your signature I 11 sign your official papers for you," said the prisoner. THE SECRET OF BEAUTY is found in CIITICURA SOAP, the most effective skin purifying and beautifying soap in the world, as well as the purest and sweetest for toi:et and nursery. It is the only preventive of pimpl‘as, blacklu ads, red rough, and oily skin, red, rough hands with shapeless nails, dry, thin, and falling hair, and simple baby blemishes, because the, only preventive of inflammation and clogging of the pores, the cause of minor affections of the skin, scalp. and hair. Sale greater than the combined sales of all other skin and complexion soaps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-12-08_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>ASTERPIECE..</head>
        <p>ASTERPIECE..</p>
        <p>01011tP.</p>
