        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTION. TO THE ELECTORS OF THE CENTRAL WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, • The election addresses which occupy such a prominent place in the newspapers and on the walls, will I fear, have sorely tried your patience. Ide not desire to add to your difficulties. Some gentle- men claim to have done a great deal. I will not enter into competition with them, but will say that I have striven bard in a quiet way to do my duty faithfully and squarely, and that if you should do me the honour to elect me I will do my level best in the future. I am, Your Obedient Servant, EDWIN IhfISON. Heather Lea, Higher Runcorn, December sth, 1894.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. TO THE ELECTORS OF HALTON WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, - We beg to offer ourselves as Candidates for the above Ward,and trust the long and active interest we have taken in everything connected with the welfare of the Town. will be a sufficient guarantee that for the future we shall not be found wanting in our endeavour to secure to the Ratepayers genera- ally, the economical and efficient working of the various works and undertakings connected therewith. The question of the Water supply to the Town, shall receive our special attention, as we consider that not only should it pay a fat r remuneration on the outlay. and secure a reduction in the Rates of the Town, but that care should be taken to utilise the present resources and prevent waste, thereby avoiding additional outlay for a farther supply, for many yeare to come. The Sanitary condition of the Town, will be carefully watohedby us. and any matter which affects the interest of the Ratepayers shall receive our thoughtful consideration, and we shall endeavour to do everything we can to lighten the burdens of the Ratepayers. Trusting to be favoured with your support, We remain, Ladies and Gentlemen, Your obedient Servants, SAMUEL TAYLOR, FRANCIS WISWALL, JOHN RoBERT FRITH, Runcorn, Ist December, 1894.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. TO THE ELECTORS OF MERSEY WARD. LADIES AND GENTLIIIVEN,— In response t, several requests from the rate- payers of this ward, I offer myself as a candidate at the forthooming election. My interests have always ben In this ward, and as repre- senting oneof the largest ratepaying works in it, I shall do my utmost to further every faci ity for promoting the busi- ness of the town, and especially of this ward. In the past I have used what influence I had outside the Board to promote th er ction of a bridge over the Bridge- - ater Canal at Halton-road, and I should, if elected, do my best to hurry it forward, as likely to increase the business facilities of this ward. I would give my best attention to the sanitary arrange- ments of the town, and would look favourably upon the g g question of Swimming Baths and Recreation Ground, pro- yid' • vid• d these things could be gone into without increasing the burden of the ratepayers. which is at present quite heavy enough. I am in favour of making as much use of the Technical In- stitute as possible, and to that end bringing it as much as possible under the centrol of the ratepayers and the body of intelligent workingmen of the town, for whose benefit it Was built. The development of the trade of Runcorn, is to my mind the great question of interest to ail ratepayers, and I should make this my great object, and do all I could to develope our interests as a Port, and especially in the matter of the speedy construction of a Graving Dock by the Ship Canal Company. In asking foe-the first time fir your votes and support, I can only add that I shall take every opportunity that pre- sents itself of increasing the business of the town, and of serving the interests of the Mersey Ward, with which I am so closely connected. I am, Ladies and Gentlemen, Yours faithfully, ROBEItT H. POSNETT. Highfield Tannery, Runcorn.</p>
