        <p>A NEW DEPARTURE IN .THE FURNISHING TRADE. The extensive alterathuis necessitated by continued increase of business at the Manchester Louvre har.ng now been almost completed, the Proprietors have pleasure in informing their old-established " clientele " and the public generally, that the increased Exhibition Space in their Show-rooms (opposite All Saints' Church) has enabled th-m to stock and display an enormously added Quantity of FURNITURE—both New and Second-hand—so that their Customers has,e now a Collection to choose from which itis not too much to say is second to none in the United Kingdom. The recent extensive Sales held by us in consequence of Extension of Premises met with such a ready response from wide-awal,f. clients, that our former Stock was speedily reduced and it became a serious question with us how to cope with the demands of fresh Customers. Whilst, of course the market is being flooded with New Modern Furniture, there scarcely ever was a time when High-class, Second-hand, Durable and Handsome SUITES were more difficult to obtain. But, ever r the alert, and first in the field, we are glad to say that we have been successful In securing one of the rarest and cornpl.test Collections of every variety of Furniture everoffered to the public since we entered the Trade. £lO,OOO OF NEWLY ADDED STOCK Now on offer at nearly one-half of its original value. Railway Fares Paid to Country Customers. Catalogues Free on Appli- cation. THOSE ABOUTITO MARRY and those who contemplate adding to their present Stock of Furniture are specially invited to pay an early visit to the Manchester Louvre (opposite All Saints' Church). No one will be pressed ti buy, and our Salesmen have instructions to show the pubii ; through our Spacious and Elegant Show- rooms without importunity, as we rely upon the Furniture to recommend itself without puffing. The Extraordinary Stock which we now hold must be reduced without delay, as the prices paid by us will not allow for loss of interest on heavy mumulated quantities. Quick turnovers and small profits alone suit our class of business. It will thus be seen what an immense advantage we are able ts show over Houses who hold their Stocks for months and months, and are °bilged to charge the Public with the intsrest th their sunk capital. Every facility is offered to purchasers. All goods are delivered free in our own carts and vans, or securely packed for transit by Rail, Carriage Paid. Every article is tested before leas; ng the premises, and, if necessary, skilled workmen will be sent out to plan carpets and At the house up. Estimates given for the com- plete furnishing of a House from £2O to £l,OOO in value. Ad- vice gratis on the choice and selection of EVERY REQUISITE FOR COTTAGE OR MANSION. Parties visiting the Manchester Louvre should enter the Oxford-street, Rusholme, or Withingten car at Piccadilly, and ask the conductor to stop at the MANCHESTER 1,01:1 ZRE, where they will be ast ,nished to find such spacious and elegantly appointed Showrooms, surpassing anything outside of London. Do not forget that we now hold THE MOST COMPLETE COLLECTION OF FURNITURE EVER OFFERED, AT ABOUT ONE-HALF ITS ORIGINAL COST. Absolutely unapproachable for E'egance, Comfort, Durability, and Cheapness. THIS WEEK'S LIST OF BARGAINS. 6ft. Brown Oak Sideboard, with lofty plate-glass back superbly carved ; original cost £75, price, £35. Solid Walnut Bedroom Suite. by Neild. mmprising sft fully enclosed wardrobe, pair of large-sized toilet tables with side reflecting mirrors, bedside pedestal, three chairs, and towel airee, price delivered. £23 10s., cheap. Bedroom Suite in satin walnut and Ash; glft. wardrobe with bevelled glass door, pair of enclosed toilet tables, pedes- tal, three cane chairs and towel airer, complete for £l2 125., worth 220. Solid Walnut Suite, smaller size than above, with the same quantity of articles, 10 guineas. Breakfast Room Suite, all complete, £3 17s 6d. Sideboard, in mahogany, £2 25.. . Heavy Parisian Bedstead, with upholstered back, curtains and vacancies, all complete, £4 15s. Wire Mdtress, to fit the above, 9s. 6d. Large Quantity of Second-hand Kitchen Furniture at less than half the usual price.. Dining Room Suite in real skins, solid frames, by Reilly, beautifully upholstered large couch, pair of easy chairs, and six standard small chairs; only been used three months, 212 12s. Complete Furnishings for Drawing-room in inlaid rose- wood. The suite is upholstered in expensive Genoa velvet, superb cabinet °remnants!, fancy tables, brass fender. Are brasses, velvet pile carpet, and hearthrug, complete for £27 10s. ; cost when new, £5O. sft. Walnut Sideboard, with very lofty glass back, enclosed with drawers. cupboards, aro., well worth £2O. Our price delivered £8 10s. Parisian Bedstead, very heavy £1 17 6 Wool Mattresses, full size 0 12 6 Hair Mattress, full size All Bras+ Belsteads, full size Railway fare paid to country customers, and all goods delivered free. GOLDSTONE AND SONS, THE MANCHESTER LOUVRE, 115 AND 117, OXFORD-STREET, ALL SAINTS', MANCHESTER. •</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL TO THE ELECTORS OF CENTRAL WARD. LADIES AND GENTLIMEN,— I appeal for your confidenee with reference to the new regime, so styled Urban District Council. My desire is in perfect accord with Liberal principles, which I shall most earnestly support. If your appreciation of my past services in several public capacities, with which positions I have been entrusted, have met with your approval, then you may rely that the same will be maintained in the new governing body of our town. There are many questions of vital Importance demanding careful attention at the present moment, which have a direct beariny upon the burdens imposed upon my fellow ratepayers. Duki 'e my tenure of office it has always been my determina- tion we" ch carefully over the spending of public money, and His my intention in the future to war, against extrava- ganee in any form. To develop the interests of Runcorn, front a sanitary, educational, and commercial point of vier, will be my special aim. Economy, however, shall not cripple efficiency. Trusting to meet with the approval of my fellow electors, I remain, Your sincere and devoted friend, JOHN BAPTIST GASTALDI. The Presbytery, irwell-lane, Runcorn. December 3rd, 1894.</p>
