        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL ELEC- TION, 1894. , TO TUB ELECTORS OF NBWTOWN WARD. LADINN AND GMNTLENEEN.— Three years ago you did me the hon3ur ef returning ise as one of your representatives on the Board of Commis- sioners. I have during that time endeavoured faithfully and fear- lessly to discharge the duties of the honourable position in which you placed me, and I trus.t my efforts have met with your approval, I have pleasure in again offering myself as a Candidate for your suffrages, I have no "axe to grind," but will at all times do my utmost to further your interests, and support all measures which I believe to be for the interests of the ratepayers, and the development of the trade of the town. Should it be your pleasure to elect me, you may be assured of my best services in the interests of the " Newtown Ward," and of the town generally. I hope to have the opportunity before the election of ad- dressing the ratep yers and fully explaining my views on the various questious beiore uti. I am. Ladle; and Gentlemen. Yours most faithfully, DANIBL BISBROWN. BISBROWN FOR NEWTOWN WARD. A. PUBLIC MEETING will be held in the PRIMITIVB MICTHODIST SCHOOLROOM, GREEN- WAY-ROAD, on Tuesday evening next at 7.30. Chairman: Mr B. CROSBY. Mr. Bisbrown (candidate for the ward) will address the meeting.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL ELEC- TION. 1894. TO THE ELECTORS OF NEWTOWN WARD. LADIDS AND GENTLEMEN, Having been urged by a number of Electors in tke Ward to allow myself to be nominated, I have, after careful consideration, consented so to do. As to my qualifications for the office, I be in the first Instance to remind you that I have been for many years a tradesman and ratepayer in the town, and consequently am fully conversant with the needs thereof. Secondly, having practically retired from business, I have leisure at my dis- posal whick I am ready and willing to devote to the further- ance of our mutual Interests. And as to Sanitary qneitions, I think I ma,- without egotism claim to have a thorough praetical knoirleolge of such matters. In the matter of general Improvements. I should prefer to '• make haste slowly," as in my opinion, owing to the great depression in trade, it would not be wise to materially increase the rates. As. regards the accommodation for bhipping which the Manchester Ship Canal Comptny undertook to provide. I think the present time most opportune for proceeding with the work, as there is every likelihood otherwise of the num- ber of unemployed being greatly augmented. All other questions affecting the welfare of the town will have my carerul attention, and I trust, in the event of being elected to the Council. the votes I may record will not fail to give you satisfaction. I am, Ladi, s and Gentlemen, Yours faithfully, JANIES 'KIRKHAM. "Glencoe," Greenway Road, Runcorn, December let, 1894.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. NEWTOWN WARD. LADIES AND G ENTLEMEN, The Local Government Act of 1894 will shortly be in force, and the exis log Br art of Commissioners will be diseolved. I therefore avail myself of the °ppm' *unity of thanking the electors of Runc3re for the numer -at marks of confidence and support I have received from them for a period extending over twenty years. during which these I have held most of the important offices relating to the admin- istration of our town affairs. bail with satisfaction the new Act with the extended powers and duties attaching to it, the abolition of plural vot- ing, the adoption of voting by ballot, and the division of the town into wards, and welcome it as the attainment of a much needed reform in our system of Local Government, which, if loyally adhered to. and faithfully worked, cannot fail to be of unspeakable benefit to every section of the community, and i respectfull) place my services at the dis- posal of the electors of t,.e Newton Ward. If yon do me the honour to elect me as one of your repre- sentatives on the Runcorn Urban District Council, I shall endeavour to the best of my ability to di charge my duties without fear or favour. deep'y regret the commercial depression which at pre- sent is so acute in our town, and recognize the nece- sity for a careful anu judicious expenditure of public money, and the urgent need which exists for the development and ex- tension of the trarte of Rurroorn (especially on the matter of the constru tion of a graving dock). These and all matters affecting the welfare of the public shall have my close and constant attention. - JAMES DOBSON. Stanley Villas, Higher Runcorn. November 20th, 184.</p>
