        <p>DISTRICT COUNCIVELECTION-CROW LANE WAR D. TO THE ELECTORS. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, I beg to offer myselt for election as your Representa- tive, and come forward as a Neutral Candidate in response to an appeal from many electors in the above Ward. I am in many ways well qualified to represent you, having lived in the district all my Ii is. and having gained very in- timate acquainttnce with the various requirements and schemes cennezted with it. My views with regard to sever ,1 of the questions mow exercising th minds of the electors ate as fellows : (1). I will particularly watch the interests of this Ward. (2). I will do all in my power to legally obtain possession of the waste land known as " Newton Common, and ties the same to the best advantage. (3). This Ward especially has very poor Postal accom- modation, and I will press forward a scheme which will remedy this grievance to some extent, (4). I will oppose the paving of streets which have very little traffic, with expensive setts ; but practical experience teaches me that setts shou,d be used in all main streets. 45). The appearance of most of our streets is disgraceful, and I would urge the necessity of a speedy settlement of the question of street-making. ' (6). The Lock-up Shops and Offices, the property of the Township, ought to be taken down, and not used for a Read- ing-room. If a Reading room is required, we have plenty of accommodation in the Town Hall. (7). lam in favour of an investigation into the Dean Trust Charity management, and if possible, transferring the same to the control of the District Council. (8). The time is premature for the establishment of a School Board for the District, and I will do my utmost ta postpone the formation of styli an expensive mode of con- trolling education. (9). I prefer to have the meetings held in the evening. (10). If advisable to retain dtposits frem Consumers of Gas, I will endeavour to obtain a certain rate of interest upon such deposits. If honoured by your choice, I will at all times endeavour to serve you faithfully to the utmost of my abilities. I beg to remain. Yours obediently, W ILLIA M BEATTY Brookfield House Wargrave Road, Earlestown, November sth, 1894.</p>
        <p>DISTRICT COUNCI L ELECTION. TO THE ELECTORS OF THE NEWTON WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, As you are aware, the Newton Township is now divided into Wards, each Ward to have three representatives. This was brought about IT the Committee you appointea fr this purpose, and who, on completion of their duties. were, ate Public Meeting held at the Town Hall, Newton, re-elected, with the addition of other gentlemen, for the pur- pose of selecting Three Canaidates for your approval, to re- present you on the new District Council. Their selection having fallen upon us, and being unanimously confirmed at the public meeting, held at the T wn Hall on Octobsz 28th, we now have pleasure in placing before you our views upon some matters requiring early attention. The Water Question is one of pressing importance, both as regards the supply and the unequal and increased charges. The removal of Night-soil and Sewerage, which so direetly affects the health of the district, will receive our special at- tention. With reference to the Post Office Question, though desir- ous to improve the Postal arrangements in other parts of the district, we shall strenuously oppose any scheme that will Increase the rates or inconvenience the Ratepayers at this end of the Township. We are of opinion that Meter Rents should be abolished Deposi s on Gas Accounts receive a reasonable amount of in- terest, anda better quality of Gas supplied ate lower price. The present appliances. belonging to the Township, for ex- tinguishing fires, we consider quite inadequate, and require re-arranging and strengt, ening. We are of opinion that the Newton Town Hall should be made the best possible use of for the inhabitants. The Toe nship Accounts will in future he audited by a public auditor, and it will be our endeavour to have them placed before you in such a a form as will he easily under- stood. We are prepared to deal with these and other matters in your interest, and, being life-long residents and considerable ratepayers, we are well aware of the requirements of the dis- trict, and therefore respectfully solicit your support and votes at the Election to take place on Monday, December 17th, 1894. We are, Ladies and Gentlemen, Your obedient servants, JOHN SIMPSON, ROBERT STONE, FRED. J. WOOD. November 28th. 1894.</p>
