        <p>WOMEN'S ELECTORAL POSITION. "A SCREAMING GAME." Does the public see, like humourists who represent the public in the Houses of Parliament, the joke of the many anomalies in what we merrily call constitutional law? Those relating to sex disabilities are amongst the best. 1. For the Throne women are eligible, and without the Queen's sign manual nothing can be done. 2. For Parliament, women may not vote nor be elected members. 3. For County Council, women may vote, but not be elected members. 4. For School Board, women (tax-payers) may be elected and act, but not vote. (Some say, however, that unmarried women and women not living with their husbands may vote for the School Board, but married women not.) 5. For Vestry, women, married or singl3, may vote, aid be elected. . 6. For Boards of Guardians, single women may vote and also be elected. 7. For Parish Councils and Urban District Councils, w women, married and single, may vote and be elected; a woman may even be chairman, but she must not act as • justice of the peace as a male chairman may. 8. For Town Councils, women may neither vote nor • be elected. All this upside-down fun and irrelevance adds enor- mously to the cost and trouble of our complicated sys- -1 tern of registration. That is where the fun comes in. Our masters love to see us all fighting like flies in a bottle. It is a screaming game 1—" Echo." HERE IS WHAT WE GIVE TO READERS OF THIS PAPER.</p>
